"id": "discriminant-root-count",
"label": "Count roots with the discriminant",
"explanation": "Since the question asks only how many values work, we can count the roots without finding them. The supplied rule turns the equation into\n\\[\n(2x)(x)-(1)(x)=3,\n\\]\nwhich is \\(2x^2-x-3=0\\). For a real quadratic \\(Ax^2+Bx+C=0\\) with \\(A\\ne0\\), a positive discriminant \\(B^2-4AC\\) means there are two distinct real roots. Here\n\\[\nA=2,\\quad B=-1,\\quad C=-3,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nB^2-4AC=(-1)^2-4(2)(-3)=25>0.\n\\]\nTherefore exactly \\(2\\) values satisfy the equation, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
