"solution": "Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(BC\\). Because \\(AM\\) is the given median, \\(AM=\\frac32\\) inches. The midpoint condition suggests making \\(AM\\) a midsegment of a larger triangle.\n\nExtend \\(BA\\) beyond \\(A\\) to a point \\(O\\) with \\(AO=AB=2\\). Thus \\(A\\) is the midpoint of \\(BO\\), and \\(BO=4\\). In triangle \\(BCO\\), the points \\(A\\) and \\(M\\) are midpoints of two sides. The triangle midsegment theorem therefore gives\n\\[\nOC=2AM=3.\n\\]\nEvery allowed vertex \\(C\\) consequently lies on the circle with center \\(O\\) and radius \\(3\\).\n\nConversely, choose any point \\(C\\) on this circle that is not on line \\(AB\\), and let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(BC\\). Applying the same midsegment theorem in triangle \\(BCO\\) gives \\(AM=OC/2=\\frac32\\), so every such point satisfies the original conditions.\n\nSince line \\(AB\\) passes through \\(O\\), it meets the circle at two points. Both make \\(ABC\\) collinear and must be excluded for a genuine triangle. Thus the exact locus is the circle with these two points removed. If the two degenerate positions are included, the resulting full circle is choice \\(\\boxed{D}\\).",
