"explanation": "The bases need attention before we compare exponents. As printed, we may take\n\\[\na=c=b=d=1,\\qquad x=1,\\ y=2,\\ q=3,\\ z=5.\n\\]\nBoth given chains of equalities hold, since every displayed power of \\(1\\) equals \\(1\\). Yet all five choices fail:\n\\[\nxy=2\\ne15=qz,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac12\\ne\\frac35=\\frac{q}{z},\n\\]\n\\[\nx+y=3\\ne8=q+z,\n\\]\n\\[\nx-y=-1\\ne-2=q-z,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^y=1\\ne243=q^z.\n\\]\nThe denominators in this example are nonzero. Thus no offered conclusion must hold under the printed conditions.\n\nFor the intended relation, assume additionally that \\(a,c>0\\), that the exponents are real, and that at least one of \\(a,c\\) differs from \\(1\\). Positive bases allow the power-of-a-power law. If \\(a\\ne1\\), then\n\\[\na^{xy}=(a^x)^y=(c^q)^y=c^{qy},\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\na^{qz}=(a^z)^q=(c^y)^q=c^{yq}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(qy=yq\\), these powers of \\(a\\) are equal. For a positive base different from \\(1\\), the exponential function is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing, so equal powers have equal exponents. Hence \\(xy=qz\\).\n\nIf \\(a=1\\), our added assumption requires \\(c\\ne1\\). The premises then give \\(c^q=c^y=1\\), forcing \\(q=y=0\\). Consequently \\(xy=qz=0\\) in this case as well.\n\nTherefore \\(\\boxed{xy=qz}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\), follows under the stated additional assumptions, but the printed question has no universally correct choice.",
