"solution": "Every required remainder is one less than its divisor. Thus adding \\(1\\) to the desired integer \\(N\\) makes it divisible by every integer from \\(2\\) through \\(10\\).\n\nTo find their least common multiple, we need the factors \\(2^3\\) from \\(8\\), \\(3^2\\) from \\(9\\), and the primes \\(5\\) and \\(7\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lcm}(2,3,\\ldots,10)\n=2^3\\cdot3^2\\cdot5\\cdot7\n=2520.\n\\]\nThese factors are necessary for any common multiple, and their product is divisible by every divisor in the list, so this is indeed the least common multiple.\n\nConsequently \\(N+1=2520t\\) for an integer \\(t\\). For positive \\(N\\), the smallest possibility is\n\\[\nN=2520-1=2519.\n\\]\nConversely, if \\(2520=km\\) for any \\(k\\) from \\(2\\) through \\(10\\), then\n\\[\n2519=k(m-1)+(k-1).\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le k-1<k\\), this gives exactly the required remainder. The other numerical choices are positive and smaller than \\(2519\\), so none can work. The answer is \\(\\boxed{2519}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
