"explanation": "The numerators are products and the denominators are sums, so taking reciprocals will separate the variables. The original fractions must be defined. Since \\(a,b,c\\ne0\\), their numerators are nonzero, which implies \\(x,y,z\\ne0\\). Thus all the following reciprocals are valid:\n\\[\n\\frac1a=\\frac1x+\\frac1y,\n\\qquad\n\\frac1b=\\frac1x+\\frac1z,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac1c=\\frac1y+\\frac1z.\n\\]\nThese equations are linear in \\(1/x,1/y,1/z\\). Adding the first two and subtracting the third cancels the unwanted terms:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac2x\n&=\\frac1a+\\frac1b-\\frac1c\\\\\n&=\\frac{bc+ac-ab}{abc}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBecause \\(abc\\ne0\\) and \\(2/x\\ne0\\), the numerator \\(ac+bc-ab\\) is also nonzero. Multiplying by the nonzero denominators and solving gives\n\\[\nx=\\frac{2abc}{ac+bc-ab},\n\\]\nwhich is choice E. This conclusion applies whenever the original defined equations have a solution.",
