"solution": "Assume \\(a\\ne0\\) and \\(c\\ne0\\), so the equation is quadratic and neither root is zero. Since the expression treats the two roots symmetrically, their sum and product let us avoid finding them individually.\n\nExpanding the root factorization, including when the roots coincide, gives\n\\[\nax^2+bx+c=a(x-r)(x-s)\n=ax^2-a(r+s)x+ars.\n\\]\nComparing coefficients yields\n\\[\nr+s=-\\frac ba,\\qquad rs=\\frac ca.\n\\]\nCombining the reciprocals over a common denominator and using the square-of-a-sum identity gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{r^2}+\\frac1{s^2}\n=\\frac{r^2+s^2}{(rs)^2}\n=\\frac{(r+s)^2-2rs}{(rs)^2}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\frac1{r^2}+\\frac1{s^2}\n=\\frac{b^2/a^2-2c/a}{c^2/a^2}\n=\\frac{b^2-2ac}{c^2}.\n\\]\nThe missing nonzero-root condition matters: \\(x^2-x=0\\) has roots \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), so the original expression is undefined in that case. With \\(a\\ne0\\) and \\(c\\ne0\\), the answer is \\(\\dfrac{b^2-2ac}{c^2}\\), choice D.",
