"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The reciprocal requires \\(r\\ne0\\). If complex values of \\(r\\) are permitted, expand the given square to obtain\n\\[\nr^2+2+\\frac1{r^2}=3,\n\\qquad r^2+\\frac1{r^2}=1.\n\\]\nThe sum-of-cubes identity is useful because the two quantities \\(r\\) and \\(1/r\\) have product \\(1\\). It gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^3+\\frac1{r^3}\n&=\\left(r+\\frac1r\\right)\n  \\left(r^2-1+\\frac1{r^2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(r+\\frac1r\\right)(1-1)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus, under the complex-number interpretation, the answer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).\n\nThere is a domain issue if \\(r\\) is intended to be real. The same hypothesis would imply\n\\[\n\\left(r-\\frac1r\\right)^2\n=r^2-2+\\frac1{r^2}\n=1-2=-1.\n\\]\nA real square cannot be negative, so no real \\(r\\) satisfies the condition. Consequently, choice C is the intended algebraic value when complex numbers are allowed; over the reals, the stated situation cannot occur.",
