"explanation": "The quadratic factor is a perfect square:\n\\[\nx^2+8x+16=(x+4)^2.\n\\]\nHence the equation becomes\n\\[\nx(x+4)^2(4-x)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. Thus every root is among \\(x=0\\), \\(x=-4\\), and \\(x=4\\), and each of these values makes the original product zero.\n\nThe factor \\(x+4\\) appears twice, while the other factors appear once. Therefore \\(-4\\) has multiplicity \\(2\\), meaning it is repeated when roots are listed according to their factors. Counting multiplicity gives \\(0,4,-4,-4\\), choice D. Listing only distinct root values gives \\(0,4,-4\\), choice C. Since the stem does not specify which convention to use, it does not uniquely distinguish C from D.",
