"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The point \\(C=(0,k)\\) must lie on the \\(y\\)-axis. Reflect \\(A=(5,5)\\) across that axis to \\(A'=(-5,5)\\). Every point on the axis is equally distant from a point and its reflection, so \\(AC=A'C\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nAC+BC=A'C+CB\\ge A'B,\n\\]\nbecause a straight segment is the shortest path between two points.\n\nEquality holds exactly when \\(C\\) lies on the segment \\(A'B\\). The endpoints have horizontal coordinates \\(-5\\) and \\(2\\), so that segment crosses the \\(y\\)-axis exactly once. This proves that the lower bound is attained at one unique value of \\(k\\).\n\nTo find the crossing, write the line through \\(A'\\) and \\(B=(2,1)\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\). Substitution gives\n\\[\n5=-5m+b,\\qquad 1=2m+b.\n\\]\nSubtracting yields \\(7m=-4\\), so \\(m=-4/7\\) and \\(b=15/7\\). On the \\(y\\)-axis, \\(y=b\\), hence\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=\\frac{15}{7}=2\\frac17},\n\\]\nchoice E.",
