"explanation": "To find \\(f(x+h)\\), replace every \\(x\\) in the formula by \\(x+h\\). When subtracting \\(f(x)\\), the constant terms cancel, while the linear terms contribute \\(-2h\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x+h)-f(x)\n&=5\\bigl((x+h)^2-x^2\\bigr)-2h\\\\\n&=5(2xh+h^2)-2h\\\\\n&=10xh+5h^2-2h\\\\\n&=\\boxed{h(10x+5h-2)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe second line uses \\((x+h)^2=x^2+2xh+h^2\\). This identity holds for all real \\(x,h\\), including \\(h=0\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
