"solution": "We need a property shared by all three equations, so find their real roots, keeping the square-root restrictions in the third equation.\n\nThe first equation gives\n\\[\n3x^2-2=25\\implies x^2=9,\n\\]\nso its roots are \\(-3\\) and \\(3\\).\n\nTwo real numbers have equal squares exactly when they are equal or opposite. Thus the second equation gives\n\\[\n2x-1=x-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2x-1=-(x-1).\n\\]\nThese yield \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=2/3\\), respectively.\n\nFor the third equation, both radicands must be nonnegative:\n\\[\nx^2-7\\ge0,\\qquad x-1\\ge0.\n\\]\nTogether these require \\(x\\ge\\sqrt7\\). Squaring the equation gives\n\\[\nx^2-7=x-1\n\\implies (x-3)(x+2)=0.\n\\]\nThe candidate \\(-2\\) violates the domain restrictions. The candidate \\(3\\) works because both original square roots equal \\(\\sqrt2\\). Thus this equation has only the root \\(3\\).\n\nAll the valid roots are at most \\(3\\). The other descriptions fail: the second equation has a nonintegral root and a zero root, the first has two roots, and the third has no negative root. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
