"gradingNote": "This question does not identify one unambiguous answer. You can study it without grading.",
"solution": "Assume that A immediately adopts a constant new speed during the pass. Let \\(u>0\\) be the increase in mph, so A travels at \\(50+u\\) mph. A gains on B at \\(10+u\\) mph, while A and C approach each other at \\(100+u\\) mph.\n\nDistances covered in the same time have the ratio of their speeds. Therefore, while A gains the required \\(30\\) feet on B, the gap between A and C shrinks by\n\\[\n30\\frac{100+u}{10+u}\\text{ feet}.\n\\]\nFor A to finish passing before meeting C, this must be less than \\(210\\) feet. Since \\(10+u>0\\), we may multiply by that denominator without reversing the inequality:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n30\\frac{100+u}{10+u}&<210,\\\\\n100+u&<7(10+u),\\\\\n30&<6u,\\\\\nu&>5.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAt \\(u=5\\), the gap shrinks by exactly \\(210\\) feet, so the two events occur simultaneously. If that boundary is allowed and the question seeks the least increase, the answer is \\(5\\) mph, choice C.\n\nIf “safely” requires finishing before meeting C, then the increase must be greater than \\(5\\) mph. In that case, increases of \\(10\\), \\(15\\), and \\(30\\) mph, choices B, D, and A, all work, and there is no least real increase. Thus the wording does not determine a unique safe choice: \\(\\boxed{\\text{C is the boundary; strict clearance requires more than }5\\text{ mph}}\\).",
