"solution": "The equation \\(x^2+y^2=25\\) represents a circle centered at the origin with radius \\(5\\). This suggests looking for opposite points that form diameters.\n\nFirst find all the intersections. Combining the two given equations gives\n\\[\n(x+y)^2=25+24=49,\n\\qquad\n(x-y)^2=25-24=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(x+y=\\pm7\\) and \\(x-y=\\pm1\\). Solving the four sign combinations gives exactly four points, which we label in order around the circle:\n\\[\nA=(3,4),\\quad B=(4,3),\n\\]\n\\[\nC=(-3,-4),\\quad D=(-4,-3).\n\\]\nEach satisfies both original equations, and the four sign combinations leave no other possibilities.\n\nThe points \\(A,C\\) are opposite about the origin, as are \\(B,D\\). Therefore the diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) are both diameters of length \\(10\\), and each has midpoint \\((0,0)\\). Diagonals that bisect each other make the quadrilateral a parallelogram; equal diagonals then make that parallelogram a rectangle.\n\nTo distinguish it from a square, compare adjacent sides:\n\\[\nAB=\\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt2,\n\\qquad\nBC=\\sqrt{(-7)^2+(-7)^2}=7\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe adjacent sides are unequal. Thus the resulting figure is a rectangle, choice D.",
