"solution": "First assume that \\(N\\) is the nonnegative integer square root. This assumption matters: if negative integers are allowed, then\n\\[\n1+2+\\cdots+288=\\frac{288\\cdot289}{2}\n=41616=(-204)^2,\n\\]\nand \\(-204<100\\). Thus, without the nonnegative convention, none of the listed choices gives all possible values of \\(K\\).\n\nUnder the stated convention, \\(K\\) is a positive integer and \\(1\\le N\\le99\\). Pairing the sum with its reverse gives\n\\[\n\\frac{K(K+1)}2=N^2\\le99^2=9801.\n\\]\nSince \\(140\\cdot141/2=9870>9801\\), we have \\(K\\le139\\).\n\nConsecutive integers have no common divisor greater than one: a common divisor would divide their difference, which is one. Dividing their even member by two preserves this property. A product of two coprime positive integers can be a square only if both factors are squares, because each prime occurs in just one factor and must have an even exponent.\n\nIf \\(K\\) is odd, the coprime factors are \\(K\\) and \\((K+1)/2\\). Therefore \\(K=a^2\\) for an odd positive integer \\(a\\le11\\), and \\((a^2+1)/2\\) must also be a square. For \\(a=1,3,5,7,9,11\\), these latter values are respectively\n\\[\n1,\\ 5,\\ 13,\\ 25,\\ 41,\\ 61.\n\\]\nOnly one and twenty-five are squares, giving \\(K=1\\) and \\(K=49\\).\n\nIf \\(K\\) is even, the coprime factors are \\(K/2\\) and \\(K+1\\). Thus \\(K=2a^2\\), where \\(1\\le a\\le8\\), and \\(2a^2+1\\) must be a square. For these eight values of \\(a\\), the candidates are\n\\[\n3,\\ 9,\\ 19,\\ 33,\\ 51,\\ 73,\\ 99,\\ 129.\n\\]\nOnly nine is a square, giving \\(a=2\\) and \\(K=8\\).\n\nThe odd and even cases exhaust the bounded possibilities. Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{1\\cdot2}{2}=1^2,\\qquad\n\\frac{8\\cdot9}{2}=6^2,\\qquad\n\\frac{49\\cdot50}{2}=35^2.\n\\]\nAll three nonnegative roots are less than one hundred. Under the nonnegative-integer convention for \\(N\\), the answer is therefore \\(\\boxed{K=1,8,49}\\), choice E.",
