"solution": "Write the positive integral side lengths in increasing order \\(a<b<c\\). This enforces the scalene condition and prevents counting different orders of the same lengths again. The perimeter is an integer less than \\(13\\), so it is at most \\(12\\). With the sides ordered, the triangle inequality we need to check is \\(a+b>c\\).\n\nIf \\(a=1\\), integrality gives \\(c\\ge b+1=a+b\\), so no triangle is possible. If \\(a\\ge4\\), the perimeter is at least \\(4+5+6=15\\), which is too large. Thus only \\(a=2\\) and \\(a=3\\) remain.\n\nFor \\(a=2\\), the conditions \\(b<c\\) and \\(c<b+2\\) force \\(c=b+1\\). The perimeter condition becomes\n\\[\n2+b+(b+1)=2b+3\\le12.\n\\]\nSince \\(b>2\\) is an integer, \\(b=3\\) or \\(b=4\\). These give \\((2,3,4)\\) and \\((2,4,5)\\).\n\nFor \\(a=3\\), the smallest possible other sides are \\(4\\) and \\(5\\). Their perimeter is already \\(12\\), so increasing either side is impossible. This gives only \\((3,4,5)\\).\n\nAll three listed triples satisfy the triangle inequality and the perimeter bound. The cases exhaust the possible shortest sides, and increasing order counts each triple once. Therefore there are \\(\\boxed{3}\\) triangles, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
