"label": "Find the vertex by completing the square",
"explanation": "Completing the square reveals the vertex directly:\n\\[\ny=-\\left(x-\\frac b2\\right)^2+\\frac{b^2}{4}-8.\n\\]\nThe squared term is zero at \\(x=b/2\\), so the vertex has height \\(b^2/4-8\\). A point on the \\(x\\)-axis has height zero. Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{b^2}{4}-8=0,\n\\qquad b^2=32,\n\\qquad b=\\pm4\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nBoth signs give a vertex on the axis. Since \\(\\sqrt2\\) is irrational, neither \\(4\\sqrt2\\) nor \\(-4\\sqrt2\\) is rational: dividing a rational value by \\(4\\) or \\(-4\\) would otherwise make \\(\\sqrt2\\) rational. Thus \\(b\\) can be positive or negative but must be irrational, choice D.",
