"explanation": "Rewrite the fraction to make its distance below \\(2\\) visible:\n\\[\nf(x)=\\frac{2x+3}{x+2}=2-\\frac1{x+2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\ge0\\), the denominator is at least \\(2\\), so it is never zero and\n\\[\n0<\\frac1{x+2}\\le\\frac12.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\frac32\\le f(x)<2.\n\\]\nThe lower endpoint is attained at \\(x=0\\), whereas \\(2\\) is never attained because \\(1/(x+2)\\) is always positive.\n\nTo verify that this is the entire range, take any \\(t\\) with \\(3/2\\le t<2\\). Choose\n\\[\nx=\\frac1{2-t}-2.\n\\]\nHere \\(0<2-t\\le1/2\\), so \\(x\\ge0\\), and substitution gives \\(f(x)=t\\). Thus\n\\[\nS=[3/2,2).\n\\]\nIts greatest lower bound is \\(m=3/2\\), which belongs to \\(S\\). Its least upper bound is \\(M=2\\): it bounds every value above, and values in the interval exceed any proposed smaller upper bound. Therefore \\(M\\notin S\\), and the answer is choice A.",
