"solution": "The two known altitudes allow us to express the same triangle's area using two different bases. Let \\(AB=c\\), \\(CD=h\\), and \\(AE=t\\). These lengths are positive because \\(ABC\\) is a nondegenerate triangle. Then\n\\[\n\\frac12 ch=\\frac12 BC\\cdot t,\n\\qquad BC=\\frac{ch}{t}.\n\\]\nThus \\(BC\\) is determined.\n\nIf \\(D\\ne B\\), triangle \\(BCD\\) is right at \\(D\\), even when \\(D\\) lies on an extension of \\(AB\\). The Pythagorean theorem gives \\(DB^2=BC^2-h^2\\). If \\(D=B\\), then \\(BC=h\\), and the same identity holds with \\(DB=0\\). Consequently, in every case,\n\\[\nDB=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{ch}{t}\\right)^2-h^2}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is nonzero since \\(t>0\\), and the radicand is nonnegative because it equals \\(DB^2\\) in the given triangle. Taking the nonnegative square root determines the length uniquely. No acute-angle condition is needed, so none of choices A through D is correct. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
