"solution": "The desired quadrilateral fills the square except for triangles \\(AEF\\) and \\(BCE\\). This lets us express its area using familiar triangle areas.\n\nLet \\(AE=AF=t\\), where \\(0\\leq t\\leq1\\). Triangle \\(AEF\\) is right with perpendicular legs \\(t\\) and \\(t\\), so its area is \\(t^2/2\\). Triangle \\(BCE\\) is right with perpendicular legs \\(BE=1-t\\) and \\(BC=1\\), so its area is \\((1-t)/2\\).\n\nTherefore the quadrilateral's area is\n\\[\nK=1-\\frac{t^2}{2}-\\frac{1-t}{2}\n=\\frac12+\\frac t2-\\frac{t^2}{2}.\n\\]\nCompleting the square reveals how large this can be:\n\\[\nK=\\frac58-\\frac12\\left(t-\\frac12\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThe subtracted square is nonnegative, so \\(K\\leq5/8\\) throughout the allowed interval. Equality occurs at \\(t=1/2\\), which places \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) at the midpoints of their sides and gives a valid quadrilateral. The maximum area is \\(\\frac58\\) square unit, choice D.",
