"solution": "Because \\(\\log_{10}x\\) is real, its argument must satisfy \\(x>0\\). Also, a logarithmic function with base greater than \\(1\\) is strictly increasing. Since \\(\\log_{10}1=0\\), we have\n\\[\n \\log_{10}x<0\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\log_{10}x<\\log_{10}1\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x<1\n\\]\non the positive domain. Combining the conditions gives exactly \\(0<x<1\\). The endpoint \\(0\\) is excluded because its logarithm is undefined, and \\(1\\) is excluded because its logarithm equals zero.\n\nThus E gives the exact range. However, every number in this range also satisfies \\(-1<x<1\\), choice B, and \\(0<x\\leq1\\), choice C. Consequently, B, C, and E all follow from the condition as written. If the question is interpreted as asking for the exact range, the answer is E.",
