"solution": "The triangle's base and area determine its altitude even though its other sides can vary. Let \\(d\\) be the square's diagonal and \\(h\\) the required altitude. Because the square has positive side length \\(s\\), its diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs \\(s\\) and \\(s\\). The Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nd^2=s^2+s^2=2s^2,\n\\qquad d=s\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe square has area \\(s^2\\), so the equal-area condition is\n\\[\n\\frac12(s\\sqrt2)h=s^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(s>0\\), division gives\n\\[\nh=\\frac{2s^2}{s\\sqrt2}=s\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe triangle area formula applies even if the altitude meets an extension of the base. Having three unequal sides therefore does not change the result: \\(\\boxed{s\\sqrt2}\\), choice A.",
