"explanation": "Take \\(n\\) to be an integer, as in the recorded sequence interpretation. The second base is negative, so an unrestricted real-index interpretation would require further domain information.\n\nEach term of \\(f(n)\\) is a constant times a power. To compare shifted indices, look for an identity involving the square of each base. Write\n\\[\nr=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\qquad\ns=\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2},\n\\]\nand let \\(A=\\frac{5+3\\sqrt5}{10}\\), \\(B=\\frac{5-3\\sqrt5}{10}\\). Then \\(f(n)=Ar^n+Bs^n\\).\n\nFor either choice \\(t=r\\) or \\(t=s\\), direct expansion gives\n\\[\nt^2=\\frac{6\\pm2\\sqrt5}{4}\n=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}\n=t+1.\n\\]\nBoth bases are nonzero, so integer power laws apply even when a shifted exponent is zero or negative. Factoring the lower power now gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{n+1}-t^{n-1}\n&=t^{n-1}(t^2-1)\\\\\n&=t^{n-1}t=t^n.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nApplying this identity separately to the two terms yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n+1)-f(n-1)\n&=A(r^{n+1}-r^{n-1})\\\\\n&\\quad+B(s^{n+1}-s^{n-1})\\\\\n&=Ar^n+Bs^n=f(n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus, for the recorded integer indices, the answer is \\(f(n)\\), choice B.",
