"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The condition that \\(n\\) is a multiple of \\(4\\) fits the repeating cycle\n\\[\ni^0=1,\\qquad i^1=i,\\qquad i^2=-1,\\qquad i^3=-i,\\qquad i^4=1.\n\\]\nWrite \\(n=4m\\), where \\(m\\) is a nonnegative integer. For each integer \\(j\\) from \\(0\\) through \\(m-1\\), a block of four consecutive terms is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(4j+1)i^{4j}+(4j+2)i^{4j+1}\\\\\n&\\quad +(4j+3)i^{4j+2}+(4j+4)i^{4j+3}\\\\\n&=(4j+1)+(4j+2)i-(4j+3)-(4j+4)i\\\\\n&=-2-2i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThese \\(m\\) blocks contain every term through \\(ni^{n-1}\\), leaving only \\((n+1)i^n=n+1\\). Therefore\n\\[\ns=m(-2-2i)+(n+1)\n=\\frac{n+2-ni}{2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(n=0\\) is allowed, there are no four-term blocks and the same formula gives \\(s=1\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{n+2-ni}{2}}\\), choice C.",
