"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "For a fixed job, completion time is inversely proportional to work rate. Let \\(a,b,c>0\\) be the respective rates of \\(A,B,C\\), measured in jobs per unit time. Their combined rates add, so the given time comparisons become\n\\[\nb+c=ma,\\qquad a+c=nb,\\qquad a+b=xc.\n\\]\nLet \\(T=a+b+c\\). Using the total rate lets us express the first two rates without solving for their individual values:\n\\[\nT=(m+1)a=(n+1)b,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\na=\\frac{T}{m+1},\\qquad b=\\frac{T}{n+1}.\n\\]\nSubtracting these from the total gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nc&=T\\left(1-\\frac1{m+1}-\\frac1{n+1}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{T(mn-1)}{(m+1)(n+1)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(m,n>0\\), since they compare positive times. Because \\(c>0\\), this formula also shows that \\(mn>1\\); thus the denominator below cannot vanish. Finally, \\(a+b=xc\\) gives\n\\[\nx=\\frac{a+b}{c}\n=\\frac{m+n+2}{mn-1}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{m+n+2}{mn-1}}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm E\\).",
