"explanation": "First interpret the stated means as formal sum-and-product data. If the proposed numbers are \\(u\\) and \\(v\\), the arithmetic mean gives\n\\[\nu+v=2\\cdot6=12,\n\\]\nand the geometric-mean condition gives\n\\[\nuv=10^2=100.\n\\]\nTo construct a quadratic with these numbers as roots, expand its factors:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x-u)(x-v)\n&=x^2-(u+v)x+uv\\\\\n&=x^2-12x+100.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the formal coefficient calculation produces choice \\(\\mathrm D\\).\n\nWe must also check whether the proposed numbers can be real. For real \\(u,v\\), a square cannot be negative, but the stated sum and product imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u-v)^2\n&=(u+v)^2-4uv\\\\\n&=12^2-4\\cdot100=-256.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is impossible. Under the usual real-number definition of geometric mean, no pair satisfies the question's premises. The equation \\(\\boxed{x^2-12x+100=0}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm D\\), is the formal intended answer, conditional on interpreting the means beyond their ordinary real-number setting.",
