"label": "Model the two time differences",
"explanation": "Let \\(v\\) be his usual rowing speed in still water and \\(c\\) the current speed, both in miles per hour. The upstream return requires \\(v>c\\), and the unequal travel times require \\(c>0\\).\n\nThe current adds to his downstream speed and subtracts from his upstream speed. Using time equal to distance divided by speed, the two conditions become\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{v-c}-\\frac{15}{v+c}=5,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{2v-c}-\\frac{15}{2v+c}=1.\n\\]\nAll denominators are positive because \\(v>c>0\\). Combining the fractions and clearing denominators gives\n\\[\nv^2-c^2=6c,\n\\qquad 4v^2-c^2=30c.\n\\]\nThe shared \\(v^2\\) term can now be eliminated. Subtracting four times the first equation from the second yields\n\\[\n3c^2=6c.\n\\]\nSince \\(c>0\\), division by \\(3c\\) gives \\(c=2\\). Then \\(v^2=4+12=16\\), so the positive rowing speed is \\(v=4>2\\).\n\nThese speeds give the required time differences:\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{6}=5,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{15}{6}-\\frac{15}{10}=1.\n\\]\nThus the current is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles per hour, choice A.",
