"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Each complete rotation carries the wheel \\(11\\) feet, so its rotation time is distance divided by speed. First express both quantities in compatible units.\n\nSince one mile is \\(5280\\) feet and one hour is \\(3600\\) seconds, a positive speed of \\(r\\) miles per hour is \\(5280r/3600\\) feet per second. Thus one rotation takes\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\cdot3600}{5280r}=\\frac{15}{2r}\n\\]\nseconds. At the increased speed \\(r+5\\), the rotation time is \\(15/(2(r+5))\\) seconds. The stated decrease therefore gives\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{2r}-\\frac{15}{2(r+5)}=\\frac14.\n\\]\nBecause \\(r>0\\), both denominators are nonzero. Combining the fractions and clearing denominators yields\n\\[\n\\frac{75}{2r(r+5)}=\\frac14,\n\\qquad r(r+5)=150.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nr^2+5r-150=(r-10)(r+15)=0.\n\\]\nThe negative value \\(-15\\) cannot represent the speed, so \\(r=10\\). At speeds \\(10\\) and \\(15\\) miles per hour, the rotation times are \\(3/4\\) and \\(1/2\\) second, respectively, giving the required decrease of \\(1/4\\) second. The answer is \\(\\boxed{10}\\) miles per hour, choice \\(\\boxed{B}\\).",
