"solution": "Gamma alone takes twice as long as all three together, so Gamma supplies half their combined work rate. Alpha and Beta must supply the other half.\n\nLet \\(t>0\\) be the number of hours needed by all three together, and take the whole job as one unit of work. Their individual completion times are \\(t+6\\), \\(t+1\\), and \\(2t\\). Thus Gamma's rate is \\(1/(2t)\\) job per hour, while the combined rate is \\(1/t\\). Alpha and Beta's combined rate is therefore also \\(1/(2t)\\), giving\n\\[\n\\frac1{t+6}+\\frac1{t+1}=\\frac1{2t}.\n\\]\nAll denominators are positive. Multiplying by \\(2t(t+6)(t+1)\\) gives\n\\[\n2t(2t+7)=(t+6)(t+1).\n\\]\nExpanding and factoring,\n\\[\n3t^2+7t-6=(3t-2)(t+3)=0.\n\\]\nThe root \\(-3\\) is inadmissible because \\(t>0\\), leaving \\(t=2/3\\).\n\nSince Alpha and Beta's combined rate is \\(1/(2t)\\), their completion time is\n\\[\n\\boxed{h=2t=\\frac43\\text{ hours}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{C}\\).",
