"solution": "A decimal integer is divisible by \\(9\\) exactly when its digit sum is divisible by \\(9\\). Thus \\(5b9\\) requires\n\\[\n5+b+9=14+b\n\\]\nto be a multiple of \\(9\\). Since \\(b\\) is a digit, \\(14\\le14+b\\le23\\). The only multiple of \\(9\\) in this interval is \\(18\\), so \\(b=4\\).\n\nIn the addition, the units column gives \\(3+6=9\\), with no carry. The hundreds column already gives \\(2+3=5\\), so there can be no carry from the tens column. Therefore \\(a+2=b=4\\), giving \\(a=2\\).\n\nIndeed, \\(223+326=549=9\\cdot61\\). Hence \\(\\boxed{a+b=6}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{C}\\).",
