"label": "Express every logarithm in terms of log x",
"explanation": "The displayed logarithms require \\(a,b,c,x>0\\), and the fractions require \\(p,q,r\\ne0\\). The common logarithm base is positive and different from \\(1\\).\n\nMultiplying each given equality by its denominator gives\n\\[\n\\log a=p\\log x,\\qquad\n\\log b=q\\log x,\\qquad\n\\log c=r\\log x.\n\\]\nTaking the logarithm of \\(b^2/(ac)\\) turns the products and powers into a linear combination:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log\\left(\\frac{b^2}{ac}\\right)\n&=2\\log b-\\log a-\\log c\\\\\n&=(2q-p-r)\\log x.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBut \\(b^2/(ac)=x^y\\), so the same logarithm is \\(y\\log x\\). Consequently,\n\\[\ny\\log x=(2q-p-r)\\log x.\n\\]\nSince \\(x\\ne1\\), we have \\(\\log x\\ne0\\), so division is valid. Thus\n\\[\n\\boxed{y=2q-p-r},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
