"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Label the four pibs \\(A,B,C,D\\). By \\(\\mathrm{P}_2\\), every pair of distinct pibs has exactly one common maa. By \\(\\mathrm{P}_3\\), that maa belongs to exactly those two pibs.\n\nDifferent pairs must give different maas: a maa serving two different pairs would belong to at least three pibs, contradicting \\(\\mathrm{P}_3\\). Conversely, every maa belongs to exactly two pibs, so it comes from one of these pairs. Thus the maas correspond exactly to\n\\[\nAB,\\ AC,\\ AD,\\ BC,\\ BD,\\ CD.\n\\]\nThere are six pairs, proving \\(\\mathrm{T}_1\\), with neither omitted nor repeated maas.\n\nThe maas in pib \\(A\\) are precisely those represented by \\(AB,AC,AD\\), so \\(A\\) contains three maas. The same argument applies to each of the four pibs, proving \\(\\mathrm{T}_2\\).\n\nFinally, take any maa and call its two pibs \\(A,B\\). Another maa shares no pib with it exactly when its pair uses neither \\(A\\) nor \\(B\\). The only such pair is \\(CD\\), and it represents exactly one maa. This proves \\(\\mathrm{T}_3\\).\n\nAll three theorems follow, so the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{E}}\\).",
