"solution": "Replace each \\(*\\) by exponentiation while preserving the parentheses. Since \\(a>0\\), the power-of-a-power law applies to the given real exponents:\n\\[\n(a*b)^n=(a^b)^n=a^{bn}=a*(bn).\n\\]\nThus statement D holds for every allowed choice of the variables.\n\nThe other statements fail for positive values. For example, take \\(a=2\\), \\(b=3\\), \\(c=2\\), and \\(n=2\\). Then\n\\[\na*b=8\\ne9=b*a,\n\\]\n\\[\na*(b*c)=2^9=512\\ne64=(a*b)*c,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\na*(b^n)=2^9=512\\ne64=(a*n)*b.\n\\]\nThese disprove A, B, and C. Since D is always true, E is false. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
