"responseType": "choice",
"question": "If \\[ \\begin{aligned} s={}&(1+2^{-\\frac{1}{32}})(1+2^{-\\frac{1}{16}})\\\\ &\\cdot(1+2^{-\\frac{1}{8}})(1+2^{-\\frac{1}{4}})\\\\ &\\cdot(1+2^{-\\frac{1}{2}}), \\end{aligned} \\] then \\(s\\) is equal to:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac12(1-2^{-\\frac{1}{32}})^{-1}\\)",
"B": "\\((1-2^{-\\frac{1}{32}})^{-1}\\)",
"C": "\\(1-2^{-\\frac{1}{32}}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac12(1-2^{-\\frac{1}{32}})\\)",
"E": "\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"acceptedResponses": [
"A"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The exponents double from one factor to the next, suggesting repeated use of \\((1-u)(1+u)=1-u^2\\). Let\n\\[\nt=2^{-1/32}.\n\\]\nThen \\(0<t<1\\), and the product becomes\n\\[\ns=(1+t)(1+t^2)(1+t^4)(1+t^8)(1+t^{16}).\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(1-t\\) makes the first two factors a difference of squares. The resulting factor combines with the next one in the same way:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1-t)s\n&=(1-t^2)(1+t^2)(1+t^4)(1+t^8)(1+t^{16})\\\\\n&=(1-t^4)(1+t^4)(1+t^8)(1+t^{16})\\\\\n&=1-t^{32}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere the last line continues the same identity through the remaining factors. Since \\(t^{32}=2^{-1}=\\tfrac12\\), we obtain \\((1-t)s=\\tfrac12\\). Also \\(1-t>0\\), so division is valid:\n\\[\ns=\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2(1-2^{-1/32})}}.\n\\]\nThis is choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
