"explanation": "Reciprocals require nonzero terms, but the statement does not require positive terms. This matters when taking a square root and when dividing by \\(S'\\).\n\nWrite the nonzero real terms as \\(a_j=ar^{j-1}\\), for \\(1\\le j\\le n\\), where \\(a\\ne0\\) and \\(r\\ne0\\). Terms equally far from opposite ends have the same product:\n\\[\na_j a_{n+1-j}=a^2r^{n-1}=K.\n\\]\nHere \\(K\\ne0\\). Thus each reciprocal is the opposite term divided by \\(K\\), giving\n\\[\nS'=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\frac1{a_j}\n=\\frac1K\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{n+1-j}\n=\\frac S K.\n\\]\nMultiplying all \\(n\\) opposite-term identities also gives\n\\[\nP^2=\\prod_{j=1}^n(a_j a_{n+1-j})=K^n.\n\\]\nConsequently, \\(S=KS'\\) and \\(P^2=K^n\\) hold without any assumption of positivity.\n\nIf all terms are positive, then \\(S'>0\\), \\(K=S/S'>0\\), and \\(P>0\\). Taking the positive square root is then justified, yielding\n\\[\nP=\\boxed{\\left(\\frac S{S'}\\right)^{n/2}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\). For even \\(n\\) with \\(S'\\ne0\\), the same formula also follows by grouping the terms into \\(n/2\\) opposite pairs, without needing positivity.\n\nHowever, the printed conditions allow the progression \\((-1,-1,-1)\\). It has \\(n=3\\), \\(S=S'=-3\\), and \\(P=-1\\), whereas choice B gives \\(1\\). In fact, every listed expression is positive for this example, so none equals \\(P\\).\n\nThere is also a possible zero denominator: the progression \\((1,-1)\\) has well-defined reciprocals but \\(S=S'=0\\). Thus \\(S/S'\\) is undefined. Choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\) is valid under a positive-term assumption, but no listed formula is universally valid as written.",
