"solution": "First assume that the stated square units are square inches. Let \\(b=AC\\) and \\(c=AB\\), both measured in inches. The geometric mean condition says\n\\[\n\\sqrt{bc}=12,\n\\qquad bc=144.\n\\]\nThus we know the product of the two sides enclosing \\(A\\), even though their individual lengths are unknown.\n\nTo connect this product to the area, let \\(h\\) be the altitude from \\(C\\) to the line \\(AB\\). If \\(A\\) is acute, the right triangle at the altitude gives \\(h=b\\sin A\\). If \\(A\\) is obtuse, the altitude meets the extension of \\(AB\\), and \\(h=b\\sin(180^\\circ-A)=b\\sin A\\). If \\(A=90^\\circ\\), then \\(h=b=b\\sin A\\) as well. Therefore the base-times-height area formula gives, in every case,\n\\[\nK=\\frac12ch=\\frac12bc\\sin A.\n\\]\nUnder the square-inch assumption,\n\\[\n64=\\frac12(144)\\sin A,\n\\qquad \\sin A=\\frac89.\n\\]\nThis gives choice D under that assumption.\n\nThe unspecified unit prevents an unconditional answer. If one square unit equals \\(k\\) square inches, then the same reasoning instead gives \\(64k=72\\sin A\\), or \\(\\sin A=8k/9\\). For example, \\(k=1\\) gives \\(8/9\\), whereas \\(k=1/4\\) gives \\(2/9\\). Both are attainable with \\(AB=AC=12\\) inches and an appropriate acute angle. Thus the displayed conditions do not determine one sine value unless the area unit is fixed.",
