"solution": "A small hundreds digit and a large units digit are promising because place value makes the hundreds digit much more costly in the numerator. The allowed number \\(189\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{189}{1+8+9}=\\frac{189}{18}=10.5.\n\\]\nWe will prove that no allowed number has a smaller quotient.\n\nLet \\(a,b,c\\) be the hundreds, tens, and units digits. They are distinct integers from \\(1\\) to \\(9\\). Write\n\\[\nN=100a+10b+c,\\qquad S=a+b+c>0.\n\\]\nTo compare \\(N/S\\) with \\(10.5=21/2\\), clear the positive denominator:\n\\[\n2N-21S=179a-b-19c.\n\\]\nWe have \\(a\\ge1\\). Also, \\(b+19c\\le179\\): if \\(c=9\\), distinctness gives \\(b\\le8\\), so \\(b+19c\\le8+171=179\\). If \\(c\\le8\\), then \\(b+19c\\le9+152=161\\).\n\nIt follows that\n\\[\n2N-21S\\ge179-179=0,\n\\qquad \\frac NS\\ge\\frac{21}{2}.\n\\]\nThe digits of \\(189\\) are distinct and nonzero, and its quotient attains this bound. Thus the minimum is \\(\\boxed{10.5}\\), choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
