"solution": "Interpret partial overlap as positive common area without complete coincidence. Label the common hypotenuse \\(AB\\). The triangles must lie on the same side of \\(AB\\), and their \\(30^\\circ\\) and \\(60^\\circ\\) angles at its endpoints must be interchanged; otherwise they would coincide completely.\n\nAt each endpoint, the side making \\(30^\\circ\\) with \\(AB\\) gives the tighter boundary next to the base. Thus the common region is triangle \\(APB\\), where these two sides meet, with\n\\[\n\\angle PAB=\\angle PBA=30^\\circ.\n\\]\nThese equal base angles make \\(APB\\) isosceles. Its altitude \\(PM\\) therefore bisects \\(AB\\), so \\(AM=6\\).\n\nWrite \\(PM=h>0\\). In right triangle \\(AMP\\), the side opposite \\(30^\\circ\\) is half the hypotenuse, so \\(AP=2h\\). The Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\n6^2+h^2=(2h)^2,\n\\qquad 3h^2=36,\n\\qquad h=2\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nConsequently, the common area is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot12\\cdot2\\sqrt3=\\boxed{12\\sqrt3},\n\\]\nwhich is choice D.",
