"label": "Locate the center with a right triangle",
"explanation": "Use the recorded meaning of the circumscribed circle: it passes through the sector's vertex and both arc endpoints and encloses the sector. Let these three points be \\(O,A,B\\), with \\(OA=OB=6\\), and put \\(h=\\theta/2\\). Since \\(\\theta\\) is acute, \\(0<h<45^\\circ\\).\n\nLet \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(OA\\). Construct \\(P\\) where the perpendicular bisector of \\(OA\\) meets the internal bisector of \\(\\angle AOB\\). The perpendicular-bisector property gives \\(PO=PA\\). Reflection across the angle bisector fixes \\(P\\) and swaps \\(A\\) with \\(B\\), so \\(PA=PB\\). Thus \\(P\\) is the center of the circle through \\(O,A,B\\), and its radius is \\(R=OP\\).\n\nTriangle \\(OMP\\) is right, with \\(OM=3\\) and \\(\\angle MOP=h\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\cos h=\\frac{3}{R},\n\\qquad R=\\frac{3}{\\cos h}.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\cos h>0\\), so division is valid.\n\nWe can also check that this circle encloses the entire sector. The point \\(O\\) is already on the circle. For any other sector point \\(Q\\), write \\(\\rho=OQ\\), so \\(0<\\rho\\le6\\), and let \\(\\phi=\\angle POQ\\), so \\(0\\le\\phi\\le h\\). Since cosine decreases on this acute-angle range,\n\\[\n2R\\cos\\phi\\ge2R\\cos h=6\\ge\\rho.\n\\]\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem after projecting \\(Q\\) onto the line \\(OP\\) gives\n\\[\nPQ^2=R^2+\\rho^2-2R\\rho\\cos\\phi\n=R^2+\\rho(\\rho-2R\\cos\\phi)\n\\le R^2.\n\\]\nThus every sector point is on or inside the constructed circle. Its radius is \\(\\boxed{3\\sec\\frac{\\theta}{2}}\\), choice D.",
