"explanation": "The expressions inside the absolute values change sign at \\(-2\\) and \\(1\\). Splitting there lets us remove both absolute-value signs correctly:\n\\[\n|x-1|+|x+2|=\n\\begin{cases}\n-2x-1,&x<-2,\\\\\n3,&-2\\le x\\le1,\\\\\n2x+1,&x>1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor \\(x<-2\\), the inequality \\(-2x-1<5\\) gives \\(x>-3\\), so this case contributes \\(-3<x<-2\\).\n\nFor \\(-2\\le x\\le1\\), the sum is \\(3<5\\), so the entire interval is included.\n\nFor \\(x>1\\), the inequality \\(2x+1<5\\) gives \\(x<2\\), so this case contributes \\(1<x<2\\).\n\nThese three cases cover every real number. Combining their solutions gives \\(\\boxed{-3<x<2}\\), choice A. The endpoints are excluded because the original inequality is strict.",
