"explanation": "Because the identity holds for every pair of real inputs, choose inputs whose sums match the statements.\n\nTaking both inputs to be zero gives \\(f(0)^2=f(0)\\). Since \\(f(0)>0\\), division by \\(f(0)\\) gives \\(f(0)=1\\), proving I.\n\nTaking the inputs to be \\(a\\) and \\(-a\\) gives\n\\[\nf(a)f(-a)=f(0)=1.\n\\]\nSince \\(f(a)>0\\), division is valid and yields \\(f(-a)=1/f(a)\\), proving II.\n\nApplying the identity repeatedly gives\n\\[\nf(3a)=f(2a)f(a)=f(a)^3.\n\\]\nTaking the unique real cube root proves \\(f(a)=\\sqrt[3]{f(3a)}\\), so III is true.\n\nTo test IV, consider \\(f(x)=1\\) for every real \\(x\\). This function is positive and satisfies \\(f(a)f(b)=1=f(a+b)\\), but \\(f(1)=f(0)\\), so it is not strictly increasing. Thus IV need not hold. Exactly I, II, and III must be true, choice D.",
