"solution": "We can find the symmetric sum without finding the individual roots. Because \\(p,q,r\\) are the three distinct roots of the monic cubic,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x-p)(x-q)(x-r)\n&=x^3-(p+q+r)x^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(pq+pr+qr)x-pqr.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x^2\\) comes from selecting one of \\(-p,-q,-r\\) and two copies of \\(x\\); the coefficient of \\(x\\) comes from selecting two of those constants. Comparing with the given polynomial therefore gives\n\\[\np+q+r=1,\n\\qquad pq+pr+qr=1.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\np^2+q^2+r^2=(p+q+r)^2-2(pq+pr+qr)=-1.\n\\]\nThese algebraic identities also hold for nonreal roots.\n\nEvery root \\(t\\) satisfies \\(t^3=t^2-t+2\\). Applying this to all three roots and adding gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np^3+q^3+r^3\n&=(p^2+q^2+r^2)-(p+q+r)+6\\\\\n&=-1-1+6=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(4\\) is absent from choices A through D, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
