"id": "parallel-lines-and-similarity",
"label": "Construct equal parallel segments",
"explanation": "We can transfer the midpoint condition to segments parallel to \\(EF\\), then compare those segments with \\(GF\\) and \\(GE\\).\n\nThrough \\(B\\) and \\(C\\), draw lines parallel to \\(EF\\), meeting line \\(AM\\) at \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\), respectively. Extend \\(AM\\) as needed. Because \\(MB\\) and \\(MC\\) lie on one line, \\(MP\\) and \\(MQ\\) lie on another, and \\(BP\\parallel CQ\\), triangles \\(MBP\\) and \\(MCQ\\) are similar by their equal angles. Their corresponding sides \\(MB\\) and \\(MC\\) are equal, so\n\\[\nBP=CQ.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(FG\\parallel BP\\), with \\(A,F,B\\) collinear and \\(A,G,P\\) collinear. Hence triangles \\(AFG\\) and \\(ABP\\) are similar, giving\n\\[\n\\frac{GF}{BP}=\\frac{AF}{AB}.\n\\]\nLikewise, triangles \\(AEG\\) and \\(ACQ\\) are similar, so\n\\[\n\\frac{EG}{CQ}=\\frac{AE}{AC}.\n\\]\nAll these segments have positive length in the given configuration. Dividing the two ratios and using \\(BP=CQ\\) and \\(AE=2AF\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{EG}{GF}\n=\\frac{AE/AC}{AF/AB}\n=\\frac{2AF/16}{AF/12}\n=\\frac32.\n\\]\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac32}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
