"explanation": "The coefficients in the first equation suggest setting\n\\[\nX=x,\\qquad Y=2y,\\qquad Z=4z.\n\\]\nThis also makes the other equations symmetric: \\(XY+YZ+ZX\\) is twice the original second left side, and \\(XYZ=8xyz\\). Thus\n\\[\nX+Y+Z=12,\\qquad XY+YZ+ZX=44,\\qquad XYZ=48.\n\\]\nThese three expressions determine the coefficients of a polynomial whose roots are \\(X,Y,Z\\). Indeed, multiplying gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t-X)(t-Y)(t-Z)\n&=t^3-(X+Y+Z)t^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(XY+YZ+ZX)t-XYZ\\\\\n&=t^3-12t^2+44t-48.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTo factor this cubic, testing \\(t=2\\) gives zero, so \\(t-2\\) is a factor. Division and then quadratic factorization give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^3-12t^2+44t-48\n&=(t-2)(t^2-10t+24)\\\\\n&=(t-2)(t-4)(t-6).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTherefore \\(X,Y,Z\\) must be \\(2,4,6\\), each appearing once. In particular, each of the three distinct numbers \\(2,4,6\\) makes \\((t-X)(t-Y)(t-Z)\\) zero, so each must equal one of its three listed roots.\n\nConversely, every ordering of \\(2,4,6\\) has sum \\(12\\), pairwise-product sum \\(44\\), and product \\(48\\), so every such ordering satisfies the transformed equations. There are \\(3\\) choices for \\(X\\), then \\(2\\) for \\(Y\\), and \\(1\\) for \\(Z\\), giving\n\\[\n3\\cdot2\\cdot1=6.\n\\]\nThe inverse substitution \\(x=X\\), \\(y=Y/2\\), \\(z=Z/4\\) is unique and reverses every transformation, so these orderings give all original solutions without duplication. The answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
