"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Each given equality of lengths identifies an isosceles triangle, so we can find the angles beside \\(\\angle EDF\\) first.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/1977_AMC_12_Q04_question_fig1.svg)\n\nSince \\(AB=AC\\), the base angles of \\(ABC\\) are equal. Thus\n\\[\n\\angle ABC=\\angle BCA=\\frac{180^\\circ-80^\\circ}{2}=50^\\circ.\n\\]\nBecause \\(CE=CD\\), triangle \\(CDE\\) is isosceles with vertex angle \\(\\angle ECD=50^\\circ\\). Its two base angles are therefore \\(65^\\circ\\), giving \\(\\angle CDE=65^\\circ\\). Likewise, \\(BF=BD\\) makes triangle \\(BDF\\) isosceles with vertex angle \\(\\angle DBF=50^\\circ\\), so \\(\\angle BDF=65^\\circ\\).\n\nThe rays \\(DC\\) and \\(DB\\) form a straight angle, and \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) lie inside that angle. Hence\n\\[\n\\angle EDF=180^\\circ-65^\\circ-65^\\circ=\\boxed{50^\\circ},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\). As shown, \\(D\\) is between \\(B\\) and \\(C\\); placing \\(D\\) at either endpoint would make \\(F=D\\) or \\(E=D\\), so the requested angle would be undefined.",
