"solution": "The circle has center \\(O=(0,0)\\) and radius \\(\\sqrt r\\). For tangency, the perpendicular distance from \\(O\\) to the line must equal this radius.\n\nThe line meets the coordinate axes at \\(U=(r,0)\\) and \\(V=(0,r)\\). Since \\(r>0\\), triangle \\(OUV\\) is a right triangle with both legs equal to \\(r\\). Thus its hypotenuse is \\(UV=r\\sqrt2\\), and its area is \\(r^2/2\\).\n\nLet \\(h\\) be the perpendicular distance from \\(O\\) to line \\(UV\\). Computing the same triangle's area with base \\(UV\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{r^2}{2}=\\frac{r\\sqrt2\\,h}{2},\n\\qquad h=\\frac r{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\nTangency therefore requires\n\\[\n\\frac r{\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt r,\n\\qquad \\frac{r^2}{2}=r.\n\\]\nBoth lengths are positive, so squaring preserves equality. Dividing by \\(r>0\\) gives \\(r=2\\). For this value, both the radius and the perpendicular distance are \\(\\sqrt2\\), confirming tangency. Hence \\(\\boxed{r=2}\\), choice C.",
