"id": "one-intersection-discriminant",
"label": "Require exactly one intersection",
"explanation": "A tangent line has exactly one point in common with a circle. Substitution turns that geometric condition into a condition on the number of real roots.\n\nFrom the line equation, \\(y=r-x\\). Substituting into the circle equation gives\n\\[\nx^2+(r-x)^2=r,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n2x^2-2rx+r^2-r=0.\n\\]\nEach real root \\(x\\) determines exactly one intersection point, because \\(y=r-x\\). The leading coefficient is always \\(2\\), so this quadratic has exactly one real root precisely when its discriminant is zero:\n\\[\n\\Delta=(-2r)^2-4\\cdot2(r^2-r)\n=4r(2-r)=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(r>0\\), this gives \\(r=2\\). At \\(r=2\\), the intersection equation becomes \\(2(x-1)^2=0\\), yielding only \\((x,y)=(1,1)\\), so the line is indeed tangent. Therefore \\(\\boxed{r=2}\\), choice C.",
