"explanation": "The different bases suggest changing all three terms to one base. Since \\(x>0\\) and \\(x\\ne1\\), we can use \\(x\\) itself as that base. The change-of-base formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\log_a x}=\\log_x a\n\\qquad(a=3,4,5).\n\\]\nNow the product rule combines the sum:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac1{\\log_3x}+\\frac1{\\log_4x}+\\frac1{\\log_5x}\n&=\\log_x3+\\log_x4+\\log_x5\\\\\n&=\\log_x(3\\cdot4\\cdot5)\\\\\n&=\\log_x60\\\\\n&=\\frac1{\\log_{60}x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe original denominators and \\(\\log_{60}x\\) are nonzero because \\(x\\ne1\\). Thus the answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
