"solution": "The number system is not specified. We first derive the equation forced by the progression, then check whether a real progression is possible.\n\nThe useful observation is that the three displayed expressions always sum to zero:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(y-z)+y(z-x)+z(x-y)\n&=xy-xz+yz-xy+xz-yz\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nLet \\(u=x(y-z)\\), which is nonzero by hypothesis. Because the terms form a geometric progression in the listed order, they are \\(u,ur,ur^2\\). Their zero sum gives\n\\[\nu(1+r+r^2)=0.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(u\\ne0\\) yields\n\\[\nr^2+r+1=0.\n\\]\nThere is an important domain qualification: for every real \\(r\\),\n\\[\nr^2+r+1=\\left(r+\\frac12\\right)^2+\\frac34>0.\n\\]\nThus no real progression satisfies the premise. If complex numbers are allowed, the required necessary equation is \\(r^2+r+1=0\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\); under a real-only interpretation, there is no such progression.",
