"solution": "Let \\(N>1\\) be an integer satisfying the conditions. Having remainder \\(1\\) when divided by each integer from \\(2\\) through \\(11\\) means that \\(N-1\\) is a positive multiple of every one of these divisors. Thus we need their least common multiple.\n\nFor each prime, take the largest power needed by any divisor in the list. These powers are \\(2^3\\), \\(3^2\\), \\(5\\), \\(7\\), and \\(11\\). Their product contains every required prime factor, so\n\\[\nL=\\operatorname{lcm}(2,3,\\ldots,11)\n=2^3\\cdot3^2\\cdot5\\cdot7\\cdot11\n=27{,}720.\n\\]\nEvery admissible integer therefore has the form \\(1+tL\\), where \\(t\\) is a positive integer. Conversely, each such integer leaves remainder \\(1\\) for every allowed divisor, since \\(1<k\\).\n\nThe two smallest occur at \\(t=1\\) and \\(t=2\\), giving \\(27{,}721\\) and \\(55{,}441\\). Their difference is \\(\\boxed{27{,}720}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
