"responseType": "choice",
"question": "For each positive number \\(x,\\) let \\[ f(x)= \\frac{\\begin{gathered} \\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^6\\\\ {}-\\left(x^6+\\frac1{x^6}\\right)-2 \\end{gathered}} {\\begin{gathered} \\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^3\\\\ {}+\\left(x^3+\\frac1{x^3}\\right) \\end{gathered}}. \\] The minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(6\\)"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"acceptedResponses": [
"E"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The denominator suggests looking for a difference of squares in the numerator. Let\n\\[\nA=\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^3,\n\\qquad B=x^3+\\frac1{x^3}.\n\\]\nBecause\n\\[\nB^2=x^6+2+\\frac1{x^6},\n\\]\nthe numerator is \\(A^2-B^2\\), while the denominator is \\(A+B\\). For \\(x>0\\), both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are positive, so cancellation is valid:\n\\[\nf(x)=\\frac{(A-B)(A+B)}{A+B}=A-B.\n\\]\nWe now need only a cubic expansion:\n\\[\n\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^3\n=x^3+3x+\\frac3x+\\frac1{x^3}.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(B\\) gives\n\\[\nf(x)=3\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right).\n\\]\nTo find its minimum, express the amount above 6 as a nonnegative square:\n\\[\nf(x)-6\n=3\\left(x+\\frac1x-2\\right)\n=\\frac{3(x-1)^2}{x}\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe denominator is positive, and equality holds at \\(x=1\\), which is allowed in the original expression. Therefore the minimum is \\(\\boxed{6}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
