"question": "The equations of \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are \\(y=mx\\) and \\(y=nx,\\) respectively. Suppose \\(L_1\\) makes twice as large an angle with the horizontal (measured counterclockwise from the positive \\(x\\)-axis) as does \\(L_2,\\) and that \\(L_1\\) has \\(4\\) times the slope of \\(L_2.\\) If \\(L_1\\) is not horizontal, then \\(mn\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)",
"B": "\\(-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "\\(-2\\)",
"E": "not uniquely determined by the given information"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"acceptedResponses": [
"C"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "A line's slope is the tangent of its inclination, so the doubled angle suggests the tangent addition formula. Let \\(\\theta\\) be the inclination of \\(L_2\\); the inclination of \\(L_1\\) is \\(2\\theta\\). Inclinations lie in \\([0,180^\\circ)\\), and \\(L_1\\) is not horizontal, so \\(0<\\theta<90^\\circ\\). Consequently \\(n>0\\). Since \\(m=4n>0\\), the inclination \\(2\\theta\\) is also acute.\n\nWe have \\(n=\\tan\\theta\\), and tangent addition gives\n\\[\nm=\\tan(2\\theta)=\\frac{2n}{1-n^2}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is nonzero because \\(2\\theta\\) is not a right angle. Substituting \\(m=4n\\) and dividing by \\(n\\ne0\\) gives\n\\[\n4=\\frac{2}{1-n^2},\n\\qquad n^2=\\frac12.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nmn=4n^2=2,\n\\]\nwhich is choice C.",
