"id": "angle-bisector",
"label": "Build a triangle with an angle bisector",
"explanation": "Represent each slope as a vertical rise over a horizontal run of \\(1\\). Let \\(\\theta\\) be the inclination of \\(L_2\\), so \\(L_1\\) has inclination \\(2\\theta\\). The inclination convention and nonhorizontality give \\(0<\\theta<90^\\circ\\), hence \\(n>0\\). Then \\(m=4n>0\\), so \\(2\\theta\\) is acute as well.\n\nDefine\n\\[\nO=(0,0),\\quad A=(1,0),\\quad\nB=(1,m),\\quad P=(1,n).\n\\]\nThe equations of the lines put \\(B\\) on \\(L_1\\) and \\(P\\) on \\(L_2\\). Because \\(m=4n>0\\), the point \\(P\\) lies inside segment \\(AB\\), with\n\\[\nAP=n,\\qquad PB=3n,\\qquad OA=1.\n\\]\nThe rays \\(OA,OP,OB\\) have inclinations \\(0,\\theta,2\\theta\\), so \\(OP\\) bisects \\(\\angle AOB\\). The internal angle bisector theorem gives\n\\[\n\\frac{OA}{OB}=\\frac{AP}{PB}=\\frac13,\n\\]\nso \\(OB=3\\). Triangle \\(OAB\\) is right at \\(A\\), and the Pythagorean theorem yields\n\\[\n1+m^2=9,\n\\qquad m^2=8.\n\\]\nSince \\(n=m/4\\), we obtain \\(mn=m^2/4=2\\), choice C.",
