"solution": "The two factors differ only in the sign of \\(c\\), so the difference-of-squares formula turns the premise into a relation among squared side lengths:\n\\[\n(a+b+c)(a+b-c)=(a+b)^2-c^2=3ab.\n\\]\nExpanding the square and rearranging gives\n\\[\na^2+b^2-c^2=ab.\n\\]\nThis expression is useful because the law of cosines connects it to the angle opposite \\(c\\). Let that angle be \\(C\\). The sides of lengths \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) enclose \\(C\\), so\n\\[\na^2+b^2-c^2=2ab\\cos C.\n\\]\nSince triangle side lengths are positive, \\(2ab\\ne0\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\cos C=\\frac{ab}{2ab}=\\frac12.\n\\]\nA triangle angle lies strictly between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(180^\\circ\\), where the unique angle with cosine \\(1/2\\) is \\(\\boxed{60^\\circ}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
