"id": "extremal-polynomial-and-reciprocals",
"label": "Find an attainable root and bound every other root",
"explanation": "For a positive input, making all three coefficients as negative as allowed gives the greatest possible cancellation of the leading cubic term. Consider the allowed polynomial\n\\[\ng(t)=t^3-2t^2-2t-2.\n\\]\nIt satisfies\n\\[\ng\\left(\\frac52\\right)=-\\frac{31}{8}<0,\n\\qquad g(3)=1>0.\n\\]\nSince a polynomial is continuous, \\(g\\) has a root \\(R\\) with \\(5/2<R<3\\).\n\nWe now prove that no allowed equation can have a positive root larger than \\(R\\). If \\(t>0\\) is any allowed root, then \\(-a_i\\le2\\) gives\n\\[\nt^3=-a_2t^2-a_1t-a_0\n\\le2t^2+2t+2.\n\\]\nDividing by the positive number \\(t^3\\), we obtain\n\\[\n1\\le H(t),\n\\qquad\nH(t)=\\frac2t+\\frac2{t^2}+\\frac2{t^3}.\n\\]\nFor positive inputs, increasing \\(t\\) strictly decreases each reciprocal term, so \\(H\\) is strictly decreasing. Because \\(g(R)=0\\), division of that equation by \\(R^3\\) gives \\(H(R)=1\\). Thus \\(t>R\\) would imply \\(H(t)<1\\), contradicting the necessary inequality.\n\nThe number \\(R\\) is itself attained by an allowed polynomial and bounds every allowed positive root. It is therefore the largest such root, so\n\\[\n\\frac52<r<3.\n\\]\nThis lies in the interval \\(\\boxed{\\frac52\\le r<3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
