"label": "Use the final digits to examine remainders",
"explanation": "The final two base-eight digits determine the remainder on division by \\(16\\). If the square is \\(N=(ab3c)_8\\), then\n\\[\nN=512a+64b+24+c.\n\\]\nSince \\(512a\\) and \\(64b\\) are multiples of \\(16\\), the remainder is \\(8+c\\). A base-eight digit satisfies \\(0\\le c\\le7\\), so this remainder lies between \\(8\\) and \\(15\\).\n\nTo find all possible square remainders, write an integer root as \\(8q+r\\), where \\(0\\le r\\le7\\). The difference\n\\[\n(8q+r)^2-r^2=16q(4q+r)\n\\]\nis divisible by \\(16\\). Squaring \\(r=0,1,\\ldots,7\\) therefore shows that the only possible square remainders are \\(0,1,4,9\\).\n\nOnly \\(9\\) lies between \\(8\\) and \\(15\\), so \\(8+c=9\\), giving \\(c=1\\). This digit is attainable: \\(27^2=729=(1331)_8\\). Thus the answer is \\(\\boxed{1}\\), choice B.",
