"label": "Substitute and use the nonzero condition",
"explanation": "The second equation gives \\(x=16y\\), while the first gives \\(x=2y^2\\). Equating these expressions eliminates \\(x\\):\n\\[\n2y^2=16y.\n\\]\nSince \\(x=16y\\ne0\\), we know \\(y\\ne0\\). We may therefore divide by \\(2y\\), obtaining \\(y=8\\). Thus\n\\[\nx=16\\cdot8=\\boxed{128},\n\\]\nchoice E. Indeed, \\(128/2=8^2\\) and \\(128/4=4\\cdot8\\), so both original equations hold.",
