"solution": "First multiply \\(x^5\\) into the third factor. This removes its denominators and makes the remaining multiplication easier. For \\(x\\ne0\\),\n\\[\nx^5\\left(1+\\frac2x+\\frac3{x^3}\\right)=x^5+2x^4+3x^2.\n\\]\nNow distribute both terms of \\(x+\\frac1x\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)(x^5+2x^4+3x^2)\n&=x^6+2x^5+3x^3+x^4+2x^3+3x\\\\\n&=x^6+2x^5+x^4+5x^3+3x.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe simplified expression is a polynomial, and its largest exponent with a nonzero coefficient is \\(6\\). Thus its degree is \\(\\boxed{6}\\), choice C. The original product requires \\(x\\ne0\\); the polynomial expression agrees with it on that domain.",
