"id": "discriminant-intersection-equation",
"label": "Use the discriminant",
"explanation": "The graphs intersect exactly when the equation \\(f(x)=g(x)\\) has a real solution. Rearranging it gives\n\\[\nx^2+2(b-a)x+1-2ab=0.\n\\]\nIts leading coefficient is always 1, so it is a quadratic for every pair \\((a,b)\\). Its discriminant is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta\n&=[2(b-a)]^2-4(1-2ab)\\\\\n&=4(a^2+b^2-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nA quadratic has no real roots exactly when its discriminant is negative. Hence the graphs do not intersect precisely when\n\\[\na^2+b^2<1.\n\\]\nThe boundary \\(a^2+b^2=1\\) gives a repeated real root, so it is excluded. The resulting region is the open disk of radius 1, with area \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
