"solution": "Because there are \\(30\\) questions, we may regard the old starting score of \\(30\\) as one initial point assigned to each question. Under that system, a correct answer then contributes \\(1+4=5\\) points, a wrong answer contributes \\(1-1=0\\), and an unanswered question contributes \\(1\\).\n\nThe new system gives \\(5\\), \\(0\\), and \\(2\\) points for these same three outcomes. Thus each unanswered question increases the score by exactly one point, while correct and wrong answers cause no change.\n\nLet \\(u\\) be the number unanswered. Comparing the two totals gives\n\\[\n84+u=93,\n\\qquad u=9.\n\\]\nThese totals are feasible: the new score leaves \\(93-2\\cdot9=75\\) points from correct answers, giving \\(15\\) correct and \\(30-15-9=6\\) wrong. The old score is indeed \\(30+4\\cdot15-6=84\\). Therefore John leaves \\(9\\) questions unanswered, choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
