"solution": "Let the integer roots be \\(u\\) and \\(v\\). Expanding\n\\[\n(x-u)(x-v)=x^2-(u+v)x+uv\n\\]\nand comparing with the given quadratic gives\n\\[\nu+v=-p,\\qquad uv=-444p.\n\\]\nThese relations let us use the fact that \\(p\\) is prime. Since \\(p\\) divides \\(uv\\), it divides at least one of \\(u,v\\), as follows from their prime factorizations. Their sum is divisible by \\(p\\), so the other root is divisible by \\(p\\) as well.\n\nWrite \\(u=pa\\) and \\(v=pb\\), where \\(a,b\\) are integers. Then\n\\[\na+b=-1,\\qquad pab=-444.\n\\]\nThus \\(p\\) must be a prime factor of\n\\[\n444=2^2\\cdot3\\cdot37,\n\\]\nso \\(p\\) is \\(2,3\\), or \\(37\\).\n\nThe product \\(ab\\) is negative. Label the positive one \\(a=t\\), where \\(t\\) is a positive integer. Since \\(a+b=-1\\), we have \\(b=-(t+1)\\), and hence\n\\[\nt(t+1)=\\frac{444}{p}.\n\\]\nFor \\(p=2\\), the required value is \\(222\\), but\n\\[\n14\\cdot15<222<15\\cdot16.\n\\]\nFor \\(p=3\\), the required value is \\(148\\), but\n\\[\n11\\cdot12<148<12\\cdot13.\n\\]\nThe products \\(t(t+1)\\) strictly increase for positive integer \\(t\\), so neither case is possible. For \\(p=37\\), the required value is \\(12=3\\cdot4\\), giving roots \\(111\\) and \\(-148\\). Indeed,\n\\[\nx^2+37x-444\\cdot37=(x-111)(x+148).\n\\]\nTherefore \\(p=37\\), so \\(\\boxed{31<p\\le41}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
