"solution": "The center of an equilateral triangle lies one-third of its altitude above any side. This follows because its altitudes are also medians, and the centroid divides each median in the ratio \\(2:1\\), measured from the vertex. This lets us convert the given gap between bases into a ratio of altitudes.\n\nThe supplied figure shows \\(A'B'C'\\) inside \\(ABC\\), with the same orientation and with \\(B'C'\\) between \\(BC\\) and their common center.\n\nLet \\(h>0\\) be the altitude of \\(ABC\\), and let \\(h'>0\\) be the altitude of \\(A'B'C'\\). Their common center is \\(h/3\\) above \\(BC\\). Since \\(B'C'\\) is \\(h/6\\) above \\(BC\\), the center is\n\\[\n\\frac h3-\\frac h6=\\frac h6\n\\]\nabove \\(B'C'\\). Applying the same center-to-base relation to the smaller triangle gives\n\\[\n\\frac{h'}3=\\frac h6,\n\\qquad h'=\\frac h2.\n\\]\nAll equilateral triangles are similar, and their area ratio is the square of their altitude ratio. Hence\n\\[\n\\frac{[A'B'C']}{[ABC]}=\\left(\\frac{h'}h\\right)^2=\\left(\\frac12\\right)^2=\\boxed{\\frac14},\n\\]\nwhere brackets denote area. The answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
