"solution": "Both large parentheses contain \\(a^2x^2+b^2y^2\\). Collecting this shared part helps reveal the denominator as a factor of the numerator.\n\nLet \\(S=a^2x^2+b^2y^2\\), and let \\(N\\) be the numerator. Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN&=(bx+ay)S+2a^2bxy^2+2ab^2x^2y\\\\\n&=(bx+ay)S+2abxy(ay+bx)\\\\\n&=(bx+ay)(S+2abxy).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last factor is a perfect square:\n\\[\nS+2abxy=a^2x^2+2abxy+b^2y^2=(ax+by)^2.\n\\]\nTherefore, on the original domain \\(bx+ay\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{bx+ay}\n=\\frac{(bx+ay)(ax+by)^2}{bx+ay}\n=(ax+by)^2.\n\\]\nThe simplified expression is \\(\\boxed{(ax+by)^2}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\). When \\(bx+ay=0\\), the original expression is undefined.",
