"solution": "The equal coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) suggest grouping solutions by their sum. Set \\(s=x+y\\). Since \\(x,y\\) are positive integers, \\(s\\ge2\\), and the equation determines\n\\[\nz=n-2s.\n\\]\nThe condition \\(z\\ge1\\) is therefore exactly \\(2s\\le n-1\\).\n\nThere is at least one solution because the stated count is \\(28\\), so \\(n\\ge5\\). Let \\(m\\ge2\\) be the largest integer satisfying \\(2m\\le n-1\\). The allowable sums are precisely \\(s=2,3,\\ldots,m\\).\n\nFor any fixed \\(s\\), choose \\(x\\) from \\(1,2,\\ldots,s-1\\); then \\(y=s-x\\) is positive and \\(z=n-2s\\) is fixed. This gives \\(s-1\\) solutions. Every solution has one value of \\(s\\), so these cases include every solution exactly once. Consequently the total is\n\\[\n1+2+\\cdots+(m-1)=\\frac{m(m-1)}2=28.\n\\]\nThis sum strictly increases as \\(m\\) increases. Since \\(1+2+\\cdots+7=28\\), we must have \\(m=8\\).\n\nThus \\(s=8\\) is allowed but \\(s=9\\) is not. These conditions give\n\\[\n2\\cdot8+1\\le n<2\\cdot9+1,\n\\]\nso \\(17\\le n<19\\). For both integers in this range, the allowable sums are exactly \\(2\\) through \\(8\\), giving the required \\(28\\) solutions. Therefore \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{17\\text{ or }18}\\), choice D.",
