"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The expressions \\(|x-2|\\) and \\(|x-3|\\) measure the distances from \\(x\\) to \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) on the number line. Since these fixed points are \\(1\\) unit apart, consider where \\(x\\) lies relative to them.\n\nIf \\(2\\le x\\le3\\), the two distances together make the entire segment from \\(2\\) to \\(3\\), so their sum is \\(1\\). This includes both endpoints, where one distance is zero.\n\nIf \\(x<2\\), its distance to \\(3\\) alone is greater than \\(1\\). If \\(x>3\\), its distance to \\(2\\) alone is greater than \\(1\\). Therefore no number outside the interval satisfies the equation.\n\nThe solution set is exactly \\([2,3]\\). A real interval of positive length contains infinitely many numbers, so there are infinitely many solutions. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\), more than \\(3\\).",
