"explanation": "The expressions \\(x-2\\) and \\(x-3\\) change sign at \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). These values divide all real numbers into three cases, allowing us to remove the absolute-value signs correctly.\n\nFor \\(x<2\\), both expressions are negative, so\n\\[\n|x-2|+|x-3|=(2-x)+(3-x)=5-2x>1.\n\\]\nFor \\(2\\le x\\le3\\), the first expression is nonnegative and the second is nonpositive, so\n\\[\n|x-2|+|x-3|=(x-2)+(3-x)=1.\n\\]\nFor \\(x>3\\), both expressions are positive, so\n\\[\n|x-2|+|x-3|=(x-2)+(x-3)=2x-5>1.\n\\]\nThus the equation holds exactly when \\(2\\le x\\le3\\), including both endpoints. This interval has positive length and contains infinitely many real numbers. Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\), more than \\(3\\).",
