"explanation": "First determine the two-digit prime factor. Any two-digit palindrome has the form \\(11a\\), where \\(a\\) is a digit from \\(1\\) through \\(9\\). This is prime only when \\(a=1\\), so that factor must be \\(11\\).\n\nWrite the year as \\(11p\\), where \\(p\\) is a three-digit prime palindrome. The year bounds give\n\\[\n1000\\leq 11p\\leq 2000.\n\\]\nSince \\(p\\) is a three-digit integer, it follows that \\(100\\leq p\\leq181\\). A palindrome in this range starts and ends with \\(1\\), so the complete candidate list is\n\\[\n101,111,121,131,141,151,161,171,181.\n\\]\nFive candidates are composite:\n\\[\n111=3\\cdot37,\\quad121=11^2,\\quad141=3\\cdot47,\n\\]\n\\[\n161=7\\cdot23,\\qquad171=3\\cdot57.\n\\]\nThe remaining numbers \\(101,131,151,181\\) are prime. To verify this, division by each of \\(2,3,5,7,11,13\\) leaves a nonzero remainder for all four. These tests suffice because every composite number has a prime divisor at most its square root, and all the candidates are less than \\(14^2\\).\n\nMultiplying the four primes by \\(11\\) gives\n\\[\n1111,\\qquad1441,\\qquad1661,\\qquad1991.\n\\]\nEach is a palindrome in the required range. Every possible factor was considered, and distinct factors give distinct years. Therefore there are \\(\\boxed{4}\\) years, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
