"explanation": "The base on the right is the sum of the two bases on the left. Let \\(t=2^x>0\\), so that \\(4^x=t^2\\), and put\n\\[\na=t-4,\\qquad b=t^2-2.\n\\]\nThe equation becomes \\(a^3+b^3=(a+b)^3\\). The sum of cubes identity reveals when this can happen:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&a^3+b^3-(a+b)^3\\\\\n&=(a+b)\\big(a^2-ab+b^2-(a+b)^2\\big)\\\\\n&=-3ab(a+b).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTherefore the original equation is equivalent to\n\\[\n(t-4)(t^2-2)(t^2+t-6)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. The first factor gives \\(t=4\\). The second gives \\(t=\\pm\\sqrt2\\), of which only \\(\\sqrt2\\) is positive. Finally,\n\\[\nt^2+t-6=(t+3)(t-2),\n\\]\nso the last factor gives \\(t=2\\), with \\(t=-3\\) excluded.\n\nSince \\(2^x\\) is strictly increasing, the three admissible values \\(4,\\sqrt2,2\\) correspond uniquely to\n\\[\nx=2,\\qquad x=\\frac12,\\qquad x=1.\n\\]\nEach makes a factor zero, so each satisfies the original equation. These are all the solutions, and their sum is\n\\[\n2+\\frac12+1=\\boxed{\\frac72},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
