"solution": "Use the usual model of independent tosses, each with heads probability \\(2/3\\). Only whether the current number of heads is even or odd matters: a tail preserves this status, while a head switches it.\n\nLet \\(E_n\\) and \\(O_n\\) be the probabilities of an even and an odd number of heads after \\(n\\) tosses. Initially, \\(E_0=1\\) and \\(O_0=0\\), because zero is even. For \\(0\\le n<50\\), independence gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{n+1}&=\\frac13E_n+\\frac23O_n,\\\\\nO_{n+1}&=\\frac13O_n+\\frac23E_n.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor example, an even count results from an even count followed by a tail, or an odd count followed by a head. These possibilities are disjoint.\n\nSubtracting the equations is useful because the same difference reappears:\n\\[\nE_{n+1}-O_{n+1}=-\\frac13(E_n-O_n).\n\\]\nStarting with \\(E_0-O_0=1\\), each toss multiplies the difference by \\(-1/3\\). Thus\n\\[\nE_{50}-O_{50}=\\left(-\\frac13\\right)^{50}=\\frac1{3^{50}}.\n\\]\nEvery count is either even or odd, so \\(E_{50}+O_{50}=1\\). Adding these last two equations gives\n\\[\nE_{50}=\\frac12\\left(1+\\frac1{3^{50}}\\right).\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
