"responseType": "choice",
"question": "Let \\(ABCD\\) be an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB=92\\) and \\(CD=19.\\) Suppose \\(AD=BC=x\\) and a circle with center on \\(\\overline{AB}\\) is tangent to segments \\(\\overline{AD}\\) and \\(\\overline{BC}.\\) If \\(m\\) is the smallest possible value of \\(x,\\) then \\(m^2=\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1369\\)",
"B": "\\(1679\\)",
"C": "\\(1748\\)",
"D": "\\(2109\\)",
"E": "\\(8825\\)"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"acceptedResponses": [
"B"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "We will locate the perpendicular feet from the circle's center, because each tangency point must lie on its leg segment.\n\nDrop perpendiculars from \\(D\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\), and let \\(E\\) be the foot from \\(D\\). The two end right triangles of the isosceles trapezoid have equal heights and equal hypotenuses, so they are congruent. Consequently,\n\\[\nAE=\\frac{92-19}{2}=\\frac{73}{2},\n\\]\nand the base angles at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are equal and acute.\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circle's center, and let \\(T\\) and \\(U\\) be its tangency points on \\(AD\\) and \\(BC\\). The center cannot be \\(A\\) or \\(B\\), since a tangent line cannot pass through the center of a circle of positive radius. Thus \\(O\\) lies strictly between them. Tangency gives \\(OT\\perp AD\\) and \\(OU\\perp BC\\). The right triangles \\(ATO\\) and \\(BUO\\) are similar because their angles at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are equal. Their corresponding sides \\(OT\\) and \\(OU\\) are equal radii, so their similarity ratio is 1. Hence\n\\[\nAO=BO=46.\n\\]\nNow \\(\\triangle ATO\\) and \\(\\triangle AED\\) are similar: they share the angle at \\(A\\), and each has a right angle. Their corresponding sides give\n\\[\n\\frac{AT}{46}=\\frac{AE}{AD}=\\frac{73/2}{x},\n\\qquad AT=\\frac{1679}{x}.\n\\]\nSince \\(T\\) belongs to \\(\\overline{AD}\\), we need \\(AT\\le x\\). Because \\(x>0\\), this implies\n\\[\nx^2\\ge1679.\n\\]\nWe must also show that equality is possible. Choose the trapezoid's height \\(h\\) so that\n\\[\nh^2=1679-\\left(\\frac{73}{2}\\right)^2\n=\\frac{1387}{4}>0.\n\\]\nThen its leg length satisfies \\(x^2=1679\\). Put \\(O\\) at the midpoint of \\(AB\\). The same similarity calculation for the perpendicular foot on the whole line \\(AD\\) gives distance \\(1679/x=x\\) from \\(A\\), so that foot is \\(D\\). The corresponding foot on \\(BC\\) is \\(C\\). By the trapezoid's reflection symmetry, \\(OD=OC>0\\). Therefore the circle centered at \\(O\\) with radius \\(OD\\) is tangent to the legs at \\(D\\) and \\(C\\).\n\nThese endpoints are included in the stated segments, so the bound is attained. Thus \\(\\boxed{m^2=1679}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
