"responseType": "choice",
"question": "A box contains \\(3\\) shiny pennies and \\(4\\) dull pennies. One by one, pennies are drawn at random from the box and not replaced. If the probability is \\(\\frac{a}{b}\\) that it will take more than four draws until the third shiny penny appears and \\(\\frac{a}{b}\\) is in lowest terms, then \\(a+b=\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(11\\)",
"B": "\\(20\\)",
"C": "\\(35\\)",
"D": "\\(58\\)",
"E": "\\(66\\)"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"acceptedResponses": [
"E"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The third shiny penny appears after draw 4 exactly when the first four draws do not contain all three shiny pennies. It is easier to count the complementary event.\n\nImagine drawing all seven pennies. The three positions occupied by shiny pennies form a set of three positions among seven. These sets are equally likely: if the pennies are temporarily distinguished, every complete ordering is equally likely, and each specified set of shiny positions allows the same number of orders within the shiny and dull groups.\n\nThere are\n\\[\n\\binom73=\\frac{7\\cdot6\\cdot5}{3\\cdot2\\cdot1}=35\n\\]\npossible shiny-position sets. All three shiny pennies appear within four draws in exactly\n\\[\n\\binom43=4\n\\]\nof them. Each ordering determines exactly one such set, so the count has neither omissions nor repetitions. The desired probability is therefore\n\\[\n1-\\frac4{35}=\\frac{31}{35}.\n\\]\nSince 31 and 35 have no common factor greater than 1, this fraction is in lowest terms. Thus \\(a+b=31+35=\\boxed{66}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
