"explanation": "The given vertices are opposite vertices, so their segment is a diagonal. Rectangle diagonals are equal and bisect each other, which strongly restricts the other two vertices.\n\nWrite the fixed vertices as \\(P=(4,3)\\) and \\(R=(-4,-3)\\). Their midpoint is \\(O=(0,0)\\), and \\(OP=\\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5\\). If another vertex is \\(Q=(x,y)\\), the opposite vertex must be \\(S=(-x,-y)\\). Equality of the diagonals then requires \\(OQ=5\\), so\n\\[\nx^2+y^2=25.\n\\]\nConversely, choose any integer-coordinate point \\(Q\\) on this circle other than \\(P\\) or \\(R\\), and put \\(S=(-x,-y)\\). The diagonals \\(PR\\) and \\(QS\\) bisect each other, so their endpoints form a parallelogram. The diagonals are also equal, making it a rectangle. The excluded points are exactly those that would repeat the fixed diagonal and give a degenerate figure.\n\nTo count the integer points, note that \\(|x|,|y|\\le5\\). The possible coordinate squares are \\(0,1,4,9,16,25\\), and the only pairs summing to \\(25\\) are \\(0+25\\) and \\(9+16\\). Thus all the points are\n\\[\n(\\pm5,0),\\quad(0,\\pm5),\\quad(\\pm3,\\pm4),\\quad(\\pm4,\\pm3),\n\\]\nwith independent sign choices in the last two groups. There are \\(2+2+4+4=12\\) points.\n\nRemoving \\(P\\) and \\(R\\) leaves \\(10\\) points. Each rectangle is counted twice, once from each endpoint of its other diagonal, and that diagonal uniquely determines the rectangle. Hence the number of rectangles is\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{2}=\\boxed{5},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
