"explanation": "Track the total in each row instead of its individual entries. When adjacent entries are added to form the next row, every interior entry contributes twice, while each end entry contributes once.\n\nFor \\(n\\ge2\\), the ends of row \\(n\\) are both \\(n-1\\). Thus the interior entries of row \\(n+1\\) have total \\(2f(n)-2(n-1)\\). Adding its two new end entries, both equal to \\(n\\), gives\n\\[\nf(n+1)=2f(n)-2(n-1)+2n=2f(n)+2.\n\\]\nThis relation also holds for \\(n=1\\), since the first two row sums are \\(0\\) and \\(2\\).\n\nAdding \\(2\\) to both sides reveals a doubling pattern:\n\\[\nf(n+1)+2=2\\bigl(f(n)+2\\bigr).\n\\]\nSince \\(f(1)+2=2\\), reaching row \\(n\\) requires \\(n-1\\) doublings. Therefore\n\\[\nf(n)+2=2^n,\n\\qquad f(n)=2^n-2.\n\\]\nIt remains to find the remainder of \\(2^{100}\\). Here \\(u\\equiv v\\pmod{100}\\) means that \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) have the same remainder upon division by \\(100\\). We have\n\\[\n2^{10}=1024\\equiv24\\pmod{100},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n2^{20}\\equiv24^2=576\\equiv76\\pmod{100}.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(76^2=5776\\equiv76\\pmod{100}\\). Consequently, multiplying a number with remainder \\(76\\) by another \\(76\\) again gives remainder \\(76\\). Repeating this shows\n\\[\n2^{100}=(2^{20})^5\\equiv76^5\\equiv76\\pmod{100}.\n\\]\nHence \\(f(100)=2^{100}-2\\) has remainder \\(\\boxed{74}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
