"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Coordinates let us test each small cube without having to draw the entire section. The large cube has side length \\(3\\), so place it in \\(0\\le x,y,z\\le3\\), with the chosen diagonal joining \\(A=(0,0,0)\\) to \\(B=(3,3,3)\\).\n\nThe cutting plane is the perpendicular bisector plane of \\(AB\\). Its points are equally distant from \\(A\\) and \\(B\\): the Pythagorean theorem applied through the diagonal's midpoint gives equal squared distances to the two endpoints. Thus its equation is\n\\[\nx^2+y^2+z^2\n=(x-3)^2+(y-3)^2+(z-3)^2.\n\\]\nCanceling the squared terms gives\n\\[\n6(x+y+z)=27,\n\\qquad x+y+z=\\frac92.\n\\]\nIndex a unit cube by its lower corner \\((i,j,k)\\), where each index is \\(0\\), \\(1\\), or \\(2\\), and put \\(s=i+j+k\\). Within this cube, the smallest value of \\(x+y+z\\) is \\(s\\), and the largest is \\(s+3\\). Every value between them occurs along the cube's diagonal from \\((i,j,k)\\) to \\((i+1,j+1,k+1)\\). Therefore the plane intersects the cube exactly when\n\\[\ns\\le\\frac92\\le s+3.\n\\]\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, this means \\(s=2,3,4\\).\n\nIt is shorter to count the excluded cubes. A sum below \\(2\\) occurs only for \\((0,0,0)\\) and the three placements of \\((1,0,0)\\), giving four cubes. A sum above \\(4\\) occurs only for \\((2,2,2)\\) and the three placements of \\((2,2,1)\\), giving four more.\n\nEvery unit cube has exactly one index triple, and the two excluded groups are disjoint. Hence the number intersected is\n\\[\n27-4-4=\\boxed{19}.\n\\]\nFor every accepted cube, \\(9/2\\) lies strictly between \\(s\\) and \\(s+3\\), so the plane cuts its interior. The answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
