"explanation": "Multiplying by \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) changes only that prime's exponent. Write\n\\[\nn=2^a3^b m,\n\\]\nwhere \\(a,b\\) are nonnegative integers and \\(m\\) is a positive integer divisible by neither \\(2\\) nor \\(3\\). Let \\(t\\) be the number of positive divisors of \\(m\\), including \\(t=1\\) when \\(m=1\\).\n\nFor a prime power \\(p^e\\), a divisor can use any exponent from \\(0\\) through \\(e\\), giving \\(e+1\\) choices. Unique prime factorization makes these choices independent and ensures that each divisor is counted once. Hence the given conditions become\n\\[\n(a+2)(b+1)t=28,\n\\qquad\n(a+1)(b+2)t=30.\n\\]\nThe positive integer \\(t\\) divides both \\(28\\) and \\(30\\), so \\(t=1\\) or \\(t=2\\). Subtracting the equations also gives\n\\[\n(a-b)t=2.\n\\]\nWe can now check both cases completely.\n\nIf \\(t=1\\), then \\(a=b+2\\), and the first equation becomes\n\\[\n(b+4)(b+1)=28.\n\\]\nThe positive factor pairs of \\(28\\) are \\((1,28),(2,14),(4,7)\\). Only \\((4,7)\\) has difference \\(3\\), so \\(b=3\\) and \\(a=5\\).\n\nIf \\(t=2\\), then \\(a=b+1\\), and the first equation becomes\n\\[\n(b+3)(b+1)=14.\n\\]\nIts factors would differ by \\(2\\), but neither positive factor pair \\((1,14)\\) or \\((2,7)\\) does. This case is impossible.\n\nThus \\(a=5,b=3,t=1\\). Since only \\(1\\) has exactly one positive divisor, \\(m=1\\). These values satisfy both original divisor counts, and\n\\[\n6n=2^6 3^4\n\\]\nhas \\((6+1)(4+1)=\\boxed{35}\\) positive divisors, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
