"solution": "Completing the squares reveals the centers and radii:\n\\[\n(x-6)^2+(y-3)^2=49,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2=k+40.\n\\]\nThe first circle has center \\((6,3)\\) and radius \\(7\\). The second has center \\((2,6)\\), and the distance between the centers is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(6-2)^2+(3-6)^2}=5.\n\\]\nIf \\(k<-40\\), the second graph has no real points. If \\(k=-40\\), it is only the point \\((2,6)\\), which is not on the first circle because its distance from the first center is \\(5\\), not \\(7\\).\n\nFor \\(k>-40\\), put \\(r=\\sqrt{k+40}>0\\). Two circles share a point exactly when the center distance is at most the sum of the radii and at least their absolute difference. Equality includes external or internal tangency. Here these conditions are\n\\[\n|7-r|\\le5\\le7+r.\n\\]\nThe right inequality is automatic because \\(r>0\\). The left gives \\(2\\le r\\le12\\). Since these bounds are nonnegative, squaring is equivalent and yields\n\\[\n4\\le k+40\\le144,\n\\]\nso \\(-36\\le k\\le104\\). Both endpoints give tangency and are included. Therefore\n\\[\nb-a=104-(-36)=\\boxed{140},\n\\]\nchoice D.",
