"label": "Replace the second equation with a line",
"explanation": "The two equations have the same left side, so subtracting them eliminates \\(x^2+y^2\\). Every common point must satisfy\n\\[\n8x-6y+4-k=0.\n\\]\nConversely, any point satisfying this line equation and the first original equation also satisfies the second original equation, by reversing the subtraction. Thus the original graphs share a point exactly when this line meets the first circle.\n\nCompleting squares in the first equation gives\n\\[\n(x-6)^2+(y-3)^2=49,\n\\]\nwhose center is \\((6,3)\\) and radius is \\(7\\). The perpendicular distance from this center to the line is\n\\[\n\\frac{|8(6)-6(3)+4-k|}{\\sqrt{8^2+(-6)^2}}\n=\\frac{|34-k|}{10}.\n\\]\nA line shares a point with a circle exactly when its distance from the center does not exceed the radius; equality gives tangency. Hence the necessary and sufficient condition is\n\\[\n\\frac{|34-k|}{10}\\le7,\n\\]\nor \\(|k-34|\\le70\\). This is equivalent to\n\\[\n-36\\le k\\le104.\n\\]\nThe subtraction was reversible, so every value in this interval gives a common point of the original graphs, including both endpoints, and no other value does. Therefore \\(b-a=104-(-36)=\\boxed{140}\\), choice D.",
