"solution": "Bisecting \\(\\angle CAD\\) creates angles equal to \\(\\angle DAB\\), allowing the angle bisector theorem to be used twice.\n\nThe point \\(D\\) is inside \\(\\overline{CB}\\): if \\(D=C\\), then \\(AC/AD=1\\), and if \\(D=B\\), then \\(\\angle DAB=0\\), contradicting the angle condition. Since only length ratios matter, scale the triangle so that \\(AC=2\\) and \\(AD=3\\). Triangle \\(ACD\\) is right at \\(C\\), so\n\\[\nCD=\\sqrt{3^2-2^2}=\\sqrt5.\n\\]\nLet the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\) meet \\(CD\\) at \\(E\\). The angle bisector theorem in triangle \\(CAD\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{CE}{ED}=\\frac{CA}{AD}=\\frac23.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nCE=\\frac{2\\sqrt5}{5},\\qquad\nED=\\frac{3\\sqrt5}{5},\n\\]\nand the right triangle \\(ACE\\) gives\n\\[\nAE^2=2^2+\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt5}{5}\\right)^2\n=\\frac{24}{5}.\n\\]\nNow \\(\\angle EAD=\\tfrac12\\angle CAD=\\angle DAB\\). Since \\(E,D,B\\) occur in that order on the same line, \\(AD\\) is the internal angle bisector of triangle \\(EAB\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AE}=\\frac{BD}{DE}.\n\\]\nSet \\(t=BD/CD\\), so \\(t>0\\), \\(BD=t\\sqrt5\\), and \\(BC=(1+t)\\sqrt5\\). The last ratio becomes \\(AB/AE=5t/3\\). Squaring this equality is valid because both sides are positive. Using the Pythagorean theorem in \\(ABC\\),\n\\[\n4+5(1+t)^2\n=\\frac{25t^2}{9}\\cdot\\frac{24}{5}.\n\\]\nExpanding and simplifying gives\n\\[\n25t^2-30t-27=0,\n\\qquad\n(5t-9)(5t+3)=0.\n\\]\nThe root \\(-3/5\\) is impossible because \\(t>0\\), leaving \\(t=9/5\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{CD}{BD}=\\frac1t=\\frac59.\n\\]\nSince \\(5\\) and \\(9\\) are relatively prime, \\(m+n=\\boxed{14}\\), choice B.",
