"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "We need both an upper bound valid for every orientation and a square attaining that bound. We will prove that any square with side greater than \\(\\sqrt5\\) has at least four interior lattice points.\n\nSuppose such a square has center \\((a,b)\\) and half-side length \\(h>\\sqrt5/2\\). Translate the picture by integers and reflect the coordinate axes as needed so that\n\\[\n0\\le a\\le\\frac12,\\qquad 0\\le b\\le\\frac12.\n\\]\nThese operations preserve the lattice and the number of interior lattice points. Each of the three points\n\\[\nO=(0,0),\\qquad U=(1,0),\\qquad V=(0,1)\n\\]\nis at distance at most \\(\\sqrt5/2\\) from the center. For example,\n\\[\n(1-a)^2+b^2\\le1+\\frac14=\\frac54,\n\\]\nand the other two distance bounds follow in the same way. Therefore all three points lie strictly inside the square's inscribed circle, and hence inside the square.\n\nWe now find a fourth point. Interchanging the coordinate axes if necessary, choose perpendicular unit directions along the square's sides as \\((c,s)\\) and \\((-s,c)\\), where\n\\[\n0\\le s\\le c,\\qquad c^2+s^2=1.\n\\]\nFor a point \\((x,y)\\), its coordinates measured from the center along these directions are\n\\[\nX=c(x-a)+s(y-b),\n\\qquad\nY=-s(x-a)+c(y-b).\n\\]\nThese formulas resolve the horizontal and vertical displacement into the two perpendicular directions. A point lies inside the square exactly when \\(|X|<h\\) and \\(|Y|<h\\).\n\nConsider \\(P=(-1,0)\\) and \\(R=(1,1)\\). Their distances in the \\(Y\\) direction satisfy\n\\[\n|Y(P)|=|s(1+a)-cb|\n\\le\\max\\left(\\frac{3s}{2},\\frac c2\\right)\n\\le\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt2}<\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2},\n\\]\nbecause \\(s\\le1/\\sqrt2\\). Also,\n\\[\n|Y(R)|=|c(1-b)-s(1-a)|\\le c\\le1<\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}.\n\\]\nThus both points already satisfy the required bound in one direction. In the other direction,\n\\[\n|X(P)|=c(1+a)+sb,\n\\qquad\n|X(R)|=c(1-a)+s(1-b).\n\\]\nTheir sum is \\(2c+s\\), and\n\\[\n(2c+s)^2=5-(c-2s)^2\\le5.\n\\]\nConsequently, at least one of \\(|X(P)|\\) and \\(|X(R)|\\) is at most \\(\\sqrt5/2\\). That point has both coordinate distances less than \\(h\\), so it is a fourth interior lattice point. This proves that a square with exactly three interior lattice points has area at most \\(5\\).\n\nTo attain this bound, take the closed region\n\\[\n-2\\le2x+y\\le3,\n\\qquad\n-2\\le-x+2y\\le3.\n\\]\nIts adjacent side displacements are \\((2,1)\\) and \\((-1,2)\\). They are perpendicular and both have length \\(\\sqrt5\\), so the region is a square of area \\(5\\).\n\nFor an interior lattice point, both displayed inequalities are strict. Adding the first linear expression to twice the second gives \\(5y\\), so\n\\[\n-6<5y<9.\n\\]\nThus the only possible integer rows are \\(y=-1,0,1\\). On these rows the allowed open intervals for \\(x\\) are, respectively,\n\\[\n\\left(-\\frac12,0\\right),\\qquad\n\\left(-1,\\frac32\\right),\\qquad\n(-1,1).\n\\]\nThey contain zero, two and one integers. Hence the interior lattice points are exactly \\((0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\\).\n\nThe maximum area is therefore \\(\\boxed{5}\\), closest to \\(5.0\\), choice D.",
