"solution": "The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so each side is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs \\(5\\) and \\(12\\). Thus the side length is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13.\n\\]\nThe area of the rhombus is half the product of its diagonals:\n\\[\nK=\\frac{10\\cdot24}{2}=120.\n\\]\nLet \\(I\\) be the center of the inscribed circle and let its radius be \\(r\\). Joining \\(I\\) to all four vertices partitions the rhombus into four triangles. Each has base \\(13\\) and height \\(r\\), because the radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the corresponding side. Therefore\n\\[\n120=4\\left(\\frac{13r}{2}\\right)=26r.\n\\]\nIt follows that \\(r=\\dfrac{60}{13}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
