"label": "Rewrite the logarithm as a power",
"explanation": "A logarithm base must be positive and different from \\(1\\), so an integer base here must satisfy \\(b\\ge2\\). Let \\(k=\\log_b729\\), where \\(k\\) is a positive integer. By the definition of a logarithm,\n\\[\nb^k=729=3^6.\n\\]\nIf \\(b\\) had any prime factor other than \\(3\\), that prime would also divide \\(b^k\\), which is impossible. Therefore \\(b=3^d\\) for some positive integer \\(d\\).\n\nSubstituting this form gives\n\\[\n(3^d)^k=3^{dk}=3^6,\n\\]\nso \\(dk=6\\). The positive factor pairs of \\(6\\) give exactly\n\\[\n(b,k)=(3,6),(9,3),(27,2),(729,1).\n\\]\nEach pair satisfies the original logarithm condition, and the four bases are distinct. The prime-factor argument excludes all other integer bases. Thus there are \\(\\boxed{4}\\) possibilities, choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
