"explanation": "Combining the two ratios produces \\(a^2+b^2\\), which can be related to the given difference \\(a-b\\). The square identity gives\n\\[\na^2+b^2=(a-b)^2+2ab.\n\\]\nUsing \\(a-b=ab\\), this becomes\n\\[\na^2+b^2=(ab)^2+2ab.\n\\]\nSince \\(ab\\ne0\\), we may use it as a common denominator and simplify:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{b}+\\frac{b}{a}-ab\n&=\\frac{a^2+b^2}{ab}-ab\\\\\n&=\\frac{(ab)^2+2ab}{ab}-ab\\\\\n&=ab+2-ab=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the value is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice E.",
