"id": "successive-substitution",
"label": "Express everything in terms of y",
"explanation": "The first two equations let us express \\(x\\) and \\(z\\) in terms of \\(y\\), reducing the system to one equation. They give\n\\[\nx=\\frac{4y-1}{y},\n\\qquad\nz=\\frac1{1-y}.\n\\]\nPositivity requires \\(y>1/4\\) from \\(x>0\\), and \\(y<1\\) from \\(1/z=1-y>0\\). Thus \\(1/4<y<1\\), so all the denominators used below are nonzero.\n\nSubstitute into \\(z+1/x=7/3\\):\n\\[\n\\frac1{1-y}+\\frac{y}{4y-1}=\\frac73.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(3(1-y)(4y-1)\\) gives\n\\[\n3(4y-1)+3y(1-y)=7(1-y)(4y-1).\n\\]\nAfter expansion and collection of terms,\n\\[\n25y^2-20y+4=(5y-2)^2=0.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(y=2/5\\), which lies in the required interval. The formulas above then give \\(x=3/2\\) and \\(z=5/3\\). Checking the original equations gives \\(3/2+5/2=4\\), \\(2/5+3/5=1\\), and \\(5/3+2/3=7/3\\), so no extraneous solution has been introduced. Hence\n\\[\nxyz=\\frac32\\cdot\\frac25\\cdot\\frac53=1,\n\\]\nchoice B.",
