"explanation": "The units digit of a product depends only on the units digits of its factors: every contribution involving a tens place is a multiple of \\(10\\).\n\nBoth bases end in \\(9\\). Since \\(9\\cdot9=81\\) ends in \\(1\\), while \\(1\\cdot9=9\\) ends in \\(9\\), successive positive powers of either base have units digits\n\\[\n9,1,9,1,\\ldots.\n\\]\nThus an odd power ends in \\(9\\). Both \\(19\\) and \\(99\\) are odd, so the two terms each end in \\(9\\). Their units digits add to \\(9+9=18\\), making the units digit of the sum \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice D.",
