"explanation": "The neighbor condition determines each new term from the previous two, so computing several terms may reveal all possible values that appear. Write the sequence as \\(a_1,a_2,\\ldots\\), with \\(a_1=x>0\\) and \\(a_2=2000\\). The condition says\n\\[\na_n=a_{n-1}a_{n+1}-1\\qquad(n\\ge2),\n\\]\nor\n\\[\na_{n+1}=\\frac{a_n+1}{a_{n-1}}.\n\\]\nStarting with two positive terms, this rule always produces another positive term. Thus every \\(x>0\\) gives a well-defined positive sequence, and all denominators below are nonzero.\n\nThe next two terms are\n\\[\na_3=\\frac{2001}{x},\\qquad\na_4=\\frac{x+2001}{2000x}.\n\\]\nFor the fifth term, combining the numerator first exposes a cancellation:\n\\[\na_5=\\frac{a_4+1}{a_3}\n=\\frac{2001(x+1)}{2000x}\\cdot\\frac{x}{2001}\n=\\frac{x+1}{2000}.\n\\]\nContinuing gives\n\\[\na_6=\\frac{(x+2001)/2000}{(x+2001)/(2000x)}=x,\n\\]\n\\[\na_7=\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)/2000}=2000.\n\\]\nThe pair \\((a_6,a_7)\\) equals \\((a_1,a_2)\\). Since each next term is uniquely determined by the preceding pair, all later terms repeat in blocks of five. Hence \\(2001\\) must occur among\n\\[\nx,\\quad 2000,\\quad \\frac{2001}{x},\\quad\n\\frac{x+2001}{2000x},\\quad \\frac{x+1}{2000}.\n\\]\nThe second entry cannot equal \\(2001\\). Setting each of the other entries equal to \\(2001\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na_1=2001&:\\quad x=2001,\\\\\na_3=2001&:\\quad x=1,\\\\\na_4=2001&:\\quad 2001=4001999x,\\\\\na_5=2001&:\\quad x=4001999.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the possible starting values are\n\\[\n\\frac{2001}{4001999},\\quad 1,\\quad 2001,\\quad 4001999.\n\\]\nThey are all positive and are listed in strictly increasing order, so they are four distinct admissible values. The repeating block proves there are no others. The answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
