"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "First combine the three known fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17\n=\\frac{21+14+6}{42}=\\frac{41}{42}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S=\\frac{41}{42}+\\frac1n\\). Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, \\(0<\\frac1n\\le1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n0<S\\le\\frac{83}{42}<2.\n\\]\nThe only integer strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\) is \\(1\\), so\n\\[\n\\frac1n=1-\\frac{41}{42}=\\frac1{42},\n\\qquad n=42.\n\\]\nThis value does make the sum equal to \\(1\\). Also, \\(42\\) is divisible by \\(2,3,6,\\) and \\(7\\), so statements A through D are true. Since \\(42<84\\), the statement \\(n>84\\) is false. The answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{E}}\\).",
