"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Most terms are zero, so count the indices giving each nonzero value. The numbers \\(11\\), \\(13\\), and \\(14\\) have no common factor greater than \\(1\\) in any pair. Thus their pairwise least common multiples are\n\\[\n13\\cdot14=182,\\qquad\n14\\cdot11=154,\\qquad\n11\\cdot13=143.\n\\]\nWe must also check that the cases do not overlap. An index satisfying two nonzero conditions would be divisible by all three numbers, hence by\n\\[\n11\\cdot13\\cdot14=2002.\n\\]\nNo positive index below \\(2002\\) has this property, so every nonzero term belongs to exactly one case.\n\nFor \\(a_n=11\\), the indices are \\(n=182k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le10\\). For \\(a_n=13\\), they are \\(n=154k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le12\\). For \\(a_n=14\\), they are \\(n=143k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le13\\). These ranges are exhaustive because the next multiple in each case is exactly \\(2002\\), which is excluded.\n\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{2001}a_n\n=11\\cdot10+13\\cdot12+14\\cdot13\n=110+156+182\n=\\boxed{448},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
