"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "An assortment is determined by the number of cookies of each kind; the order of selection does not matter. Because at least six of each kind are available, no possible assortment of six is excluded by the supply.\n\nLet \\(c\\) be the number of chocolate chip cookies. For a fixed \\(c\\), there are \\(6-c\\) cookies left to choose. The oatmeal count can be any integer from \\(0\\) through \\(6-c\\), giving \\(7-c\\) choices. Each choice determines the peanut butter count uniquely.\n\nAs \\(c\\) runs from \\(0\\) through \\(6\\), the numbers of assortments are therefore\n\\[\n7,6,5,4,3,2,1.\n\\]\nEvery assortment has exactly one chocolate chip count and one oatmeal count, so these cases include every assortment exactly once. The total is\n\\[\n7+6+5+4+3+2+1=28.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(28\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
