"id": "square-corner-decomposition",
"label": "Complete a surrounding square",
"explanation": "Complete the octagon to a surrounding square by extending the lines containing \\(AB,CD,EF,GH\\). Each interior angle of the regular octagon is\n\\[\n\\frac{(8-2)180^\\circ}{8}=135^\\circ.\n\\]\nThus consecutive extended sides of the square are perpendicular, and the four removed corners are congruent right triangles with two \\(45^\\circ\\) angles.\n\nLet \\(s>0\\) be the octagon's side length and \\(t>0\\) the equal leg lengths of a corner triangle. Its hypotenuse is an octagon side, so the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\ns^2=2t^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(L\\) is the surrounding square's side length, its top side consists of \\(AB\\) and one corner leg at each end. Therefore \\(L=s+2t\\).\n\nThe octagon fills the square except for the four corner triangles, whose interiors are disjoint. Its area is consequently\n\\[\n1=L^2-4\\left(\\frac12t^2\\right)\n=(s+2t)^2-2t^2.\n\\]\nExpanding and using \\(2t^2=s^2\\) gives\n\\[\n1=s^2+4st+2t^2\n=2s(s+2t)=2sL.\n\\]\nThe rectangle \\(ABEF\\) has width \\(s\\), and its height is the full distance \\(L\\) between the square's top and bottom sides. Its area is therefore\n\\[\nsL=\\boxed{\\frac12}\n\\]\nsquare unit, choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
