"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Coordinates let us express both radius conditions using the Pythagorean theorem. Their equations share squared terms, so subtracting will simplify them.\n\nPlace \\(D=(0,0)\\), \\(C=(4,0)\\), and \\(A=(0,4)\\). Since \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), \\(M=(2,0)\\). Write \\(P=(x,y)\\). The conditions \\(MP=2\\) and \\(AP=4\\) give\n\\[\n(x-2)^2+y^2=4,\n\\qquad\nx^2+(y-4)^2=16.\n\\]\nExpanding and simplifying yields\n\\[\nx^2+y^2=4x,\n\\qquad\nx^2+y^2=8y.\n\\]\nThus \\(4x=8y\\), or \\(x=2y\\). Substituting into the second equation gives\n\\[\n5y^2=8y,\n\\qquad\ny(5y-8)=0.\n\\]\nThe root \\(y=0\\) gives \\(x=0\\), which is the known intersection \\(D\\). Therefore the other intersection is\n\\[\nP=\\left(\\frac{16}{5},\\frac85\\right).\n\\]\nThe perpendicular foot from \\(P\\) to line \\(AD\\) is \\((0,8/5)\\). Because \\(0<8/5<4\\), this foot lies on the segment \\(AD\\). The required distance is therefore \\(\\boxed{16/5}\\), choice B.",
