"solution": "Assume first that \\(\\alpha\\) is uniformly distributed on \\((0,\\pi)\\), the usual contest interpretation. The useful distinction is that the turn \\(\\alpha\\) is measured from the forward direction, while the triangle's interior angle at \\(B\\) is\n\\[\n\\beta=\\pi-\\alpha.\n\\]\nSince \\(0<\\beta<\\pi\\), the triangle is nondegenerate. The law of cosines gives\n\\[\nAC^2=8^2+5^2-2(8)(5)\\cos\\beta\n=89-80\\cos\\beta.\n\\]\nBoth distances are nonnegative, so \\(AC<7\\) is equivalent to \\(AC^2<49\\). Thus\n\\[\nAC<7\n\\iff \\cos\\beta>\\frac12.\n\\]\nCosine decreases on \\((0,\\pi)\\): this follows from \\(\\cos\\beta=\\sin(\\pi/2-\\beta)\\) and the increase of sine on \\([-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]\\). Since \\(\\cos(\\pi/3)=1/2\\), the favorable condition is\n\\[\n\\beta<\\frac\\pi3,\n\\qquad\\text{or equivalently}\\qquad\n\\alpha>\\frac{2\\pi}{3}.\n\\]\nUnder uniform sampling, the probability is the favorable interval length divided by the total length:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi-2\\pi/3}{\\pi}=\\boxed{\\frac13},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\). The equality angle is excluded by the strict inequality and has probability zero under this model.\n\nUniformity matters. For example, a procedure could select each of \\((0,2\\pi/3)\\) and \\((2\\pi/3,\\pi)\\) with probability \\(1/2\\), then sample uniformly within the selected interval. This still produces an angle in \\((0,\\pi)\\), but its favorable probability is \\(1/2\\). Thus choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\) follows conditionally from the uniform-angle interpretation.",
