"solution": "The square's sides provide two useful additional tangents. Let \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\), so the full circle containing the semicircle has center \\(O\\) and radius \\(1\\). Since \\(AD\\) and \\(BC\\) are perpendicular to the radii \\(OA\\) and \\(OB\\), they are tangent at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), respectively.\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the tangency point on \\(CE\\), and write \\(x=AE\\), where \\(0\\le x\\le2\\). The point \\(E\\) cannot be \\(A\\), since the tangent at \\(A\\) is \\(AD\\), which does not pass through \\(C\\). Thus both \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) are outside the circle. Tangent segments from the same external point have equal lengths, giving\n\\[\nCT=CB=2,\\qquad ET=EA=x.\n\\]\nThe semicircle lies inside the square, so \\(T\\) lies between \\(C\\) and \\(E\\). Consequently, \\(CE=2+x\\).\n\nAlso, \\(DE=2-x\\). Applying the Pythagorean theorem to right triangle \\(CDE\\) gives\n\\[\n(2+x)^2=2^2+(2-x)^2.\n\\]\nExpanding and canceling \\(x^2\\), we obtain\n\\[\n4+4x=8-4x,\n\\]\nso \\(x=1/2\\), which is within side \\(AD\\). Therefore\n\\[\nCE=2+\\frac12=\\frac52.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac52}\\), choice D.",
