"id": "area-decomposition",
"label": "Use the radius as a height",
"explanation": "A radius to a tangency point supplies a known height, allowing us to relate \\(CE\\) to an area. Let \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\), let \\(T\\) be the tangency point on \\(CE\\), and set \\(x=AE\\) and \\(L=CE\\). Here \\(0\\le x\\le2\\), and \\(OA=OB=OT=1\\).\n\nThe radius \\(OT\\) is perpendicular to the tangent \\(CE\\), so triangle \\(OCE\\) has area \\(L/2\\). Triangles \\(OAE\\) and \\(OBC\\) have areas \\(x/2\\) and \\(1\\), respectively. These three triangles together form quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\).\n\nWe can also find that quadrilateral's area by removing right triangle \\(CDE\\) from the square. Since \\(DE=2-x\\), equating the two area calculations gives\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{L}{2}+1\n=4-\\frac12\\cdot2(2-x)\n=2+x.\n\\]\nThus \\(L=2+x\\), or \\(x=L-2\\).\n\nNow right triangle \\(CDE\\) gives\n\\[\nL^2=2^2+(2-x)^2\n=4+(4-L)^2.\n\\]\nExpanding yields \\(L^2=20-8L+L^2\\), so \\(8L=20\\) and \\(L=5/2\\). This gives \\(x=1/2\\), consistent with \\(E\\) lying on \\(AD\\). Therefore \\(CE=\\boxed{\\frac52}\\), choice D.",
