"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The angle bisector gives a useful ratio on \\(BC\\), and the circle lets us connect that ratio to \\(AD/CD\\). Let \\(E=AD\\cap BC\\). Because \\(AD\\) bisects the internal angle at \\(A\\), \\(E\\) lies between \\(B\\) and \\(C\\), with \\(A,E,D\\) in that order.\n\nThe angle-bisector theorem divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides, so\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{EC}=\\frac{AB}{AC}=\\frac78.\n\\]\nSince \\(BE+EC=9\\), we get\n\\[\nBE=\\frac7{15}\\cdot9=\\frac{21}{5}.\n\\]\nNow \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle ABC=\\angle ADC\\), because the last two angles subtend the same arc \\(AC\\). Also, \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle CAD\\) by angle bisection. Thus \\(\\triangle ABE\\sim\\triangle ADC\\), with corresponding vertices \\(A\\leftrightarrow A\\), \\(B\\leftrightarrow D\\), and \\(E\\leftrightarrow C\\). Their corresponding sides give\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AD}=\\frac{BE}{DC}.\n\\]\nAll these lengths are positive, so we may rearrange to obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{AD}{CD}=\\frac{AB}{BE}\n=\\frac{7}{21/5}=\\frac53.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\frac53\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
