"explanation": "Write the selected numbers as \\(a=2^i\\) and \\(b=2^j\\), where \\(1\\le i,j\\le25\\) and \\(i\\ne j\\). There are \\(25\\cdot24=600\\) equally likely ordered pairs, because \\(a\\) has 25 choices and \\(b\\) has 24 remaining choices.\n\nSuppose \\(\\log_a b=k\\) is an integer. Since \\(a,b>1\\), the logarithm is positive; since \\(a\\ne b\\), it is not \\(1\\). Thus \\(k\\ge2\\). By the definition of logarithms,\n\\[\n2^j=(2^i)^k=2^{ik},\n\\]\nso \\(j=ik\\). Conversely, every pair with \\(j=ki\\) for an integer \\(k\\ge2\\) gives \\(\\log_a b=k\\). We therefore need to count multiples of \\(i\\) larger than \\(i\\) and at most 25.\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\ldots,12\\), the numbers of possible \\(j\\)'s are respectively\n\\[\n24,\\ 11,\\ 7,\\ 5,\\ 4,\\ 3,\\ 2,\\ 2,\\ 1,\\ 1,\\ 1,\\ 1.\n\\]\nEach entry counts \\(2i,3i,\\ldots\\) up to 25. For example, when \\(i=3\\), the possibilities are \\(6,9,12,15,18,21,24\\), giving seven. For \\(i\\ge13\\), even the smallest possible multiple \\(2i\\) exceeds 25.\n\nLet \\(N\\) be the favorable count. Adding the disjoint cases gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN&=24+11+7+5+4+3\\\\\n&\\quad+2+2+1+1+1+1\\\\\n&=62.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEvery favorable ordered pair belongs to exactly one case, determined by \\(i\\). Hence the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{62}{600}=\\boxed{\\frac{31}{300}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
