"explanation": "Reversing digits makes the sum and difference of the numbers easy to factor. Write\n\\[\nx=10a+b,\\qquad y=10b+a.\n\\]\nBoth numbers are two-digit integers, so \\(a,b\\) are digits from \\(1\\) through \\(9\\). Also, \\(m>0\\) implies \\(x>y\\), hence \\(a>b\\). Factoring the difference of squares gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^2&=(x-y)(x+y)\\\\\n&=9(a-b)\\cdot11(a+b)\\\\\n&=99(a-b)(a+b).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBecause the primes \\(3\\) and \\(11\\) divide \\(m^2\\), they both divide \\(m\\). Thus \\(m=33t\\) for a positive integer \\(t\\). Substitution and cancellation give\n\\[\n(a-b)(a+b)=11t^2.\n\\]\nThe prime \\(11\\) must divide one of \\(a-b\\) and \\(a+b\\). But \\(1\\le a-b\\le8\\), so it cannot divide \\(a-b\\). Since \\(3\\le a+b\\le17\\), the only possibility is\n\\[\na+b=11.\n\\]\nThe previous equation now gives \\(a-b=t^2\\). As \\(1\\le t^2\\le8\\), we have \\(t=1\\) or \\(t=2\\). Moreover, \\(a-b=11-2b\\) is odd, excluding \\(t=2\\). Thus \\(a-b=1\\), which together with \\(a+b=11\\) gives \\(a=6\\) and \\(b=5\\).\n\nTherefore \\(x=65\\), \\(y=56\\), and \\(m=33\\). These values satisfy \\(65^2-56^2=33^2\\), and the restrictions above exclude every other digit pair. Hence\n\\[\nx+y+m=65+56+33=\\boxed{154},\n\\]\nchoice E.",
