"explanation": "The condition \\(|z_0|=1\\) keeps the entire sequence on the unit circle. Indeed, whenever \\(|z_n|=1\\), its conjugate is nonzero and\n\\[\n|z_{n+1}|=\\frac{|i|\\,|z_n|}{|\\overline{z_n}|}=1.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(z_n\\overline{z_n}=1\\), so the recurrence is equivalent to\n\\[\nz_{n+1}=iz_n^2.\n\\]\nThis form makes it useful to work backward from \\(z_{2005}=1\\).\n\nFor any target \\(w\\) of modulus \\(1\\), its predecessors satisfy \\(z^2=-iw\\). To see exactly how many there are, write \\(-iw=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta\\). The double-angle formulas show that\n\\[\nu=\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}+i\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\n\\]\nis one square root, and \\(-u\\) is another. They are distinct and both have modulus \\(1\\). There are no others: any other root \\(v\\) satisfies \\((v-u)(v+u)=0\\), hence \\(v=u\\) or \\(v=-u\\).\n\nThus each target has exactly two valid predecessors. Distinct targets cannot share a predecessor, because the rule \\(z\\mapsto iz^2\\) assigns each input only one successor. Consequently the number of possible values doubles at every backward step. Every valid initial value appears in this construction, and every constructed value produces a defined sequence ending at \\(1\\).\n\nAfter \\(2005\\) backward steps there are \\(\\boxed{2^{2005}}\\) possible values of \\(z_0\\), choice E.",
