"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The equality \\(AH=AC=2\\) suggests looking for squares. Since \\(AC\\) consists of two equal pieces of \\(AD\\), each piece has length \\(1\\). The corresponding trisection points on the rectangle’s top and bottom sides lie directly above one another. Consequently, \\(ACFH\\) and \\(BDEG\\) are both squares of side \\(2\\).\n\nThe figure identifies \\(X\\) as the intersection of the first square’s diagonals \\(AF\\) and \\(HC\\), and \\(Z\\) as the intersection of the second square’s diagonals \\(BE\\) and \\(DG\\). A square’s diagonals meet halfway across and halfway up the square. Thus \\(X\\) lies directly above \\(B\\), and \\(Z\\) lies directly above \\(C\\), both at height \\(1\\). Therefore \\(XZ\\) is horizontal and \\(XZ=BC=1\\).\n\nA square’s diagonals bisect its right angles. Hence both base angles of \\(\\triangle WXZ\\) are \\(45^\\circ\\), as are both base angles of \\(\\triangle YXZ\\). In either triangle, the perpendicular altitude bisects the base into lengths \\(1/2\\). The resulting right triangles have two \\(45^\\circ\\) angles, so their legs are equal. Thus each altitude also has length \\(1/2\\).\n\nSplitting the quadrilateral along \\(XZ\\) now gives\n\\[\n\\text{Area of }WXYZ\n=2\\left(\\frac12\\cdot1\\cdot\\frac12\\right)\n=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\tfrac12}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
