"explanation": "Interpret the children's ages as distinct positive whole numbers of years, as intended by the divisibility wording. Since there are eight children and the oldest is \\(9\\), exactly one age among \\(1,2,\\ldots,9\\) is missing.\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the plate number. At least one of ages \\(4\\) and \\(8\\) must be present, because only one age is missing. Therefore \\(L\\) is divisible by \\(4\\). It is also divisible by \\(9\\), the oldest child's age.\n\nSuppose a child is \\(5\\). Then \\(L\\) is divisible by \\(5\\), so it ends in \\(0\\) or \\(5\\). Divisibility by \\(4\\) makes it even, so it must end in \\(0\\). The digit condition now says its digits are two zeros and two copies of a nonzero digit \\(d\\).\n\nA number and its digit sum have the same remainder upon division by \\(9\\), because each decimal place value leaves remainder \\(1\\). Thus \\(2d\\) must be divisible by \\(9\\). Among \\(2,4,6,\\ldots,18\\), only \\(18\\) is divisible by \\(9\\), so \\(d=9\\).\n\nDivisibility by \\(4\\) depends on the last two digits, since the rest of the number is a multiple of \\(100\\). From digits \\(0\\) and \\(9\\), the possible endings are \\(00,09,90,99\\); only \\(00\\) is divisible by \\(4\\). Hence \\(L=9900\\). But\n\\[\n9900=7\\cdot1414+2=8\\cdot1237+4,\n\\]\nso neither \\(7\\) nor \\(8\\) could be a child's age. This excludes two ages, a contradiction. Therefore age \\(5\\) is absent.\n\nThe conditions are attainable: the number \\(5544\\) has two copies of each of two digits, and\n\\[\n5544/8=693,\\quad 5544/7=792,\\quad 5544/9=616.\n\\]\nIt is consequently divisible by every age in \\(1,2,3,4,6,7,8,9\\), including \\(6\\) because it is even and divisible by \\(3\\). Its last two digits give the father age \\(44\\).\n\nThe missing age is \\(\\boxed{5}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
