"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "First limit how many integers the set can contain. The six smallest consecutive positive integers already have sum\n\\[\n1+2+3+4+5+6=21>15.\n\\]\nThus only lengths \\(2,3,4,5\\) need checking.\n\nLet \\(a\\) be the smallest integer in a set. Adding the consecutive terms gives the following possibilities:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\text{ terms}:&\\quad 2a+1=15, &&a=7,\\\\\n3\\text{ terms}:&\\quad 3a+3=15, &&a=4,\\\\\n4\\text{ terms}:&\\quad 4a+6=15, &&a=\\frac94,\\\\\n5\\text{ terms}:&\\quad 5a+10=15, &&a=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe four-term case fails because its starting value is not an integer. The other cases give\n\\[\n\\{7,8\\},\\qquad \\{4,5,6\\},\\qquad \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}.\n\\]\nEach possible length has only one possible starting value, and the different lengths give different sets. Therefore the complete count is \\(\\boxed{3}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
