"solution": "Assume the two dice are rolled independently, as in the usual dice model. Let \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) denote their face values. The ratio terms sum to\n\\[\n1+2+3+4+5+6=21,\n\\]\nso each die has probability \\(k/21\\) of showing \\(k\\), for \\(k=1,2,\\ldots,6\\).\n\nThe ordered pairs totaling \\(7\\) are exactly\n\\[\n(1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1).\n\\]\nIndependence lets us multiply the two face probabilities for each pair. These six outcomes are mutually exclusive, so their probabilities add:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X+Y=7)\n&=\\frac{1\\cdot6+2\\cdot5+3\\cdot4+4\\cdot3+5\\cdot2+6\\cdot1}{21^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{56}{441}=\\frac{8}{63}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus independent rolls give \\(\\boxed{8/63}\\), choice (C).\n\nThe independence assumption matters. For example, choose one value \\(k\\) with probability \\(k/21\\) and have both dice always show that value. Each die still has the required individual face probabilities, but their total is always even and never \\(7\\). That model gives probability \\(0\\). Therefore the face ratios alone do not determine a unique answer.",
