"solution": "Treat the expression as a quadratic in \\(\\sin x\\). Completing the square will let us express its bounds using angle-addition formulas.\n\nWrite\n\\[\nR=\\sin^2x-\\sin x\\sin y+\\sin^2y.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n4R=(2\\sin x-\\sin y)^2+3\\sin^2y.\n\\]\nBecause \\(0\\le y\\le\\pi/2\\), we have \\(\\cos y\\ge0\\). Thus \\(R\\le3/4\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n-\\sqrt3\\cos y\\le2\\sin x-\\sin y\n\\le\\sqrt3\\cos y.\n\\]\nThe addition and subtraction formulas give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin y\\pm\\sqrt3\\cos y}{2}\n=\\sin\\left(y\\pm\\frac\\pi3\\right).\n\\]\nSo the original inequality is exactly\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(y-\\frac\\pi3\\right)\n\\le\\sin x\\le\n\\sin\\left(y+\\frac\\pi3\\right).\n\\]\nFor the lower bound, both \\(x\\) and \\(y-\\pi/3\\) lie in \\([-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]\\), where sine is strictly increasing. It therefore becomes \\(x\\ge y-\\pi/3\\).\n\nFor the upper bound, we must account for sine increasing and then decreasing:\n\nIf \\(0\\le y\\le\\pi/6\\), then \\(y+\\pi/3\\le\\pi/2\\), so the upper bound becomes \\(x\\le y+\\pi/3\\). In this case \\(x\\le2\\pi/3-y\\) is automatic because \\(2\\pi/3-y\\ge\\pi/2\\).\n\nIf \\(\\pi/6\\le y\\le\\pi/2\\), use\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(y+\\frac\\pi3\\right)\n=\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}3-y\\right).\n\\]\nThe reflected angle lies in \\([\\pi/6,\\pi/2]\\), so the upper bound becomes \\(x\\le2\\pi/3-y\\). Here \\(x\\le y+\\pi/3\\) is automatic.\n\nCombining these complete cases, the desired set is precisely the part of \\(S\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|x-y|\\le\\frac\\pi3,\n\\qquad x+y\\le\\frac{2\\pi}3.\n\\]\nThese constraints remove three corner triangles from the square of side \\(\\pi/2\\). The regions \\(x-y>\\pi/3\\) and \\(y-x>\\pi/3\\) each form a right triangle with legs \\(\\pi/6\\). The region \\(x+y>2\\pi/3\\) forms a right triangle with legs \\(\\pi/3\\).\n\nTheir interiors do not overlap. The first two inequalities cannot hold together; moreover, \\(x-y>\\pi/3\\) implies \\(x+y=2x-(x-y)<2\\pi/3\\), since \\(2x\\le\\pi\\). The reflected argument applies to the other small triangle. Hence the desired area is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\left(\\frac\\pi2\\right)^2\n-2\\cdot\\frac12\\left(\\frac\\pi6\\right)^2\\\\\n&\\quad-\\frac12\\left(\\frac\\pi3\\right)^2\n=\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll equality boundaries are included, and they have zero area. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}}\\), choice C.",
