"explanation": "Each valid selection occupies exactly three different rows. Choose those rows first; there are\n\\[\n\\binom53=\\frac{5\\cdot4\\cdot3}{3\\cdot2\\cdot1}=10\n\\]\nchoices.\n\nOnce the rows are chosen, process them from top to bottom. The block in the first chosen row can be in any of \\(5\\) columns. The next has \\(4\\) available columns, and the last has \\(3\\). Thus the number of selections is\n\\[\n10\\cdot5\\cdot4\\cdot3=600.\n\\]\nEvery valid selection determines exactly one set of occupied rows and one column choice in each row, so it is counted once. The answer is \\(600\\), choice \\(C\\).",
