"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Count pairs of faces, since the repeated numbers on the first die do not represent equally likely numerical values. The two dice have \\(6\\cdot6=36\\) equally likely pairs of faces.\n\nGroup the favorable pairs by the first die's number:\n\n- If it shows \\(1\\), the second die can show \\(4,6,8\\), giving \\(3\\) pairs.\n- If it shows \\(2\\), the second die can show \\(3,5\\). There are two faces marked \\(2\\), giving \\(2\\cdot2=4\\) pairs.\n- If it shows \\(3\\), the second die can show \\(4,6\\). There are two faces marked \\(3\\), giving another \\(4\\) pairs.\n- If it shows \\(4\\), the second die can show \\(1,3,5\\), giving \\(3\\) pairs.\n\nThese cases cover every face of the first die, and each face pair belongs to exactly one case. Therefore the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{3+4+4+3}{36}=\\frac{14}{36}=\\boxed{\\frac{7}{18}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
