"solution": "The equal areas can reveal side ratios if we compare both triangles with \\(\\triangle AEB\\). Write \\([XYZ]\\) for the area of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Convexity places \\(E\\) strictly inside both diagonals, so all the segment lengths and triangle areas used below are positive.\n\nTriangles \\(AED\\) and \\(AEB\\) have bases on \\(BD\\) and share the same height from \\(A\\). Similarly, triangles \\(BEC\\) and \\(AEB\\) have bases on \\(AC\\) and share the same height from \\(B\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\frac{[AED]}{[AEB]}=\\frac{ED}{EB},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{[BEC]}{[AEB]}=\\frac{EC}{EA}.\n\\]\nSince \\([AED]=[BEC]\\), these ratios are equal. Rearranging gives\n\\[\n\\frac{EA}{EC}=\\frac{EB}{ED}.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(\\angle AEB=\\angle CED\\) because they are vertical angles. Thus \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle CED\\) are similar by the side-angle-side similarity criterion. Their corresponding sides give\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{EC}=\\frac{AB}{CD}=\\frac9{12}=\\frac34.\n\\]\nThe two parts of \\(AC\\) therefore have ratio \\(3:4\\). Since \\(AC=14\\),\n\\[\nAE=\\frac{3}{3+4}\\cdot14=\\boxed6,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
