"explanation": "The exponents \\(12,8,4\\) suggest viewing the expression as \\(p(x^4)\\). This lets us use the known zeros without finding \\(a,b,c\\).\n\nLet \\(\\alpha=2009+9002\\pi i\\). The three prescribed zeros of the monic cubic \\(p\\) are distinct, so the factor theorem gives\n\\[\np(t)=(t-2009)(t-9002)(t-\\alpha).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\np(x^4)=(x^4-2009)(x^4-9002)(x^4-\\alpha).\n\\]\nEvery zero must therefore satisfy exactly one of the three fourth-power equations.\n\nFor a positive real number \\(t\\), put \\(s=\\sqrt[4]{t}>0\\). The factorization\n\\[\nx^4-t=(x-s)(x+s)(x-is)(x+is)\n\\]\nshows that there are four distinct zeros, exactly two of which are nonreal. Thus \\(t=2009\\) and \\(t=9002\\) contribute two nonreal zeros each.\n\nFor \\(x^4=\\alpha\\), the fundamental theorem of algebra guarantees a complex zero \\(z\\) of \\(x^4-\\alpha\\). Since \\(\\alpha\\ne0\\), we have \\(z\\ne0\\). The numbers\n\\[\nz,\\quad -z,\\quad iz,\\quad -iz\n\\]\nare four distinct fourth roots of \\(\\alpha\\). A polynomial of degree four has at most four distinct zeros, so this list is complete. None is real, because a real number's fourth power is real, whereas \\(\\alpha\\) is nonreal.\n\nThe three groups are disjoint because a number has only one fourth power. Hence the number of nonreal zeros is\n\\[\n2+2+4=\\boxed{8},\n\\]\nchoice C.",
