"solution": "Brian's statement determines Mike's species regardless of Brian's own species. If Brian is a toad, his statement is true, so Mike is a frog. If Brian is a frog, his statement is false, so he and Mike are the same species, again making Mike a frog.\n\nNow consider Chris. If Chris is a toad, his true statement makes LeRoy a frog. If Chris is a frog, his false statement makes LeRoy a toad. Thus exactly one of Chris and LeRoy is a toad; in either case, LeRoy's statement also has the required truth value.\n\nMike is a frog, so his claim that at least two are toads is false. There is therefore at most one toad. Since Chris and LeRoy already include one toad, Brian must be a frog.\n\nThese deductions are consistent: with Chris a toad and the other three frogs, Chris's claim is true and each frog's claim is false. Hence there are \\(3\\) frogs, choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
