"explanation": "Extend \\(FA\\) and \\(BC\\) to meet at \\(V\\) on the exterior side of \\(AB\\). A regular hexagon has interior angles \\(120^\\circ\\), so \\(\\angle VAB=\\angle VBA=60^\\circ\\). Thus \\(VAB\\) is equilateral.\n\nLet \\(s>0\\) be the hexagon's side length and \\(h>0\\) the altitude of \\(VAB\\) to \\(AB\\). The six triangles formed by joining the hexagon's center to its vertices are also equilateral with side \\(s\\). Hence the distance from the hexagon's center to each side is \\(h\\). Opposite sides \\(AB\\) and \\(DE\\) are parallel and separated by \\(2h\\), so their distances from \\(V\\) are \\(h\\) and \\(3h\\), respectively.\n\nThe contact point of either circle with its specified segment lies between the two slanted lines. Each slanted tangent line keeps the whole circular disk on the same side as that contact point. Thus both disks lie in the \\(60^\\circ\\) angle at \\(V\\) containing the hexagon. The first circle cannot touch \\(AB\\) at \\(A\\) or \\(B\\), since that would give two distinct tangent lines at one circle point.\n\nFor either circle, let \\(I\\) be its center and \\(r\\) its radius. Its equal perpendicular distances to the two slanted lines put \\(I\\) on the internal angle bisector. If \\(T\\) is a tangency point with one of those lines, then \\(IT\\perp VT\\) and \\(\\angle IVT=30^\\circ\\). The side opposite \\(30^\\circ\\) in a right triangle is half its hypotenuse, giving\n\\[\nVI=2IT=2r.\n\\]\nLet the first and second radii be \\(r_1\\) and \\(r_2\\). Because the circles lie outside the hexagon, the first center is between \\(V\\) and \\(AB\\), while the second center is beyond \\(DE\\) from \\(V\\). Therefore\n\\[\nh=2r_1+r_1=3r_1,\n\\qquad\n2r_2=3h+r_2.\n\\]\nSo \\(r_1=h/3\\) and \\(r_2=3h\\). Their areas have ratio\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi r_2^2}{\\pi r_1^2}\n=\\left(\\frac{3h}{h/3}\\right)^2\n=\\boxed{81},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
