"responseType": "choice",
"question": "Each of \\(2010\\) boxes in a line contains a single red marble, and for \\(1 \\le k \\le 2010,\\) the box in the \\(k\\)th position also contains \\(k\\) white marbles. Isabella begins at the first box and successively draws a single marble at random from each box, in order. She stops when she first draws a red marble. Let \\(P(n)\\) be the probability that Isabella stops after drawing exactly \\(n\\) marbles. What is the smallest value of \\(n\\) for which \\(P(n) \\lt \\dfrac{1}{2010}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(45\\)",
"B": "\\(63\\)",
"C": "\\(64\\)",
"D": "\\(201\\)",
"E": "\\(1005\\)"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"acceptedResponses": [
"A"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "To stop on draw \\(n\\), Isabella must draw white from boxes \\(1\\) through \\(n-1\\), then red from box \\(n\\). Reaching a box does not change its contents, so the relevant probabilities at box \\(k\\) are \\(k/(k+1)\\) for white and \\(1/(k+1)\\) for red.\n\nFor \\(2\\le n\\le2010\\), multiplying these successive probabilities gives\n\\[\nP(n)=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac23\\cdots\n\\frac{n-1}{n}\\cdot\\frac1{n+1}\n=\\frac1{n(n+1)}.\n\\]\nThe consecutive factors cancel, leaving the first numerator and the final denominators. For \\(n=1\\), the probability is \\(1/2\\), which agrees with the same formula.\n\nAll denominators are positive, so\n\\[\nP(n)<\\frac1{2010}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nn(n+1)>2010.\n\\]\nThe product \\(n(n+1)\\) increases as the positive integer \\(n\\) increases. The decisive adjacent products are\n\\[\n44\\cdot45=1980<2010,\n\\qquad\n45\\cdot46=2070>2010.\n\\]\nThus every \\(n\\le44\\) fails, while \\(n=45\\) works and is within the available boxes. The smallest value is \\(\\boxed{45}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
