"explanation": "Perfect squares have even prime exponents, and perfect cubes have prime exponents that are multiples of \\(3\\). This makes prime factorization useful for handling both conditions at once.\n\nFor any prime appearing in \\(n\\), let \\(e\\) be its exponent. Its exponent in \\(n^2\\) is \\(2e\\), which must be a multiple of \\(3\\). Since \\(2\\) has no factor of \\(3\\), \\(e\\) must itself be a multiple of \\(3\\). Its exponent in \\(n^3\\) is \\(3e\\), which must be even. Since \\(3\\) is odd, \\(e\\) must also be even. Thus every prime exponent in \\(n\\) is a multiple of \\(6\\).\n\nNow \\(20=2^2\\cdot5\\), so \\(n\\) must contain both primes \\(2\\) and \\(5\\). Their smallest permitted exponents are both \\(6\\). Every admissible \\(n\\) therefore contains \\(2^6\\cdot5^6\\) as a factor, and choosing exactly these factors gives\n\\[\nn=2^6\\cdot5^6=10^6=1{,}000{,}000.\n\\]\nThis number is divisible by \\(20\\); its square is \\((10^4)^3\\), and its cube is \\((10^9)^2\\), so it satisfies every condition. It has \\(7\\) digits, choice E.",
