"explanation": "Both sine and cosine are at most \\(1\\). Their sum can equal \\(2\\) only if both terms equal \\(1\\), so\n\\[\n\\cos(2A-B)=1,\n\\qquad\n\\sin(A+B)=1.\n\\]\nThe triangle's interior angles are positive and sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), so \\(0^\\circ<A+B<180^\\circ\\). In this interval, sine equals \\(1\\) only at \\(90^\\circ\\). Hence\n\\[\nA+B=90^\\circ,\n\\qquad C=90^\\circ.\n\\]\nNow \\(B=90^\\circ-A\\) and \\(0^\\circ<A<90^\\circ\\), giving\n\\[\n-90^\\circ<2A-B=3A-90^\\circ<180^\\circ.\n\\]\nCosine equals \\(1\\) at integer multiples of \\(360^\\circ\\). The only such angle in this interval is \\(0^\\circ\\), so \\(2A-B=0^\\circ\\), or \\(B=2A\\). Combining this with \\(A+B=90^\\circ\\) gives \\(A=30^\\circ\\) and \\(B=60^\\circ\\).\n\nSide \\(AB\\) is opposite the right angle, so it is the hypotenuse. Side \\(BC\\) is opposite the \\(30^\\circ\\) angle and therefore equals half the hypotenuse. Thus\n\\[\nBC=\\frac{AB}{2}=\\frac42=2,\n\\]\nchoice C.",
