"id": "reduced-ratio-integer-scores",
"label": "Use integer scores to restrict the geometric ratio",
"explanation": "The scores are integers, but the geometric ratio need not be an integer. Writing that ratio in lowest terms lets the four integer scores and the 100-point bound restrict it.\n\nThe common first-quarter score is positive: a geometric sequence starting at zero could not be increasing. Write the Raiders' ratio as \\(p/q>1\\), where \\(p,q\\) are relatively prime positive integers. If their first score is \\(t\\), then their fourth score is \\(tp^3/q^3\\). Because \\(p^3\\) and \\(q^3\\) are relatively prime, integrality forces \\(q^3\\) to divide \\(t\\). Thus, for some positive integer \\(k\\), the Raiders' scores are\n\\[\nkq^3,\\quad kpq^2,\\quad kp^2q,\\quad kp^3.\n\\]\nTheir total is\n\\[\nk(p+q)(p^2+q^2)\\le100.\n\\]\nIf \\(q\\ge3\\), then \\(p\\ge4\\), and even \\(k=1\\) gives a total at least \\(7\\cdot25=175\\). Hence \\(q\\) is \\(1\\) or \\(2\\). For \\(q=1\\), the bound permits only \\(p=2,3,4\\), since \\(p=5\\) already gives \\(156\\). For \\(q=2\\), only \\(p=3\\) fits, since \\(p=4\\) already gives \\(120\\). These bounds exhaust the possible reduced ratios.\n\nLet \\(d\\) be the Wildcats' positive integer common difference. Their four scores are\n\\[\nkq^3,\\quad kq^3+d,\\quad kq^3+2d,\\quad kq^3+3d,\n\\]\nwith total \\(4kq^3+6d\\). The Raiders' one-point victory therefore gives\n\\[\nk\\bigl((p+q)(p^2+q^2)-4q^3\\bigr)-6d=1.\n\\]\nFor \\((p,q)=(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(3,2)\\), the coefficient of \\(k\\) is respectively \\(11,36,81,33\\). The last three are divisible by \\(3\\), making the left side divisible by \\(3\\), so none can equal \\(1\\). Only \\(p/q=2\\) remains.\n\nNow the total bound gives \\(15k\\le100\\), so \\(1\\le k\\le6\\), and the margin equation becomes\n\\[\n11k-6d=1.\n\\]\nReducing modulo \\(6\\) gives \\(k\\equiv5\\pmod6\\), hence \\(k=5\\) and \\(d=9\\). The quarter scores are consequently \\((5,10,20,40)\\) and \\((5,14,23,32)\\). Their totals, \\(75\\) and \\(74\\), satisfy all the original conditions.\n\nThe combined first-half score is\n\\[\n5+10+5+14=\\boxed{34},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
