"explanation": "The largest difference fixes the span from the smallest number to the largest. To determine the interior arrangement, let the positive consecutive gaps be\n\\[\na=y-z,\\qquad b=x-y,\\qquad c=w-x.\n\\]\nThe six pairwise differences are\n\\[\na,\\ b,\\ c,\\ a+b,\\ b+c,\\ a+b+c.\n\\]\nThe largest is \\(a+b+c=9\\). Adding all six differences is useful because each outer gap occurs three times, while the middle gap occurs four times. Hence\n\\[\n1+3+4+5+6+9\n=3a+4b+3c\n=3(9)+b.\n\\]\nThe left side is \\(28\\), so \\(b=1\\), and then \\(a+c=8\\).\n\nAfter removing the differences \\(1\\) and \\(9\\), the remaining differences are \\(a,a+1,c,c+1\\), namely \\(3,4,5,6\\). Their smallest value must be one of \\(a,c\\), so one of those gaps is \\(3\\). Since they sum to \\(8\\), the other is \\(5\\). This leaves exactly two cases.\n\nIf \\((a,b,c)=(3,1,5)\\), the four numbers in increasing order are \\(z,z+3,z+4,z+9\\). Their sum gives\n\\[\n4z+16=44,\n\\]\nso \\(z=7\\) and the numbers are \\(7,10,11,16\\). Thus \\(w=16\\).\n\nIf \\((a,b,c)=(5,1,3)\\), the numbers are \\(z,z+5,z+6,z+9\\). Now\n\\[\n4z+20=44,\n\\]\nso \\(z=6\\) and the numbers are \\(6,11,12,15\\). Thus \\(w=15\\).\n\nBoth lists consist of integers and produce exactly the required six differences. The forced gap analysis excludes every other pattern, and the two cases give different largest values. Their sum is\n\\[\n16+15=\\boxed{31},\n\\]\nchoice B.",
