"label": "Compose the rational functions and bound the modulus",
"explanation": "First handle the possibility that \\(f\\) is constant. If \\(a=b\\), then \\(f(z)=1\\) wherever it is defined. When \\(b\\ne-1\\), all later iterates also equal \\(1\\), so they cannot equal every allowed input \\(z\\). When \\(a=b=-1\\), however, \\(f(z)=1\\) for \\(z\\ne1\\), while \\(f(1)\\) is undefined. Thus \\(g\\) has empty domain, and the stated identity holds because there are no inputs to check. This allowed case has \\(|b|=1\\).\n\nNow assume \\(a\\ne b\\). Cross-multiplication shows that \\(f(u)=f(v)\\) implies \\((b-a)(u-v)=0\\), so \\(f\\) is one-to-one on its domain. At each of four successive evaluations, at most one additional starting input can reach the forbidden value \\(-b\\). Therefore \\(g(g(z))\\) is defined for infinitely many \\(z\\), which allows its identity to impose coefficient conditions.\n\nComposing \\(f\\) with itself gives\n\\[\ng(z)=\\frac{(1+a)z+a(1+b)}{(1+b)z+a+b^2}.\n\\]\nFor shorter calculations, put\n\\[\np=1+a,\\quad q=a(1+b),\\quad\nr=1+b,\\quad s=a+b^2.\n\\]\nThen, at inputs where the original compositions are defined,\n\\[\ng(g(z))=\n\\frac{(p^2+qr)z+q(p+s)}{r(p+s)z+rq+s^2}.\n\\]\nSetting this equal to \\(z\\) and clearing the nonzero denominator yields\n\\[\nr(p+s)z^2+(s^2-p^2)z-q(p+s)=0.\n\\]\nA nonzero polynomial of degree at most two cannot vanish at infinitely many inputs. In particular, its quadratic coefficient must be zero, giving\n\\[\n(1+b)(1+2a+b^2)=0.\n\\]\nThus either \\(b=-1\\), which gives \\(|b|=1\\), or\n\\[\nb^2+1=-2a.\n\\]\nIn the second case, \\(|a|=1\\) implies \\(|b^2+1|=2\\). Using the triangle inequality and \\(|b^2|=|b|^2\\), we obtain\n\\[\n2\\le |b|^2+1,\n\\qquad\n|b|^2=|(b^2+1)-1|\\le2+1.\n\\]\nHence every allowed case satisfies\n\\[\n1\\le |b|\\le\\sqrt3.\n\\]\n\nBoth endpoints actually occur. Take \\(a=1,b=-1\\). Then \\(g(z)=z\\) wherever the original composition is defined, so \\(|b|=1\\) is possible. For the other endpoint, take \\(a=1,b=i\\sqrt3\\). Here\n\\[\np=2,\\quad s=-2,\\quad q=r=1+i\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nThus \\(p+s=0\\) and \\(p^2+qr=2+2i\\sqrt3\\ne0\\). The displayed composition formula therefore reduces to \\(g(g(z))=z\\) on its original domain, establishing \\(|b|=\\sqrt3\\).\n\nThe difference between the largest and smallest values is consequently \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt3-1}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
