"solution": "An area bound can control the radius: a disk inside a convex polygon keeps its center at least its radius away from every supporting side line. We will first obtain this bound, then construct a quadrilateral that attains it.\n\nSuppose a convex quadrilateral contains a disk of radius \\(r>0\\), and let its area be \\(K\\). Joining the disk's center to the four vertices divides the quadrilateral into triangles whose heights are at least \\(r\\). Therefore\n\\[\nK\\ge \\frac r2(14+9+7+12)=21r.\n\\]\nTo bound \\(K\\) from above, let \\(\\alpha=\\angle DAB\\) and \\(\\gamma=\\angle BCD\\). Both angles lie strictly between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(180^\\circ\\). Applying the cosine law to the two triangles sharing diagonal \\(BD\\) gives\n\\[\n340-336\\cos\\alpha=130-126\\cos\\gamma,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n168\\cos\\alpha-63\\cos\\gamma=105.\n\\]\nThe same two triangles give the area equation\n\\[\n2K=168\\sin\\alpha+63\\sin\\gamma.\n\\]\nThese equations have matching sine and cosine terms, so squaring and adding is useful. By Cauchy-Schwarz and \\(\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1\\),\n\\[\n\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\gamma-\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\gamma\\le 1.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2K)^2+105^2\n&=168^2+63^2\\\\\n&\\quad+2(168)(63)(\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\gamma-\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\gamma)\\\\\n&\\le (168+63)^2=231^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus \\(K\\le 42\\sqrt6\\), and the earlier lower bound gives\n\\[\nr\\le \\frac{42\\sqrt6}{21}=2\\sqrt6.\n\\]\nConcave quadrilaterals cannot improve this bound. Their convex hull is a triangle with perimeter at most \\(42\\): its new side is a diagonal no longer than the two sides it replaces. Let this triangle have side lengths \\(u,v,w\\), semiperimeter \\(t\\le21\\), and area \\(T\\). Since it contains the disk, the same triangle-area decomposition gives \\(T\\ge tr\\). Put \\(x=t-u\\), \\(y=t-v\\), and \\(z=t-w\\). These numbers are positive and sum to \\(t\\). Heron's formula gives\n\\[\nr^2\\le \\frac{T^2}{t^2}=\\frac{xyz}{t}\\le\\frac{t^2}{27}\\le\\frac{49}{3}<24.\n\\]\nHere the product bound \\(xyz\\le(t/3)^3\\) follows using only two-term AM-GM. Indeed, with \\(m=t/3>0\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nxyzm&\\le\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)^2\\left(\\frac{z+m}{2}\\right)^2\\\\\n&\\le\\left(\\frac{x+y+z+m}{4}\\right)^4=m^4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nDividing by \\(m\\) proves the bound. A triangular hull arising from collinear vertices satisfies the same argument; a zero-area figure contains no positive-radius disk.\n\nIt remains to attain \\(2\\sqrt6\\). Set \\(\\rho=2\\sqrt6\\), and choose acute angles \\(p,q\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\tan p=\\frac{\\rho}{8},\\qquad \\tan q=\\frac{\\rho}{6}.\n\\]\nCircumscribe a quadrilateral about a radius-\\(\\rho\\) circle with successive interior half-angles\n\\[\np,\\quad q,\\quad 90^\\circ-p,\\quad 90^\\circ-q.\n\\]\nSuch a quadrilateral exists: the corresponding exterior turns are all between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(180^\\circ\\), and they total \\(360^\\circ\\), so successive tangents enclose a convex quadrilateral.\n\nThe two tangent segments from each vertex have equal lengths. A radius to a contact point is perpendicular to the tangent, so the tangent length at a vertex with half-angle \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\rho/\\tan\\theta\\). The four vertex tangent lengths are therefore\n\\[\n8,\\quad 6,\\quad \\rho\\tan p=3,\\quad \\rho\\tan q=4.\n\\]\nThe consecutive side lengths are \\(8+6=14\\), \\(6+3=9\\), \\(3+4=7\\), and \\(4+8=12\\), exactly as required. This quadrilateral contains the radius-\\(\\rho\\) disk, so the largest possible radius is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt6}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
