"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "A position ends white exactly when its original square was white and the square rotating into that position was also white. This lets us count successful original colorings.\n\nThe center rotates to itself, so it must originally be black. The four corners rotate among themselves in a loop, and the four edge-middle squares form a separate loop.\n\nConsider either four-position loop, listing its positions in clockwise rotation order. A white square moves into the next position. Thus two consecutive positions in this order cannot both originally be white, since one would remain white after the rotation and repainting.\n\nCount the allowed colorings by their number of white positions. There is \\(1\\) coloring with no whites and \\(4\\) with one white. With two whites, they must occupy opposite positions, giving \\(2\\) colorings. Three or four whites necessarily include consecutive white positions, so there are no further possibilities. Each loop therefore has\n\\[\n1+4+2=7\n\\]\nallowed colorings.\n\nThe center, corners, and edge-middle positions are disjoint and together specify the entire original grid. Every choice of an allowed coloring for each loop works, so there are \\(1\\cdot7\\cdot7=49\\) successful grids, with none counted twice.\n\nThe \\(9\\) independent fair color choices produce \\(2^9=512\\) equally likely original grids. Hence the probability is\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{49}{512}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
