"explanation": "Represent the six named people by points and each mutual friendship by a line joining two points. Let \\(d\\) be their common number of friends. There is at least one friendship and at least one missing friendship, so\n\\[\nd\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n\\]\nReplace every friendship by a nonfriendship and every nonfriendship by a friendship. Each person then has \\(5-d\\) friends. Doing this twice restores the original configuration, so it gives a one-to-one correspondence between the \\(d=1\\) and \\(d=4\\) cases, and between the \\(d=2\\) and \\(d=3\\) cases. We need only count \\(d=1\\) and \\(d=2\\), then double.\n\nIf \\(d=1\\), everyone belongs to a pair. Choose Adam's partner in \\(5\\) ways. Among the four remaining people, take the first unpaired name in the given list and choose that person's partner in \\(3\\) ways. The last pair is forced. This fixed procedure counts each pairing once, giving\n\\[\n5\\cdot3=15.\n\\]\nIf \\(d=2\\), the friendship lines form separate closed loops. To see why, follow a line from someone and at each new person continue along the other line. A finite path must eventually close. It cannot first return to an internal person on the path, since that would give that person three incident lines. Thus it returns to its starting person. Everyone on this loop already has two friends, so nobody outside can connect to it. Repeating the argument accounts for everyone.\n\nEach loop has at least three people. With six people, the only possibilities are one loop of six or two loops of three.\n\nFor a six-person loop, place Adam first in a traversal and list the other five people in order. There are \\(5!=120\\) lists. Each friendship configuration gives exactly two lists, corresponding to the two traversal directions, so there are \\(120/2=60\\) configurations.\n\nFor two three-person loops, choose Adam's two companions in \\(\\binom52=10\\) ways. The other three people form the second loop. Adam's group uniquely determines the split, so each configuration is counted once.\n\nThese cases are exhaustive and disjoint. The total is therefore\n\\[\n2(15+60+10)=\\boxed{170},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
