"solution": "First find the three probabilities for one round. Alex wins with probability \\(\\frac12\\), leaving probability \\(\\frac12\\) for the other two players. Dividing this remainder in Mel's and Chelsea's ratio \\(2:1\\) gives\n\\[\np_M=\\frac13,\\qquad p_C=\\frac16.\n\\]\nFor any fixed order containing three Alex wins, two Mel wins, and one Chelsea win, independence gives probability\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac12\\right)^3\n\\left(\\frac13\\right)^2\n\\left(\\frac16\\right)=\\frac1{432}.\n\\]\nTo count these orders, choose Alex's three winning rounds in \\(\\binom63=20\\) ways. Then choose Mel's two winning rounds from the three remaining rounds in \\(\\binom32=3\\) ways. Chelsea wins the last round. Every desired order determines exactly one such pair of choices, so there are \\(20\\cdot3=60\\) orders, with none omitted or counted twice.\n\nThese orders are mutually exclusive, so their probabilities add:\n\\[\nP=60\\cdot\\frac1{432}=\\frac5{36}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac5{36}}\\), choice B.",
