"explanation": "Let \\(c_k\\) denote the decimal base used to define \\(a_k\\), with \\(c_1=0.201\\). Comparing their digits shows that the odd-indexed bases increase, the even-indexed bases decrease, and every odd-indexed base is below every even-indexed base. For example,\n\\[\nc_1<c_3<c_5<c_6<c_4<c_2.\n\\]\nThese comparisons suggest that the terms themselves may form two interlacing lists, but the exponents must also be checked.\n\nFor \\(0<c<1\\), the value \\(c^t\\) decreases as \\(t\\) increases. For a fixed positive exponent, it increases as the positive base increases. We will use both facts together.\n\nIntroduce \\(a_0=1\\), so that \\(a_1=c_1^{a_0}\\) and the recurrence \\(a_k=c_k^{a_{k-1}}\\) works from \\(k=1\\). All terms \\(a_k\\) for \\(k\\ge1\\) lie strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). Also,\n\\[\na_1<c_2<a_2<a_0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(a_1<1\\) and raising \\(c_2\\in(0,1)\\) to an exponent below \\(1\\) increases its value.\n\nWe now prove that each new term lies strictly between the preceding two terms. Suppose \\(k\\) is even and\n\\[\na_{k-1}<a_k<a_{k-2}.\n\\]\nThe decimal bases satisfy \\(c_{k-1}<c_{k+1}<c_k\\). Comparing both bases and exponents gives\n\\[\nc_{k-1}^{a_{k-2}}\n<c_{k+1}^{a_k}\n<c_k^{a_{k-1}},\n\\]\nwhich is exactly \\(a_{k-1}<a_{k+1}<a_k\\).\n\nWhen \\(k\\ge3\\) is odd, the corresponding assumptions are \\(a_{k-2}<a_k<a_{k-1}\\) and \\(c_k<c_{k+1}<c_{k-1}\\). They give\n\\[\nc_k^{a_{k-1}}\n<c_{k+1}^{a_k}\n<c_{k-1}^{a_{k-2}},\n\\]\nso \\(a_k<a_{k+1}<a_{k-1}\\). Starting with \\(a_1<a_2<a_0\\), these two steps apply alternately and prove the nesting for the entire sequence.\n\nIt follows that the odd-indexed terms strictly increase, the even-indexed terms strictly decrease, and the last odd term is below the last even term. Thus the decreasing order is\n\\[\na_2>a_4>\\cdots>a_{2010}\n>a_{2011}>a_{2009}>\\cdots>a_1.\n\\]\nAll terms are distinct. There are \\(1005\\) even-indexed terms, so\n\\[\nb_k=a_{2k}\\qquad(1\\le k\\le1005).\n\\]\nEquality \\(a_k=b_k\\) here would require \\(k=2k\\), impossible for positive \\(k\\).\n\nFor the remaining positions,\n\\[\nb_k=a_{4023-2k}\n\\qquad(1006\\le k\\le2011).\n\\]\nDistinctness now makes \\(a_k=b_k\\) equivalent to\n\\[\nk=4023-2k,\n\\qquad k=1341.\n\\]\nThis lies in the required range and is the only matching index. The requested sum is therefore \\(\\boxed{1341}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
