"explanation": "For every integer \\(m\\), the graph of \\(f\\) joins \\((m,1)\\), \\((m+\\tfrac12,0)\\), and \\((m+1,1)\\) by straight segments. Thus \\(0\\le f\\le1\\), and \\(f\\) is continuous even at integers: the fractional-part jump disappears after taking the absolute value.\n\nFix a positive integer \\(n\\). Every solution satisfies \\(0\\le x\\le n\\). The endpoint \\(x=0\\) is not a solution, while \\(x=n\\) is, since \\(f(n)=1\\). Define\n\\[\ng(x)=xf(x),\n\\qquad H(x)=nf(g(x))-x.\n\\]\nWe will count the zeros of the continuous function \\(H\\) on each unit interval.\n\nFor \\(j=0,1,\\ldots,n-1\\), the inner function has formulas\n\\[\ng(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\nx(2j+1-2x),&j\\le x\\le j+\\tfrac12,\\\\\nx(2x-2j-1),&j+\\tfrac12\\le x\\le j+1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor \\(j\\ge1\\), the first piece decreases from \\(j\\) to \\(0\\). For every \\(j\\ge0\\), the second increases from \\(0\\) to \\(j+1\\).\n\nWe also need to know that each crossing is unique. On either of these monotone pieces, if \\(u<v\\), expansion gives\n\\[\n|g(v)-g(u)|\n=(v-u)(2u+2v-2j-1)\n\\ge v-u.\n\\]\nFor the decreasing piece the last factor is at least \\(2j-1\\ge1\\); for the increasing piece it is at least \\(2j+1\\ge1\\).\n\nSplit each piece further whenever \\(g(x)\\) reaches an integer or a half-integer. On each resulting branch, \\(f(g(x))\\) moves strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). If it increases, its change between \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) is \\(2|g(v)-g(u)|\\), so\n\\[\nH(v)-H(u)\\ge(2n-1)(v-u)>0.\n\\]\nIf it decreases, \\(H\\) also strictly decreases. Thus \\(H\\) is strictly monotone on every such branch.\n\nAt a branch endpoint where \\(f(g(x))=0\\), we have \\(H(x)=-x<0\\). Where \\(f(g(x))=1\\), we have \\(H(x)=n-x>0\\), except at \\(x=n\\), where it is zero. Continuity and strict monotonicity therefore give exactly one zero on each branch, with the final branch contributing the endpoint \\(x=n\\).\n\nFor \\(j\\ge1\\), the decreasing piece takes \\(g\\) from \\(j\\) to \\(0\\), crossing \\(2j\\) half-unit ranges. It contributes \\(2j\\) zeros. The increasing piece takes \\(g\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(j+1\\), crossing \\(2j+2\\) half-unit ranges and contributing \\(2j+2\\) zeros. Together these give \\(4j+2\\).\n\nThe initial piece \\(0\\le x\\le\\tfrac12\\) is the exception to the monotonic description, but it contributes no zeros. Here\n\\[\n0\\le g(x)=\\frac18-2\\left(x-\\frac14\\right)^2\\le\\frac18,\n\\]\nso \\(f(g(x))\\ge\\tfrac34\\). Hence \\(nf(g(x))\\ge\\tfrac34>x\\). The other half of \\([0,1]\\) contributes two zeros, again agreeing with \\(4j+2\\) when \\(j=0\\).\n\nNo shared branch endpoint is a zero except \\(x=n\\): the other endpoint values of \\(H\\) are strictly positive or negative. Thus there is no double counting, and the total number of distinct solutions is\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(4j+2)=2n^2.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n2\\cdot31^2=1922<2012,\n\\qquad\n2\\cdot32^2=2048\\ge2012.\n\\]\nSince \\(2n^2\\) increases for positive integers \\(n\\), the smallest possible value is \\(\\boxed{32}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
