"label": "Complete the grid and use reciprocal pairs",
"explanation": "For a right triangle labeled as in the question,\n\\[\nf(t)=\\frac{AC}{AB}.\n\\]\nA reflection reverses counter-clockwise order, so after reflection the roles of \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) switch. The reflected triangle therefore has factor \\(AB/AC=1/f(t)\\). Whenever a finite family of triangles is preserved by a reflection, distinct reflected pairs contribute \\(1\\), and any triangle fixed by the reflection has \\(f(t)=1\\). The product over that family is consequently \\(1\\).\n\nAdd the missing point \\(O=(0,0)\\), obtaining the complete grid\n\\[\nG=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\times\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}.\n\\]\nReflection across \\(x=2\\) preserves this grid, so the product over all its right triangles is \\(1\\). Let \\(R\\) be the product over the triangles that contain \\(O\\). All factors are positive and finite, since the angle defining each tangent is acute. Removing exactly these triangles gives the desired product \\(1/R\\).\n\nWe can simplify \\(R\\) before enumerating. Among the triangles containing \\(O\\), those entirely inside the square grid \\(\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}^2\\) are preserved by reflection across \\(y=x\\), so their product is \\(1\\). Thus \\(R\\) equals the product over triangles containing \\(O\\) and at least one point on the top row \\(y=5\\).\n\nFirst classify those whose right angle is at \\(O\\) or on a coordinate axis. At \\(O\\), two nonzero perpendicular vectors with nonnegative coordinates must lie on the two axes. For each \\(i=1,2,3,4\\), the possibilities and factors are:\n\nRight angle at \\(O\\), with other vertices \\((i,0),(0,5)\\): the counter-clockwise order gives factor \\(5/i\\).\n\nRight angle at \\((i,0)\\), with other vertices \\(O,(i,5)\\): the factor is \\(i/5\\).\n\nRight angle at \\((0,5)\\), with other vertices \\(O,(i,5)\\): the factor is again \\(i/5\\).\n\nThese exhaust the axis cases because the leg through \\(O\\) forces the other leg to be horizontal or vertical. Their combined product is\n\\[\n\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^4\\frac5i\\right)\n\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^4\\frac i5\\right)^2\n=\\frac{24}{625}.\n\\]\n\nNow suppose the right angle is at \\(A=(a,b)\\), with \\(a,b>0\\), and call the third vertex \\(Q=(c,d)\\). The Pythagorean condition \\(OA^2+AQ^2=OQ^2\\) becomes\n\\[\na^2+b^2+(c-a)^2+(d-b)^2=c^2+d^2,\n\\]\nor, after cancellation,\n\\[\na(c-a)+b(d-b)=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(b=5\\), this requires\n\\[\na(c-a)=5(5-d).\n\\]\nWhen \\(d=5\\), it forces \\(Q=A\\), which is not a triangle. When \\(d\\le4\\), the right side is at least \\(5\\), but the left side is at most \\(a(4-a)\\le4\\). Thus the right-angle vertex cannot lie on the top row.\n\nConsequently \\(1\\le a,b\\le4\\), and \\(Q\\) must be the top-row point \\((c,5)\\). The condition is now\n\\[\na(a-c)=b(5-b).\n\\]\nPut \\(k=a-c\\). Then \\(1\\le k\\le a\\le4\\). If \\(b=1\\) or \\(4\\), the right side is \\(4\\), and the only factor pairs are \\((a,k)=(2,2),(4,1)\\). If \\(b=2\\) or \\(3\\), the right side is \\(6\\), and the only factor pair is \\((a,k)=(3,2)\\). This gives exactly six triangles. Every listed pair satisfies the Pythagorean equality with distinct vertices, so the converse confirms that each triangle is right.\n\nHere \\(c<a\\) and \\(5>b\\), and the counter-clockwise order is \\(A,Q,O\\). Therefore \\(f=AO/AQ\\). Since\n\\[\na-c=\\frac{b(5-b)}a,\n\\]\nthe distance formula gives \\(AQ=\\frac{5-b}{a}AO\\), and hence\n\\[\nf=\\frac{a}{5-b}.\n\\]\nFor \\(b=1\\), the two factors are \\(1/2,1\\); for \\(b=2\\), the factor is \\(1\\); for \\(b=3\\), it is \\(3/2\\); and for \\(b=4\\), the two factors are \\(2,4\\). Their product is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot1\\cdot1\\cdot\\frac32\\cdot2\\cdot4=6.\n\\]\n\nThe classification is exhaustive, and every triangle is counted once because it has a unique right-angle vertex. Thus\n\\[\nR=\\frac{24}{625}\\cdot6=\\frac{144}{625}.\n\\]\nThe requested product is\n\\[\n\\frac1R=\\boxed{\\frac{625}{144}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
