"responseType": "choice",
"question": "Bernardo chooses a three-digit positive integer \\(N\\) and writes both its base-\\(5\\) and base-\\(6\\) representations on a blackboard. Later LeRoy sees the two numbers Bernardo has written. Treating the two numbers as base-\\(10\\) integers, he adds them to obtain an integer \\(S.\\)\n\nFor example, if \\(N = 749,\\) Bernardo writes the numbers \\(10,\\) \\(\\!444\\) and \\(3,\\) \\(\\!245,\\) and LeRoy obtains the sum \\(S = 13,\\) \\(\\!689.\\) For how many choices of \\(N\\) are the two rightmost digits of \\(S,\\) in order, the same as those of \\(2N?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 5 \\)",
"B": "\\( 10 \\)",
"C": "\\( 15 \\)",
"D": "\\( 20 \\)",
"E": "\\( 25 \\)"
},
