"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Only the two rightmost digits affect the condition, so the higher digits can be ignored after the written representations are treated as decimal integers. We use \\(X\\equiv Y\\pmod q\\) to mean that \\(q\\) divides \\(X-Y\\).\n\nLet the final two base-\\(5\\) digits be \\(u,r\\), and the final two base-\\(6\\) digits be \\(v,s\\). Thus \\(0\\le u,r\\le4\\) and \\(0\\le v,s\\le5\\). When read as decimal integers, their sum satisfies\n\\[\nS\\equiv10(u+v)+r+s\\pmod{100}.\n\\]\nThe requested condition is \\(S\\equiv2N\\pmod{100}\\).\n\nFirst reduce this condition modulo \\(5\\). Since \\(N\\equiv r\\pmod5\\), it gives\n\\[\nr+s\\equiv2r\\pmod5,\n\\qquad s\\equiv r\\pmod5.\n\\]\nWithin the digit ranges, either \\(s=r\\), or \\((r,s)=(0,5)\\). The latter is impossible: \\(S\\) would be odd, whereas \\(2N\\) is even. Hence \\(r=s\\), with \\(0\\le r\\le4\\).\n\nNow \\(N-r\\) is divisible by both \\(5\\) and \\(6\\), so write\n\\[\nN=30k+r.\n\\]\nThe three-digit restriction is exactly \\(4\\le k\\le33\\), with all five choices of \\(r\\) allowed for each such \\(k\\).\n\nRemoving the final digit and dividing by the base identifies the next digit. In base \\(5\\), the quotient is \\((N-r)/5=6k\\), so \\(u\\) is the remainder of \\(k\\) upon division by \\(5\\). In base \\(6\\), the quotient is \\((N-r)/6=5k\\), so\n\\[\nu\\equiv k\\pmod5,\n\\qquad v\\equiv-k\\pmod6.\n\\]\nSince the common units digit contributes \\(2r\\) to both \\(S\\) and \\(2N\\), the remaining requirement is\n\\[\n10(u+v)\\equiv60k\\pmod{100},\n\\]\nor equivalently\n\\[\nu+v\\equiv6k\\pmod{10}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(k=5q+u\\) for an integer \\(q\\). Then \\(6k\\equiv6u\\pmod{10}\\), so the condition becomes\n\\[\nv\\equiv5u\\pmod{10}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(0\\le v\\le5\\), this has just two cases:\n\nIf \\(u\\) is even, then \\(v=0\\), so \\(k\\) is divisible by \\(6\\). In the interval \\(4\\le k\\le33\\), these values are \\(6,12,18,24,30\\). Their remainders modulo \\(5\\) are \\(1,2,3,4,0\\), respectively, so the even-remainder choices are \\(12,24,30\\).\n\nIf \\(u\\) is odd, then \\(v=5\\), so \\(k\\equiv1\\pmod6\\). The possible values are \\(7,13,19,25,31\\), whose remainders modulo \\(5\\) are \\(2,3,4,0,1\\). The odd-remainder choices are \\(13,31\\).\n\nThus exactly five values of \\(k\\) work: \\(12,13,24,30,31\\). Every one allows all five values \\(r=0,1,2,3,4\\). The conditions above are equivalent to the required last-two-digit equality, and distinct pairs \\((k,r)\\) give distinct integers \\(N\\). Therefore the count is \\(5\\cdot5=\\boxed{25}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
