"label": "Use the two cut lengths",
"explanation": "The parallel cuts create smaller equilateral triangles: corresponding angles in \\(ADE\\) and \\(AFG\\) are all \\(60^\\circ\\). Thus each cut length also equals the lengths of the two sides from \\(A\\) to that cut.\n\nLet\n\\[\nx=DE=AD=AE,\\qquad y=FG=AF=AG.\n\\]\nThe order of the points in the figure gives \\(0<x<y<1\\). Subtracting lengths along the sides gives\n\\[\nDF=EG=y-x,\\qquad FB=GC=1-y.\n\\]\nWe can now express all three perimeters using just \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{ADE}&=3x,\\\\\nP_{DFGE}&=x+y+2(y-x)=3y-x,\\\\\nP_{FBCG}&=y+1+2(1-y)=3-y.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEquating the first perimeter with each of the other two gives\n\\[\n4x=3y,\\qquad 3x+y=3.\n\\]\nThe first equation gives \\(y=4x/3\\). Substituting this into the second eliminates \\(y\\):\n\\[\n3x+\\frac{4x}{3}=3,\n\\qquad 13x=9.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(x=9/13\\) and \\(y=12/13\\). These satisfy \\(0<x<y<1\\), and all three perimeters equal \\(27/13\\), so the lengths meet the original conditions. Hence\n\\[\nDE+FG=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{12}{13}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{21}{13}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
