"solution": "Use the recorded convention that \\(\\log\\) means base \\(10\\). Rather than calculate every logarithm, we only need to bound the term added to \\(2013\\). Define\n\\[\nt_2=\\log2,\\qquad t_k=\\log(k+t_{k-1})\n\\]\nfor \\(3\\le k\\le2013\\). Then \\(A=t_{2013}\\).\n\nWe claim that \\(0<t_k<4\\) for \\(2\\le k\\le2012\\). The starting value \\(t_2=\\log2\\) satisfies this. If \\(0<t_{k-1}<4\\), then, for \\(3\\le k\\le2012\\),\n\\[\n1<k+t_{k-1}<k+4\\le2016<10^4.\n\\]\nBecause the common logarithm is increasing, taking logarithms preserves these inequalities and gives \\(0<t_k<4\\). Thus the bound holds through every required step, and all logarithms have positive arguments.\n\nAt the last inner step, positivity also gives\n\\[\nt_{2012}>\\log2012>\\log1000=3.\n\\]\nCombining this with the upper bound yields\n\\[\n2016<2013+t_{2012}<2017.\n\\]\nTaking logarithms once more,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\log2016<A<\\log2017}.\n\\]\nTherefore the answer is choice \\(\\text{A}\\).",
