"explanation": "Before counting pairwise intersections as distinct points, we must check the statement that no three parabolas meet. The focus-directrix definition supplies a direct test: a point is on one of these parabolas exactly when its distance to the origin equals its perpendicular distance to that parabola's directrix.\n\nConsider\n\\[\nT=\\left(\\frac12,\\frac12\\right).\n\\]\nIts distance to the focus \\(F=(0,0)\\) is\n\\[\nTF=\\sqrt{\\frac14+\\frac14}=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\nFor a directrix \\(y=ax+b\\), the distance from \\(T\\) to the line is\n\\[\n\\frac{|a/2-1/2+b|}{\\sqrt{a^2+1}}.\n\\]\nNow take the three allowed directrices\n\\[\ny=x+1,\\qquad y=x-1,\\qquad y=-x+2.\n\\]\nFor each, the numerator in the distance formula is \\(1\\) and the denominator is \\(\\sqrt2\\). Thus \\(T\\) has distance \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) from all three lines, and it lies on all three corresponding parabolas.\n\nConsequently, the explicitly specified family contradicts the no-triple assertion. At this location, three different pairs of parabolas share the same point, so summing pairwise intersections would count that location more than once. The recorded \\(810\\), choice C, is only the intended count conditional on the no-triple claim; it cannot be presented as an unconditional distinct-point count for the displayed family. As written, the conditions are inconsistent and no answer choice is justified.",
