"solution": "The angle bisector suggests reflecting one side of the triangle onto the other. Let\n\\[\ns=BN>0,\\qquad r=\\frac{AN}{BN}>0.\n\\]\nThe angle bisector theorem gives\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{BC}=\\frac{AN}{BN}=r.\n\\]\nReflect \\(A\\) across \\(CN\\) to a point \\(D\\). Since \\(CN\\) bisects \\(\\angle ACB\\), the point \\(D\\) lies on ray \\(CB\\), and reflection gives\n\\[\nCD=CA=2,\\qquad ND=NA=rs.\n\\]\n\nTo locate \\(D\\), orient \\(AB\\) horizontally with \\(A,N,B\\) in that order and \\(C\\) above the line. The equilateral triangle makes ray \\(NC\\) form \\(60^\\circ\\) with the rightward ray \\(NB\\). Reflecting the leftward ray \\(NA\\) across \\(NC\\) produces a ray \\(ND\\) that is \\(60^\\circ\\) below \\(NB\\). Thus \\(D\\) is below \\(AB\\), so it lies beyond \\(B\\) on ray \\(CB\\). Also, \\(\\angle NBM=60^\\circ\\), giving\n\\[\nND\\parallel BM,\\qquad \\angle BND=60^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\([UVW]\\) for the area of triangle \\(UVW\\). Since \\(AM=MC\\), triangles \\(ABM\\) and \\(CBM\\) have equal areas. Since \\(ND\\parallel BM\\), points \\(N,D\\) have equal perpendicular distances from \\(BM\\), so triangles \\(NBM\\) and \\(DBM\\) also have equal areas. Comparing triangles with bases on the same line gives\n\\[\n\\frac{[ABM]}{[NBM]}=\\frac{AB}{NB}=r+1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{[CBM]}{[DBM]}=\\frac{CB}{DB}.\n\\]\nThe equal areas therefore imply \\(r+1=CB/DB\\).\n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies between \\(C\\) and \\(D\\),\n\\[\nDB=CD-CB=AC-CB=(r-1)CB.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n(r+1)(r-1)=1,\n\\qquad r^2=2.\n\\]\nThe positive length ratio is \\(r=\\sqrt2\\), so\n\\[\nBC=\\frac2{\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2,\n\\qquad BD=2-\\sqrt2.\n\\]\n\nIn triangle \\(BND\\), the sides adjacent to its \\(60^\\circ\\) angle are \\(BN=s\\) and \\(ND=\\sqrt2s\\). The cosine law gives\n\\[\nBD^2=s^2+2s^2-2\\sqrt2s^2\\cos60^\\circ\n=(3-\\sqrt2)s^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(3-\\sqrt2>0\\),\n\\[\nBN^2=\\frac{(2-\\sqrt2)^2}{3-\\sqrt2}\n=\\frac{6-4\\sqrt2}{3-\\sqrt2}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{10-6\\sqrt2}{7}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
