"solution": "Changing from arriving \\(1\\) hour late to arriving \\(30\\) minutes early saves \\(1.5\\) hours. The first hour is the same in both travel plans, so all this saving occurs on the remaining journey.\n\nHis new speed is \\(35+15=50\\) miles per hour. Let \\(x>0\\) be the remaining distance in miles after the first hour. Since time equals distance divided by speed,\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{35}-\\frac{x}{50}=\\frac32.\n\\]\nBoth speeds are positive, so these denominators are nonzero. Multiplying by \\(350\\), the least common multiple of the denominators, gives\n\\[\n10x-7x=525,\n\\qquad x=175.\n\\]\nIncluding the first \\(35\\) miles, the airport is\n\\[\n35+175=\\boxed{210\\text{ miles}}\n\\]\nfrom his home, choice C. At this distance, continuing at \\(35\\) miles per hour would take \\(6\\) hours altogether, while the faster plan takes \\(4.5\\) hours. A scheduled arrival \\(5\\) hours after departure gives exactly the stated lateness and earliness.",
