"id": "inverse-speed-time-ratio",
"label": "Use the ratio of travel times",
"explanation": "After the first hour, David has already driven \\(35\\) miles. The two possible speeds for the remaining distance are \\(35\\) and \\(50\\) miles per hour. The faster plan changes his arrival from \\(1\\) hour late to \\(30\\) minutes early, so it saves \\(1.5\\) hours.\n\nFor the same distance, travel times are inversely proportional to speeds. Thus the slower and faster remaining times have ratio\n\\[\n50:35=10:7.\n\\]\nTheir difference is \\(3\\) equal parts, corresponding to \\(1.5\\) hours, so each part is \\(0.5\\) hour. The faster remaining time is therefore\n\\[\n7\\cdot0.5=3.5\\text{ hours}.\n\\]\nAt \\(50\\) miles per hour, the remaining distance is \\(50\\cdot3.5=175\\) miles. Adding the first \\(35\\) miles gives \\(\\boxed{210\\text{ miles}}\\), choice C.",
