"explanation": "Pairing neighboring terms lets Pascal's identity combine each pair into a single coefficient:\n\\[\n\\binom{2014}{2j-1}+\\binom{2014}{2j}=\\binom{2015}{2j}.\n\\]\nThe pairs with \\(1\\le j\\le31\\) cover all terms from \\(k=1\\) through \\(k=62\\), leaving the \\(k=0\\) term separately. Therefore\n\\[\nS=1+\\sum_{j=1}^{31}\\binom{2015}{2j}.\n\\]\nThe upper index \\(2015\\) is just \\(2\\) less than the prime modulus, making these coefficients easy to reduce.\n\nWrite \\(A\\equiv B\\pmod{2017}\\) when \\(2017\\) divides \\(A-B\\). For an even integer \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le62\\), the binomial coefficient formula gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr!\\binom{2015}{r}\n&=2015\\cdot2014\\cdots(2016-r)\\\\\n&\\equiv(-2)(-3)\\cdots(-(r+1))\\\\\n&=(r+1)!\\pmod{2017}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last product is positive because it has the even number \\(r\\) of negative factors. Since \\(2017\\) is prime and every factor of \\(r!\\) is smaller than \\(2017\\), the product \\(r!\\) has no factor \\(2017\\). We may therefore cancel \\(r!\\) in this congruence, obtaining\n\\[\n\\binom{2015}{r}\\equiv r+1\\pmod{2017}.\n\\]\nApplying this to \\(r=2,4,\\ldots,62\\) yields\n\\[\nS\\equiv1+3+5+\\cdots+63\n=\\frac{32(1+63)}2\n=1024\\pmod{2017}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(0\\le1024<2017\\), the requested remainder is \\(\\boxed{1024}\\), choice C.",
