"label": "Subtract the routes through the intersection",
"explanation": "Write \\(E\\) for one block east and \\(N\\) for one block north. Every five-block route to Jill's house contains three \\(E\\)'s and two \\(N\\)'s. Count all such routes by choosing the positions of the two northward moves. If the earlier \\(N\\) is in position \\(1,2,3,\\) or \\(4\\), the later \\(N\\) has \\(4,3,2,\\) or \\(1\\) possible positions, respectively. Thus there are\n\\[\n4+3+2+1=10\n\\]\nunrestricted routes. Each route determines exactly one pair of northward positions, so this count has no omissions or repetitions.\n\nA route through the dangerous intersection must reach it after two moves, either \\(EN\\) or \\(NE\\). From there, the remaining two eastward moves and one northward move can be ordered as \\(EEN\\), \\(ENE\\), or \\(NEE\\). Each of the two starts has three completions, giving \\(2\\times3=6\\) dangerous routes. Every dangerous route has a unique start and completion, so it is counted once.\n\nRemoving these routes leaves \\(10-6=4\\) safe ways, choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
