"solution": "Starting four years ago lets us express both ages with one unknown. Let \\(x\\geq0\\) be Claire's age then, so Pete's age was \\(4x\\). Two years later, both ages had increased by 2, and Pete was three times Claire's age. Thus\n\\[\n4x+2=3(x+2).\n\\]\nExpanding gives \\(4x+2=3x+6\\), so \\(x=4\\). Their current ages are therefore \\(4+4=8\\) for Claire and \\(4\\cdot4+4=20\\) for Pete.\n\nLet \\(t\\geq0\\) be the number of years from now until their ages have ratio \\(2:1\\). Since Claire's age \\(8+t\\) is positive, this means Pete's age is twice hers:\n\\[\n20+t=2(8+t)=16+2t.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(16+t\\) from both sides gives \\(t=4\\). Their ages will then be \\(24\\) and \\(12\\), whose ratio is \\(2:1\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\) years, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
