"solution": "Adding \\(1\\) lets the terms form a product, turning the integer equation into a factor-pair problem:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n81&=xy+x+y+1\\\\\n&=x(y+1)+(y+1)\\\\\n&=(x+1)(y+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(x>y>0\\), the factors satisfy \\(x+1>y+1\\geq2\\). The positive factor pairs of \\(81\\), written with the larger factor first, are\n\\[\n(81,1),\\qquad(27,3),\\qquad(9,9).\n\\]\nThese exhaust the possibilities: the smaller factor cannot exceed \\(9\\), and the only divisors of \\(81\\) at most \\(9\\) are \\(1,3,9\\).\n\nThe first pair would give \\(y=0\\), and the last would give \\(x=y\\). Thus \\(x+1=27\\) and \\(y+1=3\\), giving \\(x=26\\) and \\(y=2\\). They satisfy \\(26+2+26\\cdot2=80\\) and the required ordering. Therefore \\(\\boxed{x=26}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
