"solution": "First count distinct rational values, since different fraction representations do not create different choices. In lowest terms, the denominators \\(1,2,3,4,5\\) contribute respectively \\(2,2,4,4,8\\) numbers in \\([0,2)\\). For denominator \\(1\\), the values are \\(0,1\\). For denominator \\(2\\), they are the two odd halves. For denominator \\(3\\), the allowed numerators are \\(1,2,4,5\\); for denominator \\(4\\), they are \\(1,3,5,7\\); and for denominator \\(5\\), they are \\(1,2,3,4,6,7,8,9\\). Reduced representations are unique, so there are \\(20\\) choices. Choosing \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) uniformly and independently gives \\(20^2=400\\) equally likely ordered pairs, including pairs with \\(a=b\\).\n\nPut \\(x=\\cos(a\\pi)\\) and \\(y=\\sin(b\\pi)\\). Squaring twice reveals exactly when the fourth power is real:\n\\[\n(x+iy)^2=(x^2-y^2)+2xyi,\n\\]\nso the imaginary part of \\((x+iy)^4\\) is\n\\[\n4xy(x^2-y^2).\n\\]\nIt vanishes exactly when \\(x=0\\), \\(y=0\\), or \\(x^2=y^2\\).\n\nThere are two choices giving \\(x=0\\), namely \\(a=1/2,3/2\\), and two giving \\(y=0\\), namely \\(b=0,1\\). These cases contribute\n\\[\n2\\cdot20+20\\cdot2-2\\cdot2=76\n\\]\npairs. The subtraction counts the four pairs with \\(x=y=0\\) only once.\n\nIt remains to count \\(x^2=y^2\\) with both coordinates nonzero. Unit-circle symmetries show that\n\\[\n\\cos^2(a\\pi)=\\sin^2(b\\pi)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nb=\\frac12\\pm a+k\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(k\\): equal absolute trigonometric values occur at complementary angles and their reflections.\n\nIf \\(a=0\\) or \\(1\\), then \\(x^2=1\\), requiring \\(b=1/2\\) or \\(3/2\\). This gives \\(2\\cdot2=4\\) pairs. If \\(a\\) has reduced denominator \\(4\\), its four possible values all give \\(x^2=1/2\\). The four odd quarters for \\(b\\) give \\(y^2=1/2\\), contributing \\(4\\cdot4=16\\) pairs. Denominator \\(2\\) for \\(a\\) gives \\(x=0\\), already counted.\n\nFinally, suppose \\(a=m/d\\) is reduced with \\(d=3\\) or \\(5\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac12\\pm a=\\frac{d\\pm2m}{2d}.\n\\]\nThe numerator is odd and has no common factor with \\(d\\), so this fraction has reduced denominator \\(2d\\), which is \\(6\\) or \\(10\\). Adding an integer does not change that denominator. Thus no eligible \\(b\\) is possible in these cases. This exhausts all reduced denominators.\n\nThe \\(20\\) nonzero diagonal pairs are disjoint from the \\(76\\) axis pairs, and the factored imaginary part proves there are no other cases. Therefore the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{76+4+16}{400}=\\boxed{\\frac6{25}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
