"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "A product is odd exactly when both factors are odd. Thus we can count the relevant row and column labels without computing the entire table.\n\nThe labels run from \\(0\\) through \\(12\\), inclusive, so there are \\(13\\) rows and \\(13\\) columns. Each row-column pair specifies one cell, giving\n\\[\n13\\cdot13=169\n\\]\nentries in the body. Equal products appearing in different cells are still separate entries.\n\nThe odd labels are \\(1,3,5,7,9,11\\), giving six choices for each factor. Each of the six odd-labeled rows has an odd product in exactly the six odd-labeled columns. Hence there are \\(6\\cdot6=36\\) odd entries, counted once each by their cell positions.\n\nThe requested fraction is\n\\[\n\\frac{36}{169}\\approx0.2130,\n\\]\nwhich rounds to \\(\\boxed{0.21}\\) to the nearest hundredth, choice A.",
