"solution": "Label the chairs \\(1,2,\\ldots,6\\) around the table, and identify each person by the chair initially occupied. Each person may move two, three, or four positions around the circle. Thus there are two allowed destinations of the same parity and just one of the opposite parity: the chair directly opposite.\n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of people moving from odd chairs to even chairs. Since both groups contain three chairs and all chairs must remain occupied, exactly \\(k\\) people must also move from even chairs to odd chairs. We count the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\nIf \\(k=0\\), everyone stays within the original parity group. Within a group of three chairs, nobody may keep the original chair, so the three people must cycle in one of two directions. The two groups can be arranged independently, giving \\(2\\cdot2=4\\) possibilities.\n\nIf \\(k=1\\), choose the odd-chair person and the even-chair person who cross groups. There are \\(3\\cdot3=9\\) choices, and each crossing destination is forced to be the opposite chair. In either group, two people and two available chairs remain. Either both people's original chairs are available, forcing a swap, or exactly one person's original chair is available. In the latter case, that person must take the other available chair, forcing the final person's seat. Thus every choice has exactly one completion, giving \\(9\\) possibilities.\n\nIf \\(k=2\\), choose the one odd-chair person and one even-chair person who do not cross groups. There are initially \\(9\\) choices. After the other four people take their opposite chairs, the remaining available chairs are opposite these two chosen people's original chairs. If the chosen people started opposite each other, both would have to return to their original chairs, which is forbidden. There are \\(3\\) such choices. For each of the other \\(6\\) choices, the remaining seats are forced and allowed, giving \\(6\\) possibilities.\n\nIf \\(k=3\\), everyone takes the opposite chair, giving \\(1\\) possibility.\n\nEvery valid rearrangement belongs to exactly one of these cases, and the choices within each case identify it uniquely. The total is\n\\[\n4+9+6+1=\\boxed{20},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
