"explanation": "The region lies above \\(y=-0.1\\), below \\(y=\\pi x\\), and to the left of \\(x=5.1\\). Describe a square by its lower-left corner \\((a,b)\\) and side length \\(k\\). Integer vertices and sides parallel to the axes mean that \\(a,b,k\\) are integers with \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\nThe bottom and right edges satisfy the region's bounds exactly when \\(b\\ge0\\) and \\(a+k\\le5\\). For the sloping boundary, the upper-left corner is the most restrictive point: it has the greatest height and the smallest horizontal coordinate. Thus the complete containment conditions are\n\\[\nb\\ge0,\\qquad a+k\\le5,\\qquad b+k\\le\\pi a.\n\\]\nThese conditions are also sufficient, since every point of the square has \\(y\\le b+k\\) and \\(x\\ge a\\).\n\nBecause \\(b+k>0\\), the last inequality forces \\(a\\ge1\\). Hence \\(a\\le4\\) and \\(k\\le4\\). Using \\(3<\\pi<13/4\\), we have\n\\[\n3a<\\pi a<3a+1\\qquad(1\\le a\\le4).\n\\]\nSince \\(b+k\\) is an integer, its upper bound is therefore \\(3a\\). For fixed \\(a,k\\), the possible bottom coordinates are \\(b=0,1,\\ldots,3a-k\\), giving \\(3a-k+1\\) squares when \\(3a\\ge k\\).\n\nFor \\(k=1\\), the possible values \\(a=1,2,3,4\\) give \\(3+6+9+12=30\\) squares.\n\nFor \\(k=2\\), the values \\(a=1,2,3\\) give \\(2+5+8=15\\) squares.\n\nFor \\(k=3\\), the values \\(a=1,2\\) give \\(1+4=5\\) squares.\n\nFor \\(k=4\\), only \\(a=1\\) could fit horizontally, but then \\(b+4\\le\\pi\\) is impossible because \\(b\\ge0\\) and \\(\\pi<4\\).\n\nEvery eligible square has exactly one triple \\((a,b,k)\\), so these cases include every square once. The total is \\(30+15+5=50\\), choice D.",
