"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Interpret the random choices in the usual uniform sense: each number is uniformly distributed on \\([0,1]\\), and the three selections are independent. The ordered triple \\((x,y,z)\\) is then a uniform point in the unit cube, whose volume is \\(1\\).\n\nThree nonnegative lengths form a triangle with positive area exactly when all three triangle inequalities are strict. It is easier to measure the failures:\n\\[\nx\\ge y+z,\\qquad y\\ge x+z,\\qquad z\\ge x+y.\n\\]\nConsider the first failure. Its region is a tetrahedron with vertex \\((0,0,0)\\) and base in the plane \\(x=1\\). The base consists of points with \\(y,z\\ge0\\) and \\(y+z\\le1\\), a right triangle with both legs of length \\(1\\). Its area is \\(1/2\\), and the tetrahedron's height is \\(1\\). Its volume is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac13\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot1=\\frac16.\n\\]\nExchanging coordinates shows that each other failure has the same volume.\n\nWe must check overlap before adding these volumes. If both \\(x\\ge y+z\\) and \\(y\\ge x+z\\), adding gives \\(z=0\\), and then \\(x=y\\). Thus their intersection is only a line segment, with zero volume. The other pairs behave the same way. Equality boundaries, including degenerate triangles and zero side lengths, also have zero volume.\n\nThe total failure probability is consequently \\(3/6=1/2\\). The desired probability is\n\\[\n1-\\frac12=\\boxed{\\frac12},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
