"explanation": "Changing the sign of either coordinate leaves the equation unchanged, so the enclosed region has four equal quadrant portions. In the first quadrant, the absolute values disappear, giving\n\\[\nx^2+y^2=x+y.\n\\]\nCompleting the squares identifies a circle:\n\\[\n\\left(x-\\frac12\\right)^2+\n\\left(y-\\frac12\\right)^2=\\frac12.\n\\]\nIts center is \\(C=(1/2,1/2)\\), and its squared radius is \\(r^2=1/2\\).\n\nWe must identify which part of this circle belongs to the graph in this quadrant. For a nonzero first-quadrant solution, put \\(s=x+y>0\\). Then\n\\[\ns=x^2+y^2\\le(x+y)^2=s^2,\n\\]\nso \\(s\\ge1\\). Hence the nonzero graph points lie on the side \\(x+y\\ge1\\). The line \\(x+y=1\\) passes through \\(C\\) and meets the circle at \\(A=(1,0)\\) and \\(B=(0,1)\\). Thus \\(AB\\) is a diameter, and the relevant arc is the semicircle from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) through \\((1,1)\\). It stays in the first quadrant: its only axis intersections are its endpoints.\n\nThe first-quadrant portion of the enclosed region consists of triangle \\(OAB\\), where \\(O=(0,0)\\), together with the semicircular disk on the other side of \\(AB\\). Their interiors do not overlap, so its area is\n\\[\n\\frac{1\\cdot1}{2}+\\frac{\\pi r^2}{2}\n=\\frac12+\\frac\\pi4.\n\\]\nThe origin itself also satisfies the equation, but the inequality above shows that it is an isolated graph point; it contributes no area. Multiplying the quadrant area by four gives\n\\[\n4\\left(\\frac12+\\frac\\pi4\\right)\n=\\boxed{\\pi+2},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
