"solution": "The projection suggests comparing the nested triangles \\(ADQ_i\\). Write \\([XYZ]\\) for the area of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Because \\(P_i\\) lies on \\(AQ_i\\),\n\\[\n[DQ_iP_i]=[ADQ_i]-[ADP_i].\n\\]\nAlso, \\(P_iQ_{i+1}\\) is parallel to \\(AD\\), since both are perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Thus triangles \\(ADP_i\\) and \\(ADQ_{i+1}\\) have the same base \\(AD\\) and equal perpendicular heights. Therefore,\n\\[\n[DQ_iP_i]=[ADQ_i]-[ADQ_{i+1}].\n\\]\nAdding the first \\(N\\) areas cancels all the intermediate triangle areas, leaving\n\\[\nS_N=[ADQ_1]-[ADQ_{N+1}].\n\\]\nWe now need to show that the remaining triangle shrinks to zero area.\n\nLet \\(t_i=DQ_i\\), so \\(t_1=1/2\\). A square's diagonal makes a \\(45^\\circ\\) angle with its sides. Hence the right triangle \\(DQ_{i+1}P_i\\) has equal legs, giving \\(P_iQ_{i+1}=t_{i+1}\\). The right triangles \\(Q_iQ_{i+1}P_i\\) and \\(Q_iDA\\) share their angle at \\(Q_i\\), so they are similar. Their corresponding legs give\n\\[\n\\frac{t_i-t_{i+1}}{t_i}=\\frac{t_{i+1}}{1}.\n\\]\nSolving yields \\(t_{i+1}=t_i/(1+t_i)\\). Starting from \\(t_1=1/2\\), this relation keeps every length positive and makes it smaller than the preceding one. Taking reciprocals gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{t_{i+1}}=\\frac1{t_i}+1,\n\\qquad t_i=\\frac1{i+1}.\n\\]\nSince \\(AD=1\\), we obtain\n\\[\nS_N=\\frac14-\\frac1{2(N+2)}.\n\\]\nThe subtracted term becomes arbitrarily small as \\(N\\) increases. Thus the infinite sum is \\(\\boxed{\\frac14}\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
