"label": "Compute one wing in two ways",
"explanation": "Let \\(X\\) be the intersection of \\(\\overline{EB}\\) and \\(\\overline{CF}\\). The shaded wings are triangles \\(CEX\\) and \\(BFX\\). We can find the left wing's area by viewing it as the remainder of two different triangles.\n\nThe rectangle has width \\(EF=DC+CB+BA=3\\) and height \\(4\\). Let \\(h\\) be the perpendicular distance from \\(X\\) to the top edge \\(DA\\), and let \\(S\\) be the area of triangle \\(CEX\\). The distance from \\(X\\) to the bottom edge \\(EF\\) is then \\(4-h\\).\n\nTriangle \\(BCE\\) has base \\(BC=1\\) and height \\(4\\). Removing triangle \\(BCX\\), whose height is \\(h\\), leaves the left wing. Therefore,\n\\[\nS=\\frac{1\\cdot4}{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot h}{2}\n=2-\\frac h2.\n\\]\nTriangle \\(CEF\\) also contains the left wing. Removing triangle \\(EXF\\), whose base is \\(3\\) and height is \\(4-h\\), gives\n\\[\nS=\\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}-\\frac{3(4-h)}2\n=\\frac{3h}{2}.\n\\]\nThese expressions describe the same area, so\n\\[\n2-\\frac h2=\\frac{3h}{2},\\qquad 2=2h,\\qquad h=1.\n\\]\nThus the left wing has area \\(S=\\frac32\\). The right wing is triangle \\(BCF\\) with triangle \\(BCX\\) removed, so its area is\n\\[\n\\frac{1\\cdot4}{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot1}{2}=\\frac32.\n\\]\nThe two wings have disjoint interiors, so their total area is\n\\[\n\\frac32+\\frac32=\\boxed{3}\n\\]\nsquare units, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
