"explanation": "Let \\(n\\) be the positive integer number of steps. Counting horizontal and vertical toothpicks separately avoids counting any shared side twice.\n\nThere are \\(n\\) horizontal toothpicks along the bottom. The horizontal levels above it contain \\(n,n-1,\\ldots,1\\) toothpicks. Thus the horizontal total is \\(n+S\\), where\n\\[\nS=1+2+\\cdots+n.\n\\]\nAdding this sum to itself in reverse order gives \\(n\\) pairs, each totaling \\(n+1\\), so \\(S=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nVertically, the left edge contains \\(n\\) toothpicks, and the remaining vertical lines contain \\(n,n-1,\\ldots,1\\). Every toothpick belongs to exactly one of these horizontal or vertical groups. Hence the total is\n\\[\nT=2(n+S)=2n+n(n+1)=n(n+3).\n\\]\nWe need \\(n(n+3)=180\\). Since \\(12\\cdot15=180\\), \\(n=12\\) works. As positive \\(n\\) increases, both positive factors \\(n\\) and \\(n+3\\) increase, so their product strictly increases. Therefore this is the only possible step count: \\(\\boxed{12}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
