"id": "circle-centers",
"label": "Find the radius using circle centers",
"explanation": "Joining the centers of tangent unit circles turns the packing into triangles with known side lengths. We can use these triangles to find the larger circle's radius.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2019_AMC_12A_Q10_question_fig1.svg)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the center of the central small circle, \\(T\\) the center of the top small circle, and \\(P,Q\\) the centers of the two circles immediately below the top one. The displayed tangencies give\n\\[\nOP=OQ=PQ=TP=TQ=2.\n\\]\nThus \\(OPQ\\) and \\(TPQ\\) are equilateral triangles on opposite sides of \\(PQ\\). Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(PQ\\). Their altitudes lie on the same line, with \\(M\\) between \\(O\\) and \\(T\\). Since \\(PM=1\\), the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nOM=TM=\\sqrt{2^2-1^2}=\\sqrt3,\n\\qquad OT=2\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nEach of the six outer small circles touches a neighboring pair of circles that also touch the central circle. Repeating this argument shows that all six outer centers are at distance \\(2\\sqrt3\\) from \\(O\\).\n\nLet \\(K\\) and \\(R\\) be the center and radius of the larger circle. Internal tangency gives distance \\(R-1\\) from \\(K\\) to each outer small-circle center. These centers are not collinear, so the perpendicular bisectors of segments joining them determine a unique equidistant point. Since \\(O\\) is such a point, \\(K=O\\). Therefore\n\\[\nR=OT+1=2\\sqrt3+1.\n\\]\nThe small circles have disjoint interiors, so their total area is \\(13\\pi\\). The shaded area is consequently\n\\[\n\\pi(2\\sqrt3+1)^2-13\\pi\n=\\pi(13+4\\sqrt3)-13\\pi\n=\\boxed{4\\pi\\sqrt3}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice A.",
