"label": "Three horizontal pieces",
"explanation": "The common tangent suggests splitting the shaded region along \\(EG\\) and \\(AD\\). Let \\(H,O,K\\) be the midpoints of \\(AB,BC,CD\\), respectively, so they are the centers of the small semicircles. The tangent radii \\(HE,OF,KG\\) are perpendicular to \\(EG\\) and have length \\(1\\). Thus \\(EG\\) is parallel to \\(AD\\), one unit above it, and \\(EF=FG=2\\). Therefore \\(EG\\) is a diameter of the large circle.\n\nAbove \\(EG\\), the shaded region is an entire semicircle of radius \\(2\\), with area\n\\[\n\\frac12\\pi(2)^2=2\\pi.\n\\]\n\nBetween \\(EG\\) and \\(AD\\), consider rectangle \\(HEGK\\), whose dimensions are \\(4\\) by \\(1\\). The small semicircles occupy one quarter-disk from each outer semicircle and the whole middle semicircle, totaling\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi}{4}+\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\pi.\n\\]\nThe two remaining gaps are entirely inside the large circle. Indeed, the small arcs bow above their chords, so the gaps lie within triangles \\(EBF\\) and \\(FCG\\). These triangles are inside the large disk: \\(E,G\\) are on its boundary, \\(F\\) is its center, and \\(FB=FC=\\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\\sqrt2<2\\). Consequently, the shaded area in this strip is \\(4-\\pi\\).\n\nBelow \\(AD\\), no small semicircle removes any area. Let \\(X,Y\\) be the intersections of \\(AD\\) with the large circle. Since \\(FO\\) is perpendicular to chord \\(XY\\), it bisects that chord and its central angle. The Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nOX=OY=\\sqrt{2^2-1^2}=\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nTo find the central angle, extend \\(FO\\) beyond \\(O\\) to a point \\(J\\) with \\(OJ=1\\). Then \\(FJ=2\\), and\n\\[\nXJ=\\sqrt{OX^2+OJ^2}=\\sqrt{3+1}=2.\n\\]\nThus \\(FXJ\\) is equilateral, giving \\(\\angle XFO=60^\\circ\\) and hence \\(\\angle XFY=120^\\circ\\). The shaded circular segment below \\(AD\\) has area\n\\[\n\\frac{120}{360}\\pi(2)^2-\\frac12(2\\sqrt3)(1)\n=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3.\n\\]\n\nAdding the three pieces gives\n\\[\n2\\pi+(4-\\pi)+\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3\\right)\n=\\frac{7\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3+4.\n\\]\nSince \\(7\\) and \\(3\\) are relatively prime, the requested sum is \\(7+3+3+4=\\boxed{17}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
