"solution": "Use the usual geometric-probability interpretation: the coin's center is uniformly distributed over all positions that keep the coin inside the square. The coin has radius \\(\\frac12\\), so its center must stay at least \\(\\frac12\\) from every outer edge. Its allowed positions form a square of side \\(8-1=7\\), with area \\(49\\).\n\nPlace the outer square at \\([0,8]\\times[0,8]\\). The central square diamond has vertices \\((4,2)\\), \\((6,4)\\), \\((4,6)\\), and \\((2,4)\\). A coin reaches this diamond exactly when its center is inside the diamond or within distance \\(\\frac12\\) of its boundary.\n\nThese centers form the diamond itself, four outward rectangles along its sides, and four quarter-disks around its vertices. The pieces have disjoint interiors: outside the diamond, the nearest point lies either on a side or at a vertex. Their total area is\n\\[\n(2\\sqrt2)^2\n+4(2\\sqrt2)\\left(\\frac12\\right)\n+\\pi\\left(\\frac12\\right)^2\n=8+4\\sqrt2+\\frac\\pi4.\n\\]\nThis entire region lies inside \\([\\frac32,\\frac{13}{2}]^2\\), so none is excluded by the allowed-center square.\n\nNext consider the lower-left black triangle, described by \\(x,y\\ge0\\) and \\(x+y\\le2\\). Its sloping edge makes a \\(45^\\circ\\) angle with the axes. By Pythagoras, moving that edge outward by perpendicular distance \\(\\frac12\\) increases each axis intercept by \\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\). Thus the relevant parallel boundary is\n\\[\nx+y=2+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}.\n\\]\nWithin the allowed-center region, the centers reaching this corner triangle are exactly\n\\[\nx\\ge\\frac12,\\qquad y\\ge\\frac12,\n\\qquad x+y\\le2+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}.\n\\]\nTo check that the finite sloping edge suffices, these inequalities give\n\\[\n|x-y|\\le x+y-1\n\\le1+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}<2.\n\\]\nHence the perpendicular foot on \\(x+y=2\\) lies on the actual edge between \\((2,0)\\) and \\((0,2)\\). Centers already inside the black triangle also satisfy the displayed inequalities.\n\nThe favorable region at this corner is therefore an isosceles right triangle with legs \\(1+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\). All four corners contribute\n\\[\n4\\cdot\\frac12\\left(1+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)^2\n=3+2\\sqrt2.\n\\]\n\nThere is no overlap between regions associated with different black components. Distinct corner triangles are at least \\(4\\) apart. A corner triangle and the central diamond have facing parallel edges separated by \\(2\\sqrt2\\); for example, the lower-left pair lies on \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=6\\). Both distances exceed the coin's diameter \\(1\\), so one coin cannot reach two different components.\n\nThe total favorable center area is\n\\[\n8+4\\sqrt2+\\frac\\pi4+3+2\\sqrt2\n=11+6\\sqrt2+\\frac\\pi4.\n\\]\nTangencies and other boundary positions have area zero, so whether touching counts does not change the probability. Under the uniform-center model,\n\\[\nP=\\frac{11+6\\sqrt2+\\pi/4}{49}\n=\\frac{44+24\\sqrt2+\\pi}{196}.\n\\]\nThus \\(a=44\\), \\(b=24\\), and \\(a+b=\\boxed{68}\\), choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
