"solution": "Let \\(\\tau(n)\\) be the number of positive divisors of \\(n\\), so one step sends \\(n\\) to \\(f(n)=2\\tau(n)\\). We need to know which starting numbers eventually reach \\(12\\), since \\(f(12)=12\\).\n\nIf \\(n=p_1^{a_1}\\cdots p_k^{a_k}\\), its divisors independently choose each prime exponent from \\(0\\) through \\(a_i\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\tau(n)=(a_1+1)\\cdots(a_k+1).\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\le50\\), the possible prime-factorization shapes are\n\\[\n1;\\quad p,p^2,p^3,p^4,p^5;\\quad pq,p^2q,p^3q,p^4q,p^2q^2;\\quad pqs,\n\\]\nwhere the letters denote distinct primes. This list is complete: four distinct primes already have product at least \\(210\\); three distinct primes with a repeated factor have product at least \\(60\\); and for two primes, the next excluded exponent patterns have minimum values \\(2^3 3^2=72\\) and \\(2^5 3=96\\).\n\nThe divisor counts from these shapes are \\(1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9,10\\). If \\(\\tau(n)\\) is \\(1,2,3,4,5\\), or \\(8\\), the first value is respectively \\(2,4,6,8,10\\), or \\(16\\). All of these reach \\(8\\) and stay there:\n\\[\n2\\longrightarrow4\\longrightarrow6\\longrightarrow8\\longrightarrow8,\n\\qquad16\\longrightarrow10\\longrightarrow8.\n\\]\nThe remaining divisor counts \\(6,9,10\\) give first values \\(12,18,20\\); each goes to \\(12\\) and stays there. All these paths settle long before the fiftieth step.\n\nNow list the starting numbers with those counts. For \\(\\tau(n)=6\\), the shapes are \\(p^5\\) or \\(p^2q\\), giving\n\\[\n12,18,20,28,32,44,45,50.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\tau(n)=9\\), the shape \\(p^2q^2\\) gives only \\(36\\). For \\(\\tau(n)=10\\), the shape \\(p^4q\\) gives only \\(48\\). Thus there are \\(8+1+1=\\boxed{10}\\) starting numbers, choice D.",
