"solution": "The number of turns can stay unknown: every segment makes the same acute angle \\(\\theta\\) with the forward direction along the field's 100-meter length.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2023_AMC_12A_Q15_question_fig1.svg)\n\nLet the positive segment lengths be \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ldots,\\ell_k\\), including the final segment ending on \\(BC\\). By the right-triangle definition of cosine, a segment of length \\(\\ell_i\\) advances \\(\\ell_i\\cos\\theta\\) in the direction of \\(AB\\).\n\nEvery segment moves forward, so these advances add to \\(AB=100\\). The total walking distance is \\(120\\), hence\n\\[\n100=(\\ell_1+\\cdots+\\ell_k)\\cos\\theta\n=120\\cos\\theta.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\cos\\theta=\\frac56,\n\\qquad \\theta=\\boxed{\\arccos\\frac56}.\n\\]\nThis is an acute angle, so the path can be continued forward, turning at the long sides until it reaches \\(BC\\). The calculation includes a final partial crossing and makes no assumption about the number of segments. The answer is choice \\(\\mathrm A\\).",
