"explanation": "Similarity lets us express the needed widths using one common side ratio. Let \\(L=AB\\), let \\(H\\) be the enclosing rectangle's height, and set \\(k=L/H\\). Because the figure is drawn to scale, the enclosing rectangle and the rectangle marked 32 have their longer sides horizontal, while the rectangles marked 36 and 4 have their longer sides vertical. Thus \\(k>1\\) is their common long-side to short-side ratio.\n\nFor a similar rectangle of area \\(S\\), if its short side is \\(s>0\\), then its long side is \\(ks\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nS=ks^2,\n\\qquad\ns=\\sqrt{\\frac Sk},\n\\qquad\nks=\\sqrt{Sk}.\n\\]\nThe widths of the rectangles marked 36 and 4 are therefore \\(6/\\sqrt{k}\\) and \\(2/\\sqrt{k}\\). Their bottom edges together form the top edge of the rectangle marked 32, whose width is \\(\\sqrt{32k}\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\frac8{\\sqrt{k}}=\\sqrt{32k}.\n\\]\nBoth sides are positive, so squaring preserves the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{64}{k}=32k,\n\\qquad k^2=2.\n\\]\nSince \\(k>0\\), we have \\(k=\\sqrt2\\).\n\nThe eleven labeled rectangles fill the enclosing rectangle without overlapping interiors. Adding their areas gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nLH&=36+16+8+18+1+9\\\\\n&\\quad+4+2+49+25+32\\\\\n&=200.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nCombining its area and side ratio,\n\\[\nL^2=\\frac LH\\,(LH)=200\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nL^4=(200\\sqrt2)^2=80000=10^4\\cdot8.\n\\]\nTaking the positive fourth root, because \\(L\\) is a length, gives \\(L=10\\cdot8^{1/4}=\\boxed{10\\sqrt[4]{8}}\\), choice D.",
