"solution": "The grid counts must be multiplied by brick side lengths to obtain the actual block dimensions. The old block has edge lengths \\(3a,3b,3c\\). Since renaming the brick sides does not change their sum, call the side used in the new seven-brick direction \\(a\\). The new edge lengths are then \\(7a,2b,2c\\). We must determine how these match the old edges when every dimension increases by \\(1\\).\n\nAn old edge and its corresponding new edge cannot use the same brick side \\(t\\). Such a match would require either\n\\[\n2t=3t+1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad7t=3t+1.\n\\]\nThese give \\(t=-1\\) or \\(t=1/4\\), neither a positive integer.\n\nThus the old edge \\(3a\\) must correspond to \\(2b\\) or \\(2c\\). Interchange the names \\(b,c\\), if necessary, so that it corresponds to \\(2b\\). Of the remaining new edges \\(2c\\) and \\(7a\\), the old edge \\(3c\\) cannot correspond to \\(2c\\). It must therefore correspond to \\(7a\\), leaving \\(3b\\) to correspond to \\(2c\\). This covers every possible matching, up to renaming the sides, and gives\n\\[\n2b=3a+1,\\qquad\n2c=3b+1,\\qquad\n7a=3c+1.\n\\]\nThe first two equations express both \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) in terms of \\(a\\), allowing us to eliminate them:\n\\[\nb=\\frac{3a+1}{2},\n\\qquad\nc=\\frac{3b+1}{2}=\\frac{9a+5}{4}.\n\\]\nSubstitute into the third equation:\n\\[\n7a=3\\left(\\frac{9a+5}{4}\\right)+1\n=\\frac{27a+19}{4}.\n\\]\nThus \\(28a=27a+19\\), so\n\\[\na=19,\\qquad b=29,\\qquad c=44.\n\\]\nThese are positive integers. They are pairwise relatively prime because any common divisor of a pair must divide the corresponding expression\n\\[\n2b-3a=1,\\qquad\n2c-3b=1,\\qquad\n7a-3c=1.\n\\]\nFinally, the matched old edge lengths \\(57,87,132\\) become \\(58,88,133\\), so each increases by exactly \\(1\\), as required.\n\nTherefore\n\\[\na+b+c=19+29+44=\\boxed{92},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
