"explanation": "Let \\(R\\) and \\(r\\) be the positive radii of the larger and smaller semicircles. The shaded area is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi R^2}{2}-\\frac{\\pi r^2}{2}\n=\\frac{\\pi}{2}(R^2-r^2).\n\\]\nThus we only need the difference of the squared radii, rather than either radius separately.\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the center of the larger semicircle, and drop a perpendicular from \\(O\\) to the line \\(CD\\), meeting it at \\(M\\). The smaller semicircle's center lies on \\(AB\\). Its radius to the tangency point is perpendicular to \\(CD\\), so the distance between the parallel lines \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) is \\(r\\). Since \\(O\\) also lies on \\(AB\\), we have \\(OM=r\\).\n\nA perpendicular from a circle's center to a chord bisects that chord. Therefore\n\\[\nCM=MD=\\frac{16}{2}=8.\n\\]\nThe right triangle \\(OMC\\) has legs \\(r\\) and \\(8\\), and hypotenuse \\(OC=R\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nR^2=r^2+8^2,\n\\qquad R^2-r^2=64.\n\\]\nThe shaded area is consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot64=\\boxed{32\\pi}\n\\]\nsquare units, choice \\(C\\).",
