"solution": "The sectors have different sizes, so we count colors on fixed sectors without identifying rotations or reflections. Label the sectors \\(1,2,\\ldots,6\\) clockwise, starting at any fixed sector. Remember that sectors \\(6\\) and \\(1\\) are neighbors.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2025_AMC_12B_Q13_question_fig1.svg)\n\nThe three choices for the color of sector \\(1\\) give equal counts by relabeling colors. First suppose sector \\(1\\) is red. The other red sector cannot be \\(2\\) or \\(6\\), so it must be \\(3\\), \\(4\\), or \\(5\\).\n\nIf the other red sector is \\(3\\), sector \\(2\\) can be green or blue. Sectors \\(4,5,6\\) must alternate between green and blue. Since each color must occur twice, their order is forced once sector \\(2\\) is chosen. For example, if sector \\(2\\) is green, sectors \\(4,5,6\\) must be blue, green, blue. This gives \\(2\\) completions.\n\nIf the other red sector is \\(4\\), the remaining sectors form two adjacent pairs, \\((2,3)\\) and \\((5,6)\\). Each pair must contain one green and one blue, in either order. This gives \\(2\\cdot2=4\\) completions, all using each color twice.\n\nIf the other red sector is \\(5\\), sector \\(6\\) can be green or blue, and the alternating order on sectors \\(2,3,4\\) is then forced by the color totals. This gives \\(2\\) completions.\n\nThese cases exhaust the possible positions of the second red sector and do not overlap. Restoring all three choices for sector \\(1\\), the total is\n\\[\n3(2+4+2)=\\boxed{24},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
