"responseType": "choice",
"question": "The region shown below consists of \\(24\\) squares, each with side length \\(1\\) centimeter. What is the area, in square centimeters, of the largest circle that can fit inside the region, possibly touching the boundaries?\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2025_AMC_8_Q12_question_fig1.svg)",
"questionType": "mixed",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3\\pi\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\pi\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\pi\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\pi\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\pi\\)"
},
"choiceOrder": [
"A",
"B",
"C",
"D",
"E"
],
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"acceptedResponses": [
"C"
],
"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "The inward corners of the steps let us find a circle that fits and prove that no larger circle can fit. All lengths below are in centimeters.\n\nReading the unit grid, the six rows contain \\(2,4,6,6,4,2\\) squares. The region fits inside a \\(6\\times6\\) square. Let \\(O\\) be the center of that square.\n\nEach inward corner is \\(1\\) unit from \\(O\\) in one grid direction and \\(2\\) units in the perpendicular direction. By the Pythagorean theorem, its distance from \\(O\\) is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\\sqrt5.\n\\]\nThese are the nearest boundary points to \\(O\\). Along each adjoining boundary segment, moving away from the inward corner increases one of these offsets; the four outermost sides are at least \\(3\\) units from \\(O\\). Therefore a circle centered at \\(O\\) with radius \\(\\sqrt5\\) fits, touching the inward corners.\n\nWe must also check whether moving the center could allow a larger circle. Consider any fitting circle with center \\(P\\) and radius \\(r\\). If \\(r\\le2\\), its area is at most \\(4\\pi<5\\pi\\), so suppose \\(r>2\\).\n\nBecause the circle fits inside the \\(6\\times6\\) bounding square, its center must be less than \\(1\\) unit from each central midline. By the region's reflection symmetry, we may assume \\(P\\) is on or to the right of \\(O\\) and on or above \\(O\\). Let its rightward and upward offsets be \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), where\n\\[\n0\\le a<1,\\qquad 0\\le b<1.\n\\]\nChoose the inward corner \\(Q\\) that is \\(1\\) unit right and \\(2\\) units above \\(O\\). The horizontal and vertical distances from \\(P\\) to \\(Q\\) are \\(1-a\\) and \\(2-b\\).\n\nThe corner \\(Q\\) cannot lie strictly inside the circle, because then some missing area immediately beyond that corner would also lie inside it. Thus \\(r\\le PQ\\), and the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nr^2\\le PQ^2=(1-a)^2+(2-b)^2\\le1^2+2^2=5.\n\\]\nSo every fitting circle has area at most \\(5\\pi\\), and the centered circle attains it. The largest area is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\) square centimeters, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
