"solution": "We only need to track whether Charlotte is at an inner or outer point, because all points of the same type have the same numbers of each type of neighbor.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2026_AMC_8_Q21_question_fig1.svg)\n\nEach outer point has two neighbors, both inner. Each inner point has four neighbors: two inner and two outer. Thus, from any inner point, Charlotte has probability \\(\\frac12\\) of moving to either type.\n\nCharlotte starts at an outer point, so her first move certainly takes her to an inner point. After her second move, she has probability \\(\\frac12\\) of being inner and probability \\(\\frac12\\) of being outer.\n\nOn the third move, every route that was at an outer point moves to an inner point. Of the probability already at inner points, half moves to outer points. These two cases cover every possible route, so the requested probability is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12=\\boxed{\\frac14}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\text{B}\\).",
